import{_ as s,P as o,d as r,e as c,f as n,H as l,q as a,y as i}from"./chunks/framework.D7CAgLCq.js";const y=JSON.parse(`{"title":"Part 2 Boundaries and Methods","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"Part 2 Boundaries and Methods","xkChars":0,"xkWords":7214,"xkLessons":{"intro-02-part":{"title":"Part 2 Boundaries and Methods","lead":"Whether something may enter discussion, what kind of support it can receive, and whether action may be based on it are three different questions. Part 2 first defines the theory's scope of use, then explains what evidence each kind of conclusion requires.","explanation":["Source location: Chinese source, lines 213–220; original anchor part_2. Short quotation: “That an object belongs to the broadly conceived empirical structural world means only that it can in principle be defined.” See the corresponding section on this page for the current source; the original research extract available for download preserves the historical manuscript.","Broadly conceived concerns the variety of objects: natural processes, technology, ecology, human action, and institutions may all be candidates. Empirical narrows admission: comparable materials must exist, and the object, states, relations or transitions, interfaces, time, scale, and identity must be made clear. This does not claim that a single mechanism already explains all these domains, still less prove that the world necessarily has a common purpose.","The human structured world is one class of these objects. Discussing people requires additional roles, culture, norms, and authorization. Human purposes must not be assigned to wind, water, algorithms, or ecological processes. Equally, the fact that an object concerns people does not make factual judgments inherently carry authority to act on them. Part 1's general outline is recast here as working boundaries; the later H, N, and O respectively take up domain adaptation, value premises, and operational procedures.","Each application must state its current scope of application anew: what the object is, which scale profile applies, which period the time window covers, and on what basis the identity criterion identifies the earlier and later object as the same. Then specify environment, region, data window, extrapolation unit, and exclusions. The broad overall object domain does not replace this narrow definition. Modeling a queue in one window, for example, does not explain every queue or permit directly transplanting its treatment into another institutional setting.","Purely metaphysical propositions may be discussed as philosophical questions, but without comparable materials and empirical elements they cannot borrow this book's evidential levels for empirical judgments. This restriction concerns the status of the conclusion; it does not declare the question meaningless. Conversely, empirical elements merely allow a testable question to be posed. Specific support must still be established through subsequent tests."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Object domain","leftText":"Which kinds of object may in principle be studied.","rightTitle":"Scope of the present application","rightText":"The objects, time, scale, environment, and exclusions actually studied on this occasion."},{"leftTitle":"Explaining an object","leftText":"Explains how it forms, operates, undergoes conversion, or dissolves.","rightTitle":"Assigning an object a purpose","rightText":"Claims that it pursues a goal; this requires support from its object type and materials."}],"boundaries":["A system without empirical elements cannot receive the standing of an empirical conclusion; nor does naming an observable object automatically confer support from formal tests.","Narrowing the scope of application generally corrects extrapolation; it does not withdraw all supported local facts.","Selection in natural processes, outputs of technical systems, and human intentions must not be equated merely because the same word for choice or selection appears."],"questions":["A conclusion comes from one term at one school. A researcher says, “Schools all belong to the human structured world, so it applies to every school.” What is missing?","If a mathematical model explains the outcome of resource allocation, has it already answered what distribution would be justified?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-2-1/figures/intro-02-part.png","caseLinks":[],"caseNotes":[],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"intro-02-part","start_line":227,"end_line":234,"startLine":227,"endLine":234}],"outputId":"intro-02-part","sourceHash":"fcf100a5c8688d1e0337c52750ce3ad21fb5dbcc250f97946602ce094345f825","argumentSteps":["Premise: empirical judgments require comparable materials and clear referents; otherwise, different observations cannot establish what is being compared.","Connection: a broad object domain cannot eliminate differences in environment, time, and measurement, so the overall scope must be narrowed to the current scope of application on each occasion.","Conclusion: structural explanation may be conducted across many kinds of object, but each conclusion holds only within a scope supported by materials and bridges.","Responsibility: description and explanation rest on materials and inference. Who should be protected and who may act additionally require disclosed normative premises and authorization; they cannot be inferred from the object's being open to study."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: defining the scope of one conveyor belt","text":"Suppose the study covers only one conveyor segment during a factory's day shift. B is its entrance and exit, X its belt speed and number of queued items, R the arrival and departure rules, I the upstream and downstream interfaces, and T one shift. The scale profile SP has nine axes: abbreviated here, A aggregation level is this equipment segment, X spatial extent runs from the specified entrance to exit, T time span is one shift, and R observation resolution is the recording and sampling protocol for belt speed and the queue. The identity criterion K requires the equipment and interfaces to retain specified relations. The five axes not developed—organizational level, causal level, scope of influence, network topology scope, and scope of jurisdiction and authorization—are omitted only in this teaching example. A complete application must still state each axis's status, with reasons where genuinely inapplicable. The scale profile must not be reduced to resolution alone. The example specifies which process segment is being asked about. If the product or interfaces change on the night shift, the old definition does not automatically cover it.","assumptions":"All equipment and periods are illustrative; no actual observational data are included.","boundary":"The example only shows what each item is for. It neither establishes explanatory gain from this grouping nor authorizes changes to production."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Teaching boundary: naming all things that go badly as a system without common states, a window, or comparable materials.","effect":"A name cannot produce a testable empirical object. It may remain a narrative question, but must not be assigned a level of empirical support.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"A conclusion comes from one term at one school. A researcher says, “Schools all belong to the human structured world, so it applies to every school.” What is missing?","answer":"What is missing is a basis for bridging and extrapolation across objects, periods, and environments. Membership in one overall object domain merely permits study; it does not guarantee the same mechanism or effect.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"If a mathematical model explains the outcome of resource allocation, has it already answered what distribution would be justified?","answer":"No. The model may explain the results of given rules. Justification requires explicit value premises, benefits and risks, the decision-maker, and authorization procedures.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-2-1/figures/intro-02-part.png","alt":"Broad empirical object domain, with comparable materials; current definition, object/scale profile/time window/identity criterion; bounded structural explanation, with conclusions holding under conditions; additional questions for human objects, roles/meaning/values; normative and operational procedures, reasons for protection/authorization; scope of adoptable actions, with separate evidence and permissions. A conceptual diagram based on this introduction. The upper and lower paths must not automatically imply each other; this is not a chart of empirical data.","caption":"A conceptual diagram based on this introduction. The upper and lower paths must not automatically imply each other; this is not a chart of empirical data."}],"diagramBrief":"A static diagram with two paths: the upper path asks how far explanation extends, and the lower asks who may do what. An object/time-window switch could highlight only definitions needing rechecking, without displaying fabricated success rates.","applicability":{"realCase":"not_applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"This introduction defines the object domain and scope of use; the teaching construction directly illustrates its logic. Additional historical facts cannot validate that choice of scope, so an actual case is not forced into the discussion."},"openQuestions":[],"taskId":"S-2-1","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical Chinese research manuscript, full-text lines 205–238; see sourceRefs for each section's exact range","claim_support":["intro-02-part: terms, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; this does not certify empirical facts","2.1: terms, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; this does not certify empirical facts","2.1.1: terms, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; this does not certify empirical facts","2.1.2: terms, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; this does not certify empirical facts"],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2.1.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"theory-f1","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari's conditional formal propositions and nine-axis definitions","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Chinese source: §4.18–§4.19, lines 981–1028; precise F1 definition, lines 985–988; §5.2 nine axes, lines 1039–1059","claim_support":["2.1.2: constancy within the same representation as a condition for target recovery, and the fact that static recovery does not automatically imply dynamic closure.","The introduction's teaching example: SP is a nine-axis scale profile, not observation resolution alone."],"limits":["Conditional propositions and definitions within a model do not supply external empirical support.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2.1.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"snow-context","title":"John Snow, On the Mode of Communication of Cholera","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/72894/pg72894-images.html","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"Second edition, 1855; digital transcription dated 2024-02-07","locator":"Second edition, 1855, printed pages 39–41 and 51–52; Gutenberg text, The Cholera near Golden Square and The Pump-well in Broad Street","claim_support":["2.1.1: Snow describes death registration and investigations of drinking water, the interview on September 7, and removal of the pump handle the following day.","2.1.1: the original work acknowledges that incidence had fallen before the pump was disabled and that it was uncertain whether its water was still causing illness at that time."],"limits":["Only the relevant passages of the original work were read and used; registers and individual data were not rechecked.","The investigation account is neither a modern randomized intervention nor a G contract registered before results under this framework."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v2.1","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2.1, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2.1 / 2026-11-19","locator":"第二部分　边界与方法; SHA-256 2128af7cfa4c63b58207521a375aa34c2aea230d47e1c04a5afec3334e290d28","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2.1; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-2-1/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-2-1/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-2-1/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-2-1/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v2.1; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"9088d3d8c48f5f215093578dee52c518e1805812203991ce670a780658813366","source_sha256":"2128af7cfa4c63b58207521a375aa34c2aea230d47e1c04a5afec3334e290d28"}},"2.1":{"title":"2.1 Two-Stage Object Admission","lead":"First state the question clearly, then test it. Defining a candidate is like specifying the address of a research question; formal support depends on comparisons and results agreed beforehand. These two steps must not be reversed.","explanation":["Source location: Chinese source, lines 221–232; original anchor section-2-1. Short quotation: “Having all seven elements establishes only that the question has been stated clearly.” See the corresponding section on this page for the current source; the original research extract available for download preserves the historical manuscript.","For the structural object (§3.1), S*=〈B,X,R,I,T,SP,K〉lists, in order, boundaries, state variables, relations or transition constraints, interfaces, time window, scale profile, and identity criterion. The asterisk reminds readers that it is a candidate. Filling in all seven elements does not establish an optimal boundary, relative independence of the object, or the existence of a mechanism. Exploration remains possible when essential elements are missing, but the definition must not be falsely presented as clear.","The second stage governs only claims seeking formal test support in the name of the four empirical questions, and strong empirical claims of conclusion rules and conditional mechanisms that depend on such support. The four questions ask, respectively, about grouping gain, specified channels, historical conditional increments relative to the current representation, and representational closure and conversion. The historical-increment question in particular does not ask the general question of whether history ever influenced the present. Researchers must select the corresponding question rather than use one class of results to endorse another.","Before viewing the results, fix the following items in advance, change them no further once fixed, and leave a record that can be checked: the null model, a unique subtype, the single success criterion permitted for that subtype, controls and models, sample and extrapolation units, indicators, positive thresholds, decision and null-conclusion rules, and falsification and suspension conditions. Multiple targets or models also require fixing the primary target or aggregation rule, model selection or ensembling, and multiple-comparison rules. Fixing them in advance is intended not to make the world obey a form, but to prevent moving the question and success threshold after seeing the results.","A confirmatory or replication test gains eligibility for the corresponding positive support only after it is completed as set out in a pre-registration finalized in advance and passes its criteria. A falsification test supports only the pre-registered falsification or null judgment. Exploration, drafts, deviations after evidence, and undisclosed deviations must not enter branches of conclusion rules requiring formal support through a formal-support edge. Deviations may be honestly recorded and a new proposal made, but the exploratory history must not be erased.","Retain at least four statuses: the candidate object has been defined; not positively supported; the null model is supported; and grouping gain receives limited positive support. Failure to pass the positive rule may reflect a small effect, inadequate measurement, insufficient sample size, or an unsuitable model; it does not automatically mean absence. Supporting equivalence or sufficiency within a specified tolerance additionally requires advance null-conclusion rules and statistical power or sensitivity, with the corresponding criteria met. §2.1.1 preserves what was already grounded in earlier research; §2.1.2 prevents formal proof from being treated as real-world samples.","This book expressly limits the second-stage result to grouping gain. Defining an object, supporting object facts with reliable material, and obtaining support from a grouping-gain test for grouping gain within a task are three different things. A reliable membership record may support facts about a team and its members, without showing that grouping by this team predicts outcomes better than a matched ungrouped null model.","For example, an institution established by law cannot have its legal standing or membership denied merely because it has never undergone grouping-gain testing. Conversely, accurate prediction from statistical grouping does not establish a unified will. Settle specific standing under constitutive conditions, predictive advantage under a grouping-gain test, and causal effects through the relevant channels separately."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Structural object defined","leftText":"The question about the object can be described.","rightTitle":"Support from a formal test","rightText":"A specific empirical question fixed in advance receives limited support."},{"leftTitle":"Not positively supported","leftText":"The positive rule was not passed; the issue may remain unresolved.","rightTitle":"Null-model support","rightText":"Meaningful departures have been excluded under the prespecified null rule."},{"leftTitle":"Truth or falsity of an empirical effect","leftText":"Whether the difference being asked about exists in the world.","rightTitle":"Eligibility for formal support","rightText":"Whether the current information and research procedure suffice to provide the specified support under this framework."}],"boundaries":["This procedure is not a universal truth check for all domain knowledge, historical records, and ordinary understanding. Existing research must not be invalidated wholesale for lacking the pre-registration of a formal test.","An arbitrarily drawn threshold does not become scientific by being a threshold. Measurement validity, sample selection, competing explanations, and extrapolation conditions still require separate support.","Repeatedly adjusting a model on the same data does not turn it into independent confirmation. Retain its exploratory status before designing a genuinely new test."],"questions":["A set of results fails to reach the positive threshold, but no equivalence tolerance was pre-registered. Should its status be recorded as the null model is established?","After writing out all seven elements of the structural object, may one immediately state that the system has objective independent standing?","If the same information has already helped select a model, does subsequently locking the file count as finalizing before results?"],"diagramPath":"","caseLinks":[],"caseNotes":[],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"2.1","start_line":235,"end_line":246,"startLine":235,"endLine":246}],"outputId":"section-2-1","sourceHash":"fcf100a5c8688d1e0337c52750ce3ad21fb5dbcc250f97946602ce094345f825","argumentSteps":["First premise: analysts may initially choose boundaries and representations, but the choice itself is not evidence that the choice has empirical standing.","Second premise: if comparison methods, thresholds, or models change with results already seen, a positive result may arise from retrospective selection and cannot be interpreted under the original pre-registration.","Connection: first identify a candidate through the definition of the structural object, then fix the test's pre-registration for the requested subtype before the evidence, making both the question and the decision procedure reviewable.","Conclusion: eligibility attaches to specific tests satisfying the pre-registration, not to blank templates or to every completely filled-out candidate.","Responsibility for null judgments: not rejecting the null model is not equivalent to supporting the null model in a prespecified sense. Equivalence/sufficiency, tolerance, and identification sensitivity must each satisfy their requirements."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: defining a queue does not prove that grouping is useful","text":"Suppose researchers want to know whether grouping service requests by interface predicts waiting time more accurately than not grouping them. First state the boundary, state variables, relations or transition constraints, interface, time window, scale profile, and identity criterion; then fix the baseline, error measure, minimum improvement, and evaluation rules before an independent evaluation window. If grouping is changed after evaluation results are seen, the resulting pattern may be a new candidate, but must not masquerade as confirmation finalized beforehand. No study has been carried out here, and no numerical improvement has been invented.","assumptions":"The comparison concerns grouping gain, not the effect of a causal intervention. Actual information is required to form a formal test.","boundary":"This is a teaching example of the procedure. It does not claim to have passed a grouping-gain test or infer improved service quality from a complete candidate."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Teaching conditional counterexample: after only two very noisy measurements fail to reach a positive threshold, someone announces that the effect is zero.","effect":"Without an advance null rule and sensitivity, the result is at most not positively supported. Failure with a small sample does not automatically exclude meaningful differences.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Teaching conditional counterexample: a researcher uses the same evaluation results to choose one of the ten best groupings, then describes it as the “sole pre-registered model.”","effect":"This violates finalization before evidence. Its honest status is exploration with a record of deviations, not confirmatory support.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"A set of results fails to reach the positive threshold, but no equivalence tolerance was pre-registered. Should its status be recorded as the null model is established?","answer":"No. It may be recorded as not positively supported or unresolved; a zero conclusion requires additional rules and sufficient sensitivity.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"After writing out all seven elements of the structural object, may one immediately state that the system has objective independent standing?","answer":"No. The definition of the structural object fixes only what the candidate refers to; relative independence, grouping gain, and other empirical claims require corresponding comparisons and evidence.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"If the same information has already helped select a model, does subsequently locking the file count as finalizing before results?","answer":"Not for material already used to select the model. Disclose exposure to the material and its exploratory use, and finalize a new pre-registration when suitable new confirmatory material is used.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[],"diagramBrief":"This page uses the current text to explain the v2 distinction between object facts and grouping gain; the original research figure remains available among the historical-material downloads.","applicability":{"realCase":"not_applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"This section concerns procedural conditions for formal support. A construction can clearly show differences in status; actual historical material is placed in the next section to test the boundary of “old evidence status.” This section does not fabricate an actual pre-registered test."},"openQuestions":[],"taskId":"S-2-1","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical Chinese research manuscript, full-text lines 205–238; see sourceRefs for each section's exact range","claim_support":["intro-02-part: terms, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; this does not certify empirical facts","2.1: terms, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; this does not certify empirical facts","2.1.1: terms, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; this does not certify empirical facts","2.1.2: terms, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; this does not certify empirical facts"],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2.1.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"theory-f1","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari's conditional formal propositions and nine-axis definitions","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Chinese source: §4.18–§4.19, lines 981–1028; precise F1 definition, lines 985–988; §5.2 nine axes, lines 1039–1059","claim_support":["2.1.2: constancy within the same representation as a condition for target recovery, and the fact that static recovery does not automatically imply dynamic closure.","The introduction's teaching example: SP is a nine-axis scale profile, not observation resolution alone."],"limits":["Conditional propositions and definitions within a model do not supply external empirical support.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2.1.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"snow-context","title":"John Snow, On the Mode of Communication of Cholera","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/72894/pg72894-images.html","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"Second edition, 1855; digital transcription dated 2024-02-07","locator":"Second edition, 1855, printed pages 39–41 and 51–52; Gutenberg text, The Cholera near Golden Square and The Pump-well in Broad Street","claim_support":["2.1.1: Snow describes death registration and investigations of drinking water, the interview on September 7, and removal of the pump handle the following day.","2.1.1: the original work acknowledges that incidence had fallen before the pump was disabled and that it was uncertain whether its water was still causing illness at that time."],"limits":["Only the relevant passages of the original work were read and used; registers and individual data were not rechecked.","The investigation account is neither a modern randomized intervention nor a G contract registered before results under this framework."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v2.1","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2.1, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2.1 / 2026-11-19","locator":"2.1　两阶段对象准入; Chinese source lines 221–232; SHA-256 2128af7cfa4c63b58207521a375aa34c2aea230d47e1c04a5afec3334e290d28","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2.1; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-2-1/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-2-1/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-2-1/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-2-1/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v2.1; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"6db43ae9dec763e3270e92f6ec9e9d1fc6af7add968fc7a8d4a019899c7b15ae","source_sha256":"2128af7cfa4c63b58207521a375aa34c2aea230d47e1c04a5afec3334e290d28"}},"2.1.1":{"title":"2.1.1 Domain evidence and eligibility for formal tests","lead":"Earlier research can be valuable while lacking eligibility for formal tests in this framework. Preserve what it originally established; if stronger standing is sought, add a genuinely new test.","explanation":["Source location: Chinese source lines 233–240; source anchor section-2-1-1; brief quotation: “nor do they lose their originally supported content merely because they have not yet been converted into a formal test.” See the corresponding section on this page for the current source; the downloadable original research extract preserves the historical draft.","This section prevents two opposite misuses. One rewrites past exploration or historical reconstruction as pre-registered confirmation. The other discards all locatable historical material and research conclusions because today's forms did not exist then. The source permits retaining descriptions, explanations, or bounded causal conclusions actually supported by the research design, while prohibiting fabrication of a pre-registration from before the results.","“Existing finding → source of a new question/model → new pre-registration → test with new material” is a viable continuation. Independent material, a later time window, or appropriate replication may offer a new evaluation opportunity, but independence must be explained: one cannot tune a model using all the data and then relabel part of it as a previously unseen validation set. A later window still requires checking changes in objects and measurements.","If no feasible new test exists, state the existing domain conclusions and relations awaiting testing, rather than forcing them into a formal test. Formal proof, textual interpretation, and normative argument can also contribute, using their respective grounds: axioms and derivations, versions and context, and value premises and reasons. They supply one another with questions and constraints, without impersonating real mechanisms or authorization.","This section uses Snow's case solely to examine the coexistence of historical material and current eligibility. The field investigation, interviews, and limitations in the 1855 text are historical evidence open to criticism; they do not lose their content because a contemporary pre-registration for a formal test is absent. Nor have we used them to reconstruct a nonexistent pre-outcome record."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Domain evidence","leftText":"Supports bounded conclusions under the original research design.","rightTitle":"Formal G eligibility","rightText":"The standing of a specific test obtained under this framework's pre-registration finalized before the evidence."},{"leftTitle":"Reinterpreting old material","leftText":"Can propose different models and questions.","rightTitle":"Fabricating past pre-registration","rightText":"Disguises a retrospective finding as a pre-outcome commitment, changing the status of the material."},{"leftTitle":"Independent analysis","leftText":"Another algorithm may reveal implementation problems.","rightTitle":"Independent confirmatory material","rightText":"Its evaluation has not already been used by the same model-selection process; its provenance and dependencies must still be stated."}],"boundaries":["Snow's material on water exposure and the effect of removing the pump handle concern two different causal questions. Support for the former does not mean the latter has been separately identified.","Historical evidence need not become a formal test to be discussed; likewise, being “historically famous” does not exempt a particular causal narrative from criticism.","This lesson did not recheck the original death registers or obtain individual-level data. The case relies on relevant passages in Snow's original work, rather than repeating a historical epidemiological study."],"questions":["Can an investigation from 1855 without a record of a formal test still serve as material for the historical question of water exposure?","If a researcher cannot obtain any new material, must earlier findings be rejected?","Can “the fall in incidence after removal of the pump handle” be taken directly as the net effect of removing it?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-2-1/figures/section-2-1-1.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"Broad Street, London, 1854: preserve the evidence and the differences between questions","href":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/72894/pg72894-images.html"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"Broad Street, London, 1854: preserve the evidence and the differences between questions","text":"In the second edition of 1855, Snow reported consulting death registers and investigating drinking-water habits; he presented the circumstances to the parish on September 7, 1854, and the pump handle was removed the next day. The original also states that incidence had already fallen substantially before use of the well water ceased, so it could not establish whether the water still had a disease-causing effect at that time. Here we preserve the distinction between “what the investigation reported” and “whether the net intervention effect was identified.”","sourceLabel":"John Snow, On the Mode of Communication of Cholera, 1855, 2nd ed.","sourceUrl":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/72894/pg72894-images.html","sourceLocator":"Printed pages 39–41 and 51–52; the passages The Cholera near Golden Square and The Pump-well in Broad Street","boundary":"This is the author's historical account of his investigation at the time, not this lesson's revalidation of every cause of the disease. The material can support a bounded exposure explanation; temporal sequence alone cannot estimate the effect of removing the handle, nor constitute a modern G pre-registration.","href":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/72894/pg72894-images.html"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"2.1.1","start_line":247,"end_line":254,"startLine":247,"endLine":254}],"outputId":"section-2-1-1","sourceHash":"fcf100a5c8688d1e0337c52750ce3ad21fb5dbcc250f97946602ce094345f825","argumentSteps":["Premise: the way existing material was produced is already historical; its pre-event or post-event status cannot be retrospectively changed today.","Connection: evaluating the scope of evidence and evaluating eligibility under a particular framework are separate questions, so they can be recorded separately without canceling each other.","Conclusion one: preserve domain content actually supported by the design while stating that it has not automatically obtained the positive support of a formal test.","Conclusion two: if new eligibility is sought, first set down a new question and evaluate it with suitable new material; if no feasible new test exists, retain the domain conclusion and its pending-test status.","Responsibilities: sources supply historical facts and research design; the present explanation supplies the mapping and the inference ceiling. Normative claims and authorization have separate grounds."],"teachingExamples":[],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"real_counterevidence","text":"Snow himself noted that incidence had already fallen markedly before use of the well water ceased, and stated that it was impossible to determine whether active disease-causing matter remained in the well when it was closed.","effect":"This limits the narrative that “removing the handle caused the entire decline,” but does not also invalidate his investigation of water exposure.","sourceIds":["snow-context"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Can an investigation from 1855 without a record of a formal test still serve as material for the historical question of water exposure?","answer":"Yes, according to its investigative design, account, and limitations; do not fabricate a G pre-registration for that time or automatically award formal support under this framework.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"If a researcher cannot obtain any new material, must earlier findings be rejected?","answer":"No. Retain originally supported domain conclusions and explain that the new formal eligibility has not been established.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Can “the fall in incidence after removal of the pump handle” be taken directly as the net effect of removing it?","answer":"No. A counterfactual comparison is needed, addressing competing explanations such as the decline having already begun, population departure, and limitations of the material.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-2-1/figures/section-2-1-1.png","alt":"Historical/existing material retains its actual research status; domain conclusions retain bounded support under the original design; original limitations and counterexamples continue to constrain conclusions; findings generate new questions without fabricating old pre-registrations; a new pre-registration plus suitable new material separates exploration from evaluation; support or unresolved status for a new test does not retroactively alter the old research. Concept diagram based on 2.1.1; Snow's original work is only a historical example, and the diagram does not represent a completed new formal test.","caption":"Concept diagram based on 2.1.1; Snow's original work is only a historical example, and the diagram does not represent a completed new formal test."}],"diagramBrief":"The figure separates old material from new eligibility into two routes. When switching between “suitable new material available/unavailable,” the lower route may stop at a new question, while the existing bounded support on the upper route remains.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"Historical material with explicit dates, provenance, and acknowledged limitations can directly show that domain evidence and eligibility under a new framework do not cancel each other."},"openQuestions":["Reestimating the net effect of removing the handle using a modern comparative design would require fuller material on exposure, departures, onset times, and comparable counterfactuals; this lesson does not have that material."],"taskId":"S-2-1","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical Chinese research manuscript, full-text lines 205–238; see sourceRefs for each section's exact range","claim_support":["intro-02-part: terms, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; this does not certify empirical facts","2.1: terms, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; this does not certify empirical facts","2.1.1: terms, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; this does not certify empirical facts","2.1.2: terms, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; this does not certify empirical facts"],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2.1.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"theory-f1","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari's conditional formal propositions and nine-axis definitions","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Chinese source: §4.18–§4.19, lines 981–1028; precise F1 definition, lines 985–988; §5.2 nine axes, lines 1039–1059","claim_support":["2.1.2: constancy within the same representation as a condition for target recovery, and the fact that static recovery does not automatically imply dynamic closure.","The introduction's teaching example: SP is a nine-axis scale profile, not observation resolution alone."],"limits":["Conditional propositions and definitions within a model do not supply external empirical support.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2.1.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"snow-context","title":"John Snow, On the Mode of Communication of Cholera","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/72894/pg72894-images.html","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"Second edition, 1855; digital transcription dated 2024-02-07","locator":"Second edition, 1855, printed pages 39–41 and 51–52; Gutenberg text, The Cholera near Golden Square and The Pump-well in Broad Street","claim_support":["2.1.1: Snow describes death registration and investigations of drinking water, the interview on September 7, and removal of the pump handle the following day.","2.1.1: the original work acknowledges that incidence had fallen before the pump was disabled and that it was uncertain whether its water was still causing illness at that time."],"limits":["Only the relevant passages of the original work were read and used; registers and individual data were not rechecked.","The investigation account is neither a modern randomized intervention nor a G contract registered before results under this framework."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v2.1","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2.1, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2.1 / 2026-11-19","locator":"2.1.1 领域证据与正式检验的资格; Chinese source lines 233–240; SHA-256 2128af7cfa4c63b58207521a375aa34c2aea230d47e1c04a5afec3334e290d28","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2.1; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-2-1/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-2-1/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-2-1/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-2-1/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v2.1; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"19d115f4e5cb73152cd79c72f94e03fdffb021fb1db24b6c7844e6b7abcba3e2","source_sha256":"2128af7cfa4c63b58207521a375aa34c2aea230d47e1c04a5afec3334e290d28"}},"2.1.2":{"title":"2.1.2 Connecting formal conclusions and empirical cases","lead":"Mathematics can rigorously answer “does this hold under these assumptions?” Real-world application must also answer “does this object satisfy these assumptions?” One answer may be determinate while the other remains unresolved.","explanation":["Source location: Chinese source lines 241–246; source anchor section-2-1-2; brief quotation: “more computations do not increase independent empirical support.” See the corresponding section on this page for the current source; the downloadable original research extract preserves the historical draft.","A formal proposition first fixes the state domain, mapping, target, permitted operations, and logical premises. A proof explains how the conclusion follows from those premises; exhaustive checking over a finite domain checks every declared case; numerical checking must additionally specify precision, boundaries, and implementation error. These three can cross-check errors, but computation does not generate new real-world samples.","Connection to reality must be established item by item: what observable quantities the model variables correspond to; whether measurement and representation preserve distinctions needed for the target; which premises already have supporting material; whether error and the applicable range are acceptable; and what counterexample would overturn the mapping. The F layer in source §4.18–§4.19 particularly stresses that static recovery, dynamic closure, and intervention transferability are different propositions.","A formal counterexample suffices to refute the unconditional inference it targets. For instance, “an aggregate can always recover each component” fails with a single explicit mathematical counterexample; no factory or hospital experiment is needed first. But the counterexample does not show that every real-world aggregate is useless. If the target becomes the total, the same representation may be entirely sufficient.","Real-world adoption carries six independent responsibilities: formal feasibility; existence of an algorithm; computability within budget; timely availability of material; correct actual execution; and authority to adopt it. Passing one step does not imply passing the next. Knowing a decision rule exists may leave it both impossible to compute in time and impossible to supply with timely inputs; even computability and executability do not automatically confer authority to act upon people."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Proof established","leftText":"The inference is valid within the declared axioms and semantics.","rightTitle":"Real-world fit established","rightText":"Sufficient material supports the variable mapping and premises for the target object."},{"leftTitle":"Exhaustive checking of four states","leftText":"Covers every state of this particular finite model.","rightTitle":"Four real-world observations","rightText":"Material from the world, with issues of sampling, measurement, and dependence."},{"leftTitle":"An algorithm exists","leftText":"There is a rule for obtaining an answer.","rightTitle":"Timely, correct, and authorized execution","rightText":"Computational budget, material, implementation, and permission are separately satisfied."}],"boundaries":["The mathematical counterexample already refutes “any aggregate can recover any detail,” but does not refute sufficiency when the target is the total.","Allowing additional information, such as x₂, changes the arrangement for permitted operations and representation; the new recoverability cannot be counted as success under the old arrangement.","Exhaustive checking over a finite domain must list the complete domain; randomly sampling many states is still not proof over an infinite domain. Numerical near-zero does not automatically mean exact zero."],"questions":["Does running the same four-state exhaustive check one million times add one million independent pieces of empirical support?","In the counterexample above, can x₁ be recovered if additional access to x₂ is allowed?","If a rule has been proved to exist, can one thereby declare that it can automatically go live the next day?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-2-1/figures/section-2-1-2.png","caseLinks":[],"caseNotes":[],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"2.1.2","start_line":255,"end_line":260,"startLine":255,"endLine":260}],"outputId":"section-2-1-2","sourceHash":"fcf100a5c8688d1e0337c52750ce3ad21fb5dbcc250f97946602ce094345f825","argumentSteps":["Mathematical premises: state domain X={0,1}²; representation π(x₁,x₂)=x₁+x₂; target q(x₁,x₂)=x₁; the recovery function g may read only π.","Take x=(0,1) and x′=(1,0). Then π(x)=π(x′)=1, whereas q(x)=0 and q(x′)=1.","If a g correct for every state existed, g(1) would have to equal both 0 and 1, a contradiction. Thus, on this complete state domain and under this representation, no single-valued function exactly recovers q.","If the target changes to qΣ=x₁+x₂, then gΣ(z)=z gives exact recovery. The conclusion is target-relative; it does not say all aggregation fails.","Real-world responsibility: interpreting x₁ and x₂ as real quantities requires separate support for the state domain, measurement, input availability, and permitted operations; the four-state proof supplies neither real-world occurrence frequencies nor intervention effects."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Mathematical construction: the same sum, two different first components","text":"The four states are (0,0), (0,1), (1,0), and (1,1); their π values are 0, 1, 1, and 2, and their q values are 0, 0, 1, and 1, respectively. The target is not constant within the group π=1, so reading only the sum cannot recover the first component. If the target becomes the sum, every representation value directly supplies the answer. This is exact integer computation over a complete finite domain.","assumptions":"The domain contains only these four states; the recovery function is single-valued and reads only π, without identity labels or additional measurements.","boundary":"This is an example of mathematical recoverability, not an estimate of real-world effects, human types, or statistical error; it cannot establish that a real system happens to satisfy the model."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Mathematical boundary: q=x₁ is not recoverable on the original four-state domain; if the admissible domain shrinks to {(0,0),(1,1)}, each value of the same π corresponds to only one state.","effect":"Restricting the admissible domain changes the premises and therefore recoverability; failure on the original domain cannot be generalized to every restricted domain.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Does running the same four-state exhaustive check one million times add one million independent pieces of empirical support?","answer":"No. It may help reveal implementation errors, but it still checks the same model and produces no new real-world observations.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"In the counterexample above, can x₁ be recovered if additional access to x₂ is allowed?","answer":"Yes: x₁=π−x₂. But this expands the input conditions; it does not overturn the original impossibility of recovery when “only π may be read.”","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"If a rule has been proved to exist, can one thereby declare that it can automatically go live the next day?","answer":"No. An algorithm, budget, timely material, correct implementation, and permission to adopt it still each need support.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-2-1/figures/section-2-1-2.png","alt":"State x=(0,1), target q=0; shared representation π=1, with only this sum readable; state x′=(1,0), target q=1; recovering the first component requires g(1) to be both 0 and 1; recovering the total requires only gΣ(1)=1. Mathematical construction diagram, exact domain {0,1}²; represents no real-world sample or effect, and is based on 2.1.2 and the target-relativity of the conditional formal proposition “Task Recovery.”","caption":"Mathematical construction diagram, exact domain {0,1}²; represents no real-world sample or effect, and is based on 2.1.2 and the target-relativity of the conditional formal proposition “Task Recovery.”"}],"diagramBrief":"Combine the two states with the same representation to show the recovery conflict. An interaction may switch the target between “first component/total” or change the admissible domain; changes must be accompanied by explicit changes to the premises.","applicability":{"realCase":"not_applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"This section directly addresses formal validity and empirical connection. A complete finite mathematical construction suffices to test an unconditional inference; unrelated historical facts are not added as purported empirical evidence."},"openQuestions":[],"taskId":"S-2-1","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical Chinese research manuscript, full-text lines 205–238; see sourceRefs for each section's exact range","claim_support":["intro-02-part: terms, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; this does not certify empirical facts","2.1: terms, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; this does not certify empirical facts","2.1.1: terms, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; this does not certify empirical facts","2.1.2: terms, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; this does not certify empirical facts"],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2.1.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"theory-f1","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari's conditional formal propositions and nine-axis definitions","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Chinese source: §4.18–§4.19, lines 981–1028; precise F1 definition, lines 985–988; §5.2 nine axes, lines 1039–1059","claim_support":["2.1.2: constancy within the same representation as a condition for target recovery, and the fact that static recovery does not automatically imply dynamic closure.","The introduction's teaching example: SP is a nine-axis scale profile, not observation resolution alone."],"limits":["Conditional propositions and definitions within a model do not supply external empirical support.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2.1.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"snow-context","title":"John Snow, On the Mode of Communication of Cholera","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/72894/pg72894-images.html","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"Second edition, 1855; digital transcription dated 2024-02-07","locator":"Second edition, 1855, printed pages 39–41 and 51–52; Gutenberg text, The Cholera near Golden Square and The Pump-well in Broad Street","claim_support":["2.1.1: Snow describes death registration and investigations of drinking water, the interview on September 7, and removal of the pump handle the following day.","2.1.1: the original work acknowledges that incidence had fallen before the pump was disabled and that it was uncertain whether its water was still causing illness at that time."],"limits":["Only the relevant passages of the original work were read and used; registers and individual data were not rechecked.","The investigation account is neither a modern randomized intervention nor a G contract registered before results under this framework."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v2.1","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2.1, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2.1 / 2026-11-19","locator":"2.1.2　形式结论与经验个案的接续; Chinese source lines 241–246; SHA-256 2128af7cfa4c63b58207521a375aa34c2aea230d47e1c04a5afec3334e290d28","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2.1; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-2-1/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-2-1/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-2-1/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-2-1/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v2.1; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"02ad0f6c348f0e925b65d1ab4d9fa66396b4e98e5e1c97c9b508214ba0380210","source_sha256":"2128af7cfa4c63b58207521a375aa34c2aea230d47e1c04a5afec3334e290d28"}},"2.2":{"title":"2.2 Roles of propositions and responsibilities of argument","lead":"A sentence such as “this system works, so it should be used” mixes at least object definition, an effect judgment, a value choice, and permission to act. Break the sentence apart first to know what each part requires as support.","explanation":["Source location: Chinese source lines 247–272; source anchor section-2-2; brief quotation: “The identifiers serve to distinguish burdens of argument; they do not mean that all kinds of proposition have the same truth conditions.” See the corresponding section on this page for the current source; the downloadable original research extract preserves the historical draft.","D answers “what does this word refer to here?” Specifying meanings for “structure” or “repair” supports consistent judgment but does not establish that the corresponding object exists in the world or has been repaired. G answers specific empirical questions: which object, which comparison, and whether a difference under which conditions exceeds a threshold. The four empirical questions are types of question; specific tests carry particular factual assertions. A fact may be true despite insufficient current support, and a procedurally complete inquiry may still reach a wrong conclusion through a mistaken model. Formal eligibility cannot define the world.","E constrains how much is known: measurement blind spots, competing explanations, residuals, and conditions for cross-scale transfer must be retained. N makes value choices explicit: what to protect and what to restrict. These have different responsibilities; acknowledging epistemic limits does not automatically establish a value, and choosing to protect someone does not automatically establish every description of their circumstances. O organizes fixing descriptions, checking evidence, stating normative premises, authorization, option comparison, bounded execution, review, stopping, withdrawal, and remedy into procedures. It constrains how one may proceed without replacing factual grounds.","H is especially easy to misread. It is a marker for adaptation to the human domain, not a sixth kind of truth wholly exclusive of definitions, empirical claims, and norms. “Meaning and Coordination,” “Power, Mediation, and Reflexivity,” and “Historical Trace Carriers” are empirical hypotheses requiring case-specific evidence; “Action Uptake and Classification of Responsibility” is an operational typology; “Institutional Write-Back” is a classification rule; “Open-Ended Undertaking” is a normative boundary. Belonging together to the human-domain clauses does not make them all a priori axioms of human nature, still less make human meaning and institutional rules the roots of all natural processes.","F consists of conditional formal propositions established by proof under explicit state domains, mappings, targets, operation sets, and mathematical premises. Mathematical counterexamples can defeat universal inferences that exceed their premises; whether reality satisfies those premises still requires evidence. The five propositions in source §4.18 and the task families and dynamic refinements in §4.19 respectively show boundaries for recovery, dynamics, intervention, composition, and approximation; their uses cannot be treated as identical merely because they are all formulas.","C does not add another class of facts about the world alongside D and G; it supplies rules for how results are determined and under which mechanism conditions they are used, with “Normative Bridging” (§4.14.1) providing the bridge to normative procedures. Common method constraints are not additional laws of the world either. Adding more numbered labels to a conclusion increases its locatability, not its independent evidence.","The minimal bridge to action contains six items: a descriptive or explanatory conclusion, explicit normative premises, current evidence, lawful authorization, an action ceiling, and stopping or withdrawal conditions. If any is missing, the relevant real-world intervention should stop. Description, candidate generation, evidence gathering, inaction, or proposals for reversible probes may continue; proposing an option does not itself authorize its implementation. These are necessary review items, not a guarantee that listing them automatically satisfies every domain's legal, professional, or safety requirements."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Semantic validity","leftText":"An object fits a classification by definition.","rightTitle":"Empirical validity","rightText":"The object and corresponding relations are supported by material, not generated by definition."},{"leftTitle":"H domain marker","leftText":"Indicates which kinds of additional conditions apply to human objects.","rightTitle":"Logical role","rightText":"Further determine whether it is empirical, classificatory, procedural, or normative."},{"leftTitle":"Conclusion rules","leftText":"Constrain how existing grounds may be used.","rightTitle":"Additional evidence","rightText":"Requires independent materials and cannot increase merely through numbering."},{"leftTitle":"Explanation/diagnosis","leftText":"Describes what may happen and why.","rightTitle":"Authority to act","rightText":"Qualification to take a specified action toward a target; it does not follow from depth of understanding."}],"boundaries":["A valid proof does not establish that a measurement mapping is true; meeting a procedural requirement does not establish the existence of a substantive causal relation.","The action bridge is a minimal set of necessary conditions, not a complete administrative, medical, or legal permit; this lesson performs no real-world diagnosis or disposition.","Value premises may be criticized and revised, but renaming them ‘objective mechanisms’ must not exempt them from public justification."],"questions":["‘“Open-Ended Undertaking” is a normative boundary, so “Meaning and Coordination” also needs no empirical materials.’ Why does this inference fail?","Does a test that has received formal support define whether an effect is factually true or false?","If stopping conditions are missing but an option is considered highly beneficial, does the bridge permit immediate implementation?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-2-2/figures/section-2-2.png","caseLinks":[],"caseNotes":[],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"2.2","start_line":261,"end_line":286,"startLine":261,"endLine":286}],"outputId":"section-2-2","sourceHash":"fcf100a5c8688d1e0337c52750ce3ad21fb5dbcc250f97946602ce094345f825","argumentSteps":["Premise: definitional conventions, empirical assertions, mathematical consequences, and value commitments have different conditions for validity; a uniform label of ‘proven’ must not replace them.","Decomposition: separate a compound statement into identifiable clauses and specify each clause's object and role; for H, also state whether it is empirical, operational, classificatory, or normative.","Connection: definitions require semantic consistency; empirical claims require designs and materials; formal claims require premises and proofs; norms require values and reasons; action additionally requires permissions and stopping mechanisms. Satisfying one does not complete the rest.","Conclusion: moving from ‘the explanation is credible’ to ‘action may be taken’ requires completing the action bridge; a missing component blocks only dispositions that depend on it, without automatically withdrawing independently established facts or proofs.","Evidential responsibility: the framework's own D, C, or methodological descriptions must not serve as object facts; procedural compliance or normative justification must not be presented as proof of a causal mechanism."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: separating ‘accurate, therefore mandatory’","text":"Consider: ‘We define a tool with prediction error below e as accurate; tool A meets this criterion on a specified test set; therefore everyone should be compelled to use it, and its developers have the right to deploy it directly.’ The first sentence is a D-style semantic convention; the second is an empirical assertion whose test design and scope require checking; the ‘should be compelled’ in the third requires N-style value reasons, while deployment qualification requires authorization and operational procedures. Neither follows from the error result. A proof of an algorithmic error bound would instead be an F-style conditional conclusion, and whether reality meets its assumptions would still need checking.","assumptions":"The tool, threshold e, and tests are hypothetical; they do not represent a real product or completed experiment.","boundary":"Used only to distinguish argumentative responsibilities; it does not establish any tool's reliability, justification, or permission for deployment."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Teaching boundary: stipulating that ‘every operation called repair is beneficial’, then using the fact that an operation is recorded as repair to prove that it is beneficial.","effect":"This inserts a normative conclusion into a definition and then supports it circularly; pre-specified goals, observed results, and value assessments should be separated.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Teaching boundary: someone predicts a team's reactions most accurately and therefore claims permission to reassign its members.","effect":"Epistemic advantage does not generate authorization; stop at explanation or a proposal rather than cross the permission boundary.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"‘“Open-Ended Undertaking” is a normative boundary, so “Meaning and Coordination” also needs no empirical materials.’ Why does this inference fail?","answer":"H marks domain adaptation, not one uniform logical type. “Meaning and Coordination” is an empirical hypothesis requiring case-specific evidence; the normative status of “Open-Ended Undertaking” must not be transferred to it.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Does a test that has received formal support define whether an effect is factually true or false?","answer":"No. Specific tests and procedures govern how much support we may give; whether an effect exists in the world is the question they seek to answer, not something a record creates.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"If stopping conditions are missing but an option is considered highly beneficial, does the bridge permit immediate implementation?","answer":"No. The relevant disposition should be suspended; options may be completed, a reversible probe proposed, or inaction retained. A judgment of benefit also requires its own evidence and normative reasons.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-2-2/figures/section-2-2.png","alt":"D/E: semantic and epistemic boundaries; empirical clauses in G/H: comparisons, materials, and failure modes; F: mathematical premises and proof; limited descriptive/explanatory conclusions cannot authorize action by themselves; N plus current evidence and lawful authorization still requires an action ceiling; O plus stopping/withdrawal conditions governs review of the relevant real-world disposition. A responsibility diagram based on 2.2; H is not a single logical type, and C is a rule for use. Arrows do not represent empirical causation.","caption":"A responsibility diagram based on 2.2; H is not a single logical type, and C is a rule for use. Arrows do not represent empirical causation."}],"diagramBrief":"The upper layer shows three different kinds of grounds, and the lower layer shows the action bridge. Selecting ‘authorization missing’ blocks only the disposition; it does not erase an existing mathematical proof or independent facts.","applicability":{"realCase":"not_applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"This section provides argumentative roles and necessary bridging conditions; decomposing teaching statements directly demonstrates non-equivalence. Real-world cases cannot independently validate classificatory conventions, so historical facts are not forced into the lesson."},"openQuestions":[],"taskId":"S-2-2","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Lines 239–264 of the complete historical Chinese research manuscript; see sourceRefs for each section's precise range","claim_support":["2.2: Terms, contracts, burdens of argument, and source boundaries; not a basis for certifying empirical facts"],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2.1.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"theory-v2.1","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2.1, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2.1 / 2026-11-19","locator":"2.2　命题角色与论证责任; Chinese source lines 247–272; SHA-256 2128af7cfa4c63b58207521a375aa34c2aea230d47e1c04a5afec3334e290d28","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2.1; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-2-2/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-2-2/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-2-2/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-2-2/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v2.1; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"07684f87d29f77e36ac0d595e1135398b93628bcedfdd92b9803ddc05a0b94cd","source_sha256":"2128af7cfa4c63b58207521a375aa34c2aea230d47e1c04a5afec3334e290d28"}},"2.3":{"title":"2.3 Four Types of Dependency Must Be Kept Separate","lead":"‘It is needed’ may mean that a conclusion cannot be inferred without it, cannot be published, applies only to a domain, or constrains action. Draw these four meanings separately, or procedures will be mistaken for causes and norms for evidence.","explanation":["Chinese source location: lines 273–285; source anchor section-2-3; short quotation: ‘Only hard inferential premises enter the inferential acyclic graph that leads from premises to inferred conclusions’. The current source appears in the corresponding subsection on this page; the downloadable original research excerpt preserves the historical manuscript.","An inferential premise is a hard premise of inference: without it, the stated conclusion cannot be obtained in this disclosed argument. For example, inferring ‘all states with the same representation have the same target value, so a target-recovery function exists’ requires the premise that target values are constant across states sharing a representation. It may enter the inferential acyclic graph that leads from premises to inferred conclusions, with direction consistently understood as running from a premise to the conclusion that depends on it.","A method requirement is a requirement concerning evidence, causation, analogy, sources, or domain classification: such requirements specify how this judgment must be recorded, examined, and published. A missing requirement removes the corresponding publication qualification, but a method requirement is not an additional empirical cause in the world. Rules fixed in advance provide a fixed basis for evaluation under the original question, while actual execution and deviations still need checking; a document that has been set down does not cause the physical effect.","A domain restriction is a kind of specialization: restricting a general proposition to a domain, object, or subtype requires listing additional conditions and scope. It does not automatically establish the existence of the domain's object, nor turn classificatory clauses into supporting materials. Objects of application record which subjects, actions, or propositions a norm or procedure constrains; a privacy rule applying to a record does not, in reverse, establish the truth of the facts claimed in that record.","The original table labels the four relations respectively ‘yes, no, no, no’ for entry into the inferential acyclic graph. This does not make the latter three unimportant; it requires separate graphs or layers to preserve their different meanings. A publication process may include cycles of review, return, and resubmission; this is procedural structure. Procedural cycles must not be misread as empirical-inference cycles merely to fit everything into a single acyclic graph.","A case may involve multiple relations simultaneously, each of which should be recorded separately. A factual claim might inferentially use comparison data, need to meet requirements set down in advance for publication, be restricted to a subtype in scope, and be normatively constrained in action. It must not be represented by a single untyped ‘dependency’ edge. If a method requirement is not met, first remove the corresponding publication or support qualification; if a hard premise fails, the inferred conclusion depending on it loses that basis. The two failures affect different things."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Inferentially necessary","leftText":"Without this premise, this argument fails.","rightTitle":"Necessary for publication","rightText":"If the procedure is unmet, the claim must not be published at that level; this does not directly change what is true or false in the world."},{"leftTitle":"Domain specialization","leftText":"Adds scope and conditions.","rightTitle":"Empirical support","rightText":"Materials support specific relations in the specialized object; these two functions are not interchangeable."},{"leftTitle":"Normative applicability","leftText":"Specifies who or what must be constrained.","rightTitle":"Factual verification","rightText":"Determines whether a particular description is supported; being protected does not mean the description is established."}],"boundaries":["The acyclic-graph restriction concerns argumentative dependencies; it prohibits neither feedback in real systems nor review cycles in operational procedures.","A method requirement must not be deleted because it is absent from the inferential acyclic graph: it still determines whether the corresponding conclusion may be published and used with a particular status.","If a condition initially treated as procedural is also written into a mathematical proposition's premises, separately state its formal role in that proposition and its role as a method requirement; the two uses of the same wording must not silently collapse into one edge."],"questions":["How should a valid mathematical derivation under given premises be recorded when the conditions for sources are unmet?","What edge is mistaken in ‘privacy rules apply to this interview, therefore everything the interviewee says is true’?","May a rule specialized to human organizations be inferred backward through a domain-restriction relation into a law of all natural systems?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-2-3/figures/section-2-3.png","caseLinks":[],"caseNotes":[],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"2.3","start_line":287,"end_line":299,"startLine":287,"endLine":299}],"outputId":"section-2-3","sourceHash":"fcf100a5c8688d1e0337c52750ce3ad21fb5dbcc250f97946602ce094345f825","argumentSteps":["Premise: different ‘dependencies’ pose different counterfactual questions: does removing it break the inference, disqualify publication, change scope, or make action impermissible?","Classification: answer those questions for each edge and retain its relation type. A node may participate in several relation types, but sharing a name must not merge the meanings of its edges.","Connection: hard inferential premises enter the inferential acyclic graph leading from premises to conclusions only where the validity of the conclusion is what is being explained. Methodological, specialization, and applicability relations are stored separately.","Conclusion: layered graphs prevent inferring real mechanisms from publication requirements or real facts from normative applicability; they also identify which inferred conclusion should be suspended when a premise is compromised.","Responsibility: stating a type is not itself proof; every hard premise still requires the corresponding derivation or materials, and method requirements must be assessed against the actual conditions."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: one conclusion about a recovery function and four relations","text":"Proposition P: if π(x)=π(x′) always implies q(x)=q(x′), then g can be defined on the image of π such that q=g∘π. Set g(z) to the q value of any preimage; the constancy premise ensures that choosing a different preimage does not change the result, making it a hard inferential premise. Requiring disclosure of the state domain and checking records is a method requirement; restricting X to a finite set is a specialization; an institution's rule prohibiting use of g for unauthorized dispositions toward people is normative applicability. The latter three must not be treated as evidence establishing q's constancy.","assumptions":"Discusses only a teaching setting involving sets, mappings, and a stipulated norm; it does not claim that any institution actually adopted the rule.","boundary":"The formal example supplies no real-world measurement sufficiency; publishing checking records also creates neither data nor authorization."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Teaching boundary: ‘Publication requires ethical review, therefore ethical review is an empirical cause of the effect observed in the experiment.’","effect":"This misreads method requirements or objects of application as a causal mechanism. Procedures may constrain research behavior, but this permission relation does not itself establish the target physical or social effect.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Teaching boundary: a procedural graph contains ‘failed review → revision → renewed review’, and this is used to declare the entire argument invalid through circularity.","effect":"A procedural cycle is not an inferential cycle. Check separately whether the inferential graph formed by hard premises contains cycles; the same judgment must not be applied indiscriminately across graph layers.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"How should a valid mathematical derivation under given premises be recorded when the conditions for sources are unmet?","answer":"Retain the formal derivation's conditional validity. Missing sources may block the real-world mapping or publication qualification, but that alone must not be used to declare the mathematical derivation false.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"What edge is mistaken in ‘privacy rules apply to this interview, therefore everything the interviewee says is true’?","answer":"Relations such as objects of application supply only protections and constraints on use; they must not serve in reverse as factual evidence.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"May a rule specialized to human organizations be inferred backward through a domain-restriction relation into a law of all natural systems?","answer":"No. Specialization narrows scope and adds conditions; it must not be reversed to expand a claim into a universal assertion about the world.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-2-3/figures/section-2-3.png","alt":"Hard inferential premise, that is, the inferential premise: the conclusion's inferential basis, the only relation type entering the inferential acyclic graph; method requirement; domain specialization, that is, the domain restriction; scope/publication/action stated separately without conflating edges; normative or procedural applicability, that is, objects of application. A type diagram based on the original table in 2.3; only the upper-left inferential relation belongs to the inferential acyclic graph leading from premises to inferred conclusions, and the other arrows are not empirical causes.","caption":"A type diagram based on the original table in 2.3; only the upper-left inferential relation belongs to the inferential acyclic graph leading from premises to conclusions, and the other arrows are not empirical causes."}],"diagramBrief":"The graph's arrows must display relation types. Interaction may show only the inferential layer, hiding other edges without deleting their requirements; selecting an edge shows whether its absence affects inference, publication, scope, or action.","applicability":{"realCase":"not_applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"This section specifies dependency semantics; explicit mathematical examples and conditional counterexamples suffice to test the invalid inferences. Unrelated historical materials are not treated as empirical validation of graph types."},"openQuestions":[],"taskId":"S-2-3","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Lines 265–277 of the complete historical Chinese research manuscript; see sourceRefs for each section's precise range","claim_support":["2.3: Terms, contracts, burdens of argument, and source boundaries; not a basis for certifying empirical facts"],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2.1.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"theory-v2.1","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2.1, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2.1 / 2026-11-19","locator":"2.3　四类依赖必须分开; Chinese source lines 273–285; SHA-256 2128af7cfa4c63b58207521a375aa34c2aea230d47e1c04a5afec3334e290d28","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2.1; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-2-3/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-2-3/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-2-3/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-2-3/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v2.1; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"8a68e3abd47c7615a3bbaed4cc48f4680ab0b6320c0854fd0bf10f64cf5f153a","source_sha256":"2128af7cfa4c63b58207521a375aa34c2aea230d47e1c04a5afec3334e290d28"}},"2.3.1":{"title":"2.3.1 Layered Responsibilities of the Common Method Kernel","lead":"Two theoretical texts may share requirements for careful recording and inference, but must not stamp one another as true. What is aligned is each layer's responsibilities and conditions, not names or numbers.","explanation":["Chinese source location: lines 286–300; source anchor section-2-3-1; short quotation: ‘Neither serves as an authoritative source for the other, nor do they constitute double empirical evidence’. The current source appears in the corresponding subsection on this page; the downloadable original research excerpt preserves the historical manuscript.","This section addresses the limits of using this framework and World Model side by side: methodological responsibilities may be shared, while each text retains its own objects, arguments, and way of being used. ‘May share’ does not mean correspondence has been verified across all versions. The current materials provide the current Xikari text, but not a full, identifiable version of World Model; this lesson therefore explains the declaration in this passage without claiming to have verified the other text.","The first layer in the original table, ‘definitions’, fixes assessable meanings for objects and terms; it can help the two texts establish what they are discussing, but does not prove that those objects exist in reality. The second layer, ‘common method constraints’, governs representations, the status of materials, and inference—for example, not treating simulations as observations or method requirements as empirical causes. Shared adherence to these constraints creates no new natural or social law.","The third layer, F, answers what necessarily follows under mathematical premises. A proof's validity depends on its domain, mapping, target, operations, and assumptions; the same theorem name appearing in two texts does not eliminate the need to align premises and check proofs. The fourth layer, G, organizes specific empirical questions; actual support comes from specific tests with objects, comparisons, failure modes, and materials. Two texts retelling the same study do not constitute two independent observations.","The fifth layer, C, asks what existing grounds permit to be determined and used. It depends on relevant results, additional mechanistic conditions, and application windows; a rule's presence in the common kernel must not fill a missing mechanistic connection. The sixth layer, governance invariants, constrains publication, evaluation, suspension, and action; these limit the use of power and are not additional causes of changes in the research object.","D, G, and C numbers support stable references; the common kernel's Chinese numbering supports alignment across texts. Neither replaces the complete conditions. Actual checking requires comparing complete meanings, premises, evidential statuses, targets, and failure rules side by side: record differences where the same name has different conditions, while differently named clauses with corresponding conditions may also admit a grounded mapping.","This section continues 2.2's argumentative roles and 2.3's dependency layers, preventing the circular move ‘I said it in another text too, so it has more support’. A common method may clarify interoperability; whether the framework is more useful for a class of real tasks remains a separate question requiring a comparison design and materials."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Common method constraints","leftText":"How both texts record and use grounds.","rightTitle":"Shared laws of the world","rightText":"Empirical or formal claims about real-world regularities, requiring the corresponding proofs or materials."},{"leftTitle":"Stable numbering","leftText":"Makes conditions easier to locate.","rightTitle":"Complete conditions","rightText":"Meaning, premises, application window, evidence, and failure rules; numbering cannot replace them."},{"leftTitle":"Agreement between two texts","leftText":"May derive from shared definitions or the same materials.","rightTitle":"Two independent sources of empirical support","rightText":"Require genuinely distinct materials or designs relevant to the claim; they must not be counted by document number."},{"leftTitle":"A declaration of interoperability","leftText":"Proposes checking responsibilities and possible ways of connecting.","rightTitle":"Verified interoperability","rightText":"Actual versions, mappings, and materials on execution and testing are all available for checking."}],"boundaries":["No identifiable version of the other text has been provided, so full terminological equivalence, full-text consistency, or an already shared method kernel must not be claimed.","The six layers are not an evidential ladder that automatically upgrades from low to high. A definition does not become an empirical fact by passing through a methodological layer, and the governance layer is no better than empirical materials at establishing causation.","A failed correspondence between texts should restrict that particular connection; a mere difference in names should not invalidate all independently supported conclusions in the other text."],"questions":["If two texts use the same Chinese number, may their clauses be merged directly?","Does jointly adopting ‘simulation must not be written back as observation’ increase a simulation's empirical truth?","Without the other text, what can this section complete, and what can it not complete?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-2-3-1/figures/section-2-3-1.png","caseLinks":[],"caseNotes":[],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"2.3.1","start_line":300,"end_line":314,"startLine":300,"endLine":314}],"outputId":"section-2-3-1","sourceHash":"fcf100a5c8688d1e0337c52750ce3ad21fb5dbcc250f97946602ce094345f825","argumentSteps":["Premise: the two texts each have their own object domains, ways of being used, and arguments; repeated names do not imply identical premises and semantics.","Connection one: comparing definitions, methods, conditional formal propositions, empirical questions, conclusion rules, and governance layer by layer distinguishes semantic compatibility, formal instances, empirical support, and limits on power.","Connection two: reusing the same source adds no independent material, so agreement across texts cannot be totaled as double empirical support.","Conclusion: shared methodological responsibilities can constitute interoperability requirements; whether particular texts satisfy the correspondence must be checked item by item after the correct versions are obtained.","Responsibility and present limit: the current Xikari source supports this section’s description of its interface; it cannot by itself establish the actual content of World Model or that interoperability has been carried out successfully."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: identically named “closure” must be aligned condition by condition","text":"Suppose Text A’s “closure” requires only recovery of the current target q from representation π, while Text B’s “closure” additionally requires the representation to update on its own after every admissible action. The names match, but B adds dynamic and action conditions. Alignment should record “A requires static recovery; B has additional dynamic requirements,” rather than treating them interchangeably. Conversely, two different names may admit a formal mapping only when premises, domains, and conclusions are equivalent item by item.","assumptions":"A and B are two texts constructed for teaching; they do not describe the actual content of World Model.","boundary":"The example illustrates an alignment method, not actual correspondence between books or any framework’s empirical effectiveness."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Teaching boundary: two lectures cite the same study and then report “two independent studies support this.”","effect":"Repeated retelling adds no original material; record the shared source and each interpretation without increasing the count of independent support.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"unresolved_boundary","text":"Without obtaining the specified version of World Model, treating Xikari’s statement about shared methods as evidence of complete agreement between both texts.","effect":"Actual correspondence to the other text remains unchecked; Xikari’s own account can presently be explained, but the other text’s content cannot be supplied on its behalf.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"If two texts use the same Chinese number, may their clauses be merged directly?","answer":"No. Complete definitions, premises, object domains, failure conditions, and ways of use must be checked; identifiers are only location tools.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Does jointly adopting ‘simulation must not be written back as observation’ increase a simulation's empirical truth?","answer":"No. The requirement instead limits the status in which material can be used; actual support still needs external observation and bridging.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Without the other text, what can this section complete, and what can it not complete?","answer":"One can explain the current text’s six layers of responsibility and interoperability rules; one cannot verify the other text’s actual content, equivalence of the books, or actual successful execution.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-2-3-1/figures/section-2-3-1.png","alt":"Definitions: what objects and terms refer to; common method constraints: representation/status/inference; conditional formal propositions F: premises and proof; empirical questions G: specific tests/comparisons/failure; conclusion rules C: grounds/conditions/applicability windows; governance invariants: publication/suspension/action. Six-cell responsibility diagram based on the original table in 2.3.1; position denotes neither rank nor causal order, and no review of the other text is claimed.","caption":"Six-cell responsibility diagram based on the original table in 2.3.1; position denotes neither rank nor causal order, and no review of the other text is claimed."}],"diagramBrief":"The six cells correspond to six checking tasks, without automatic-escalation arrows. An interactive version could select text versions and show differences in the complete conditions cell by cell. Since the other version has not been supplied, it should display “awaiting review,” not default to equivalence.","applicability":{"realCase":"not_applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"This section explains cross-text interfaces and argumentative responsibilities; unrelated historical facts are unsuitable validation. The specified version of the other text is currently absent, so a completed real cross-book case is not fabricated."},"openQuestions":["An actual interoperability comparison requires World Model’s author, complete version, date, and corresponding clauses; this guide only explains the requirements of the available Xikari source."],"taskId":"S-2-3-1","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical research manuscript, complete Chinese source lines 278–292; exact section ranges in sourceRefs","claim_support":["2.3.1: terminology, contracts, argumentative responsibilities, and source boundaries; not certification of empirical facts"],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2.1.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"theory-v2.1","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2.1, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2.1 / 2026-11-19","locator":"2.3.1　共同方法内核的分层责任; Chinese source lines 286–300; SHA-256 2128af7cfa4c63b58207521a375aa34c2aea230d47e1c04a5afec3334e290d28","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2.1; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-2-3-1/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-2-3-1/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-2-3-1/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-2-3-1/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v2.1; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"4cc3262421fbaba32c5f14d59f48a37c82bb8ae4e1f6e470e5611716b96c80df","source_sha256":"2128af7cfa4c63b58207521a375aa34c2aea230d47e1c04a5afec3334e290d28"}},"2.4":{"title":"2.4 Conditions for Causal Claims","lead":"Seeing change after an action does not yet answer how much change the action caused. Conversely, a comparison answering the total-effect question may not identify every intermediate link.","explanation":["Source location: Chinese source lines 301–306; source anchor section-2-4; short quotation: “limited identification of the effect; the specific mechanism is unresolved.” The current source appears in the corresponding section on this page; the downloadable original research excerpt preserves the historical manuscript.","The conditions for causal claims first specify the treatment version and outcome. “Discontinuing a water source” differs from “issuing advice”; onset on a given day differs from death on that day; a local population differs from all city residents. Next fix the object, population, window, scale, and effect target. Without them, two studies calling something “effective” may answer entirely different questions.","Why the comparison identifies the target is central to a causal claim. A common input may affect treatment and outcome; self-selection makes recipients different at baseline; attrition changes the observed sample; measurement changes alter records; outcomes may influence treatment in reverse; one unit’s treatment may affect others. Address relevant issues individually rather than mechanically adding controls regardless of the task.","Identifying a total effect and identifying a specified structural channel carry different burdens. A claim that A affects Y through M needs the corresponding temporal order and mechanism chain, plus blocking, replacement, or natural variation meeting identification conditions that distinguish alternative explanations. Cross-level claims also check bridging by scale transformation (§3.4) and the epistemic constraint “Testing Cross-Scale Transfer” (§4.8.5). A design identifying only a total effect should not be forced to exhaust every microscopic process first; neither may the total effect fill in an untested mediator.","Evaluate causal strength, mechanism resolution, and extrapolation scope separately. A credible comparison within a bounded window does not establish a fine-grained account of every channel or transfer to every population. The reverse caution also matters: two mechanisms may offset, so a near-zero net total effect does not mean each mechanism is zero.","The next section, 2.4.1, distinguishes estimands such as “receiving an opportunity,” “actually receiving an intervention,” and “institutional expansion.” Section 2.4.2 uses an exact counterexample to show why accurate estimates of two average link effects still do not establish a composed whole-chain effect."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Temporal order","leftText":"A occurs before Y, satisfying one part of the required causal ordering.","rightTitle":"Causal identification","rightText":"Can exclude or control the relevant competing explanations and support the specified counterfactual comparison."},{"leftTitle":"Total effect","leftText":"The combined change in the terminal outcome following a treatment change.","rightTitle":"Specified mechanism","rightText":"Which channel produces change and under which common conditions."},{"leftTitle":"Limited identification","leftText":"Answers a question within a population, window, and set of assumptions.","rightTitle":"Broad extrapolation","rightText":"Continued validity for new objects and scales requires additional grounds."}],"boundaries":["Snow’s table contains separate columns for fatal cases arranged by onset date and counts by death date, not all infections or all cases of onset. Their denominators and event clocks must not be mixed.","Snow’s account of decline before discontinuation limits simple before-and-after attribution; it does not prove that removing the pump handle had no effect. Without a comparable counterfactual, a zero effect is not automatically identified either.","Retain the limited total-effect conclusion when a mechanism remains unidentified; when the total effect is not significant, retain independently grounded knowledge of local processes as well."],"questions":["A randomized comparison supports a treatment’s total effect but measures no mediator. May the total effect be reported?","If the total effect is near zero, may one write “all channels are absent”?","What can the decline in onset before handle removal establish in Snow’s case?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-2-4/figures/section-2-4.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"Broad Street: separate exposure evidence from the net effect of removing the handle","href":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/72894/pg72894-images.html"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"Broad Street: separate exposure evidence from the net effect of removing the handle","text":"Snow’s Table I for the 1854 Broad Street epidemic arranges fatal cases separately by onset date and death date: fatal-case onsets numbered 143 on September 1 and 28 on September 7; the pump handle was removed on September 8. The original also notes population departure and decline before discontinuation. This section adds the event-clock and counterfactual distinction: these records do not allow low values after removal to independently establish removal’s net effect.","sourceLabel":"Snow, second edition, 1855, Table I and the author’s discussion","sourceUrl":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/72894/pg72894-images.html","sourceLocator":"Printed page 49, Table I; post-table discussion on pages 50–52; page 40 records the September 7 meeting and handle removal the following day","boundary":"The table includes cases with unknown onset dates and counts only eventually fatal cases. This guide estimates neither all infections nor a reconstructed control group and does not infer a zero effect of handle removal.","href":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/72894/pg72894-images.html"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"2.4","start_line":315,"end_line":320,"startLine":315,"endLine":320}],"outputId":"section-2-4","sourceHash":"fcf100a5c8688d1e0337c52750ce3ad21fb5dbcc250f97946602ce094345f825","argumentSteps":["Premise: causal questions include a counterfactual comparison of treatment and outcome; seeing only the realized path does not directly reveal the outcome on the path not taken.","Connection: a credible design must explain why comparable groups, periods, or variation represent the target counterfactual and address confounding, selection, interference, and measurement issues relevant to that design.","Conclusion: report a bounded total effect under the identification conditions; a specified mechanism also requires channel-discriminating evidence, and extrapolation requires additional bridging.","Reverse check: a zero total effect may arise from offsetting paths; local paths may also exist without connecting in the same object and window.","Evidential responsibility: historical documents support their actual observations and comparisons; narrative coherence does not automatically supply a net-effect estimate."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Mathematical teaching construction: two paths exactly offset","text":"Let a∈{0,1}. One path adds a to the outcome and another subtracts a, giving terminal y(a)=a−a=0. Each constructed path varies with a, but y(1)−y(0)=0. This shows that the unconditional inference “zero net effect → no mechanisms exist” is invalid.","assumptions":"Both terms are additive components with the same units, object, and window, and the functions are fully given.","boundary":"Offsetting is stipulated, not estimated in a real system; changing units or adding interactions requires recalculation."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"real_counterevidence","text":"Snow’s Table I records 143 fatal-case onsets on September 1 and 28 on September 7; the pump handle was removed only on September 8. The author also acknowledges a substantial decline before discontinuation.","effect":"A decline already underway cannot all be attributed to later handle removal; neither does this establish that removal’s net effect must be zero.","sourceIds":["snow-table"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"A randomized comparison supports a treatment’s total effect but measures no mediator. May the total effect be reported?","answer":"Yes, within the design assumptions and scope, explicitly leaving the specific mechanism unresolved; a mediator chain must not be fabricated.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"If the total effect is near zero, may one write “all channels are absent”?","answer":"No. Paths can offset, and measurement or precision may also be insufficient; examine the claimed channels separately.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"What can the decline in onset before handle removal establish in Snow’s case?","answer":"It challenges the simple narrative attributing the entire decline to later handle removal; it does not itself identify removal’s net effect.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-2-4/figures/section-2-4.png","alt":"September 1, 1854: 143 fatal-case onsets; September 7: 28 fatal-case onsets; September 8: Broad Street pump handle removed. Counting object: onset dates of eventually fatal cases. Competing explanations: departure, exposure changes, and others. A counterfactual comparison remains missing; no net effect of removal is reported. Source: Snow 1855, Table I, p.49, and discussion, pp.50–52. Distances between boxes are not proportional to time; arrows show sequence only, not causation.","caption":"Source: Snow 1855, Table I, p.49, and discussion, pp.50–52. Distances between boxes are not proportional to time; arrows show sequence only, not causation."}],"diagramBrief":"Static event-timing diagram, with figures read from the original table’s “Fatal Attacks” column and explicitly distinguished from all cases. A view could switch between onset and death dates, but must display the different columns and unknown dates rather than treating the declining curve as a net intervention effect.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"This historical case is used here only to identify temporal order, event definitions, and intervention-attribution boundaries, without repeating the preceding question about eligibility."},"openQuestions":["Estimating removal’s net effect would require a comparable counterfactual and materials on departure and changed water exposure; this guide does not turn a historical table into a randomized experiment."],"taskId":"S-2-4","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical research manuscript, complete Chinese source lines 293–314; exact section ranges in sourceRefs","claim_support":["2.4: terminology, contracts, argumentative responsibilities, and source boundaries; not certification of empirical facts","2.4.1: terminology, contracts, argumentative responsibilities, and source boundaries; not certification of empirical facts","2.4.2: terminology, contracts, argumentative responsibilities, and source boundaries; not certification of empirical facts"],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2.1.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"snow-table","title":"John Snow, On the Mode of Communication of Cholera","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/72894/pg72894-images.html","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"Second edition, 1855; digital transcription dated 2024-02-07","locator":"Second edition, 1855, printed pages 40, 49 (Table I), and 50–52","claim_support":["2.4: 143 fatal-case onsets on September 1, 28 on September 7; pump handle removed on September 8.","2.4: the author distinguishes onset and death times and discusses departure and decline before handle removal."],"limits":["Fatal cases are not all cases; onset times include estimates and unknowns.","Only the relevant original passages and table were checked; this is not a re-estimation of the net intervention effect."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"oregon-abstract","title":"Finkelstein et al., The Oregon Health Insurance Experiment: Evidence from the First Year","url":"https://www.nber.org/papers/w17190","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"2011-07，DOI 10.3386/w17190","locator":"NBER Working Paper 17190, first two sentences of the official abstract; official text returned by search","claim_support":["2.4.1: Oregon’s 2008 lottery offered uninsured low-income adults an opportunity to apply for Medicaid."],"limits":["Direct webpage and PDF access currently returned 403; only the official abstract returned by search was used, without claiming the full text had been read.","Supports only the design facts, not all identification conditions of this guide’s teaching formula in that study or particular health outcomes."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v2.1","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2.1, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2.1 / 2026-11-19","locator":"2.4　因果主张的条件; Chinese source lines 301–306; SHA-256 2128af7cfa4c63b58207521a375aa34c2aea230d47e1c04a5afec3334e290d28","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2.1; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-2-4/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-2-4/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-2-4/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-2-4/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v2.1; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"71d5d05adefbfb67389426f1b41a7b3177918db9f4d89963cca3f243ca6970d5","source_sha256":"2128af7cfa4c63b58207521a375aa34c2aea230d47e1c04a5afec3334e290d28"}},"2.4.1":{"title":"2.4.1 Estimands and Mechanism Responsibilities","lead":"Ask “what exactly changed?” before discussing effects. A lottery offering an opportunity, a person choosing uptake, and full institutional expansion involve different counterfactuals and cannot share one undifferentiated “treatment effect” label.","explanation":["Source location: Chinese source lines 307–314; source anchor section-2-4-1; short quotation: “Offering someone an opportunity, that person actually receiving an intervention, and expansion of an entire institution are different treatments.” The current source appears in this page’s corresponding section; the downloadable original research excerpt preserves the historical manuscript.","Let Z denote receiving an opportunity, D actual uptake, and Y the outcome. Comparing randomized Z can identify the effect of offering the opportunity under the corresponding design conditions; directly comparing D=1 with D=0 may mix in self-selection. Even if Z changes D, additional conditions such as an exclusion restriction are needed to interpret the opportunity effect as a particular local effect of receiving treatment. When an additional assumption fails, withdraw the interpretation depending on it, without automatically withdrawing a still-credible randomized opportunity comparison.","The population average, the local average for a specified complier group, and each individual’s outcome answer different questions. A local effect may apply only to those whose uptake changes because the opportunity changes. It cannot directly generalize to never-takers, always-takers, all individuals, or full institutional expansion. Expansion may also change supply, congestion, interaction, and resource allocation, making the treatment version different.","More controls are not necessarily better. If the target is the total effect A→M→Y, holding M fixed changes the question. Controlling a variable jointly affected by A and other causes can also open an association that was previously absent. First specify variables’ positions in the generating process rather than adding every correlated variable to a regression. Even a larger sample does not remove indistinguishability produced by the design.","Failure of point identification does not require pretending to know everything or nothing. Retain bounds, sets of possible directions, exclusions by necessary conditions, or specific process knowledge supported by assumptions and material, and list the unanswered targets. Partial identification must actually follow from the corresponding constraints; assigning an arbitrary interval does not make it rigorous.","The source closes with common event substitutions: agreement text, verification, execution, and everyday use are not the same thing. An announcement, public understanding, arrival, border handling, and full clearance likewise cannot have one node stand for the entire chain. Each changed treatment or outcome definition requires rechecking its event, time, object, and dependency route."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Receiving an opportunity Z","leftText":"Can be compared through random assignment.","rightTitle":"Actual uptake D","rightText":"Affected by eligibility, choice, and execution; not necessarily random."},{"leftTitle":"Local average effect","leftText":"Average change for a specified group of compliers.","rightTitle":"Population or individual effect","rightText":"Needs a different target and identification basis; it cannot be read directly from a local average."},{"leftTitle":"Failure of point identification","leftText":"The target quantity cannot be uniquely determined.","rightTitle":"No information at all","rightText":"A stronger claim; bounds, directions, or process constraints may remain."}],"boundaries":["“Most people did not take up the opportunity” does not mean random assignment necessarily failed; distinguish the opportunity effect from the uptake effect.","This guide offers no current medical, insurance, or policy advice. The Oregon facts are limited to the historical design checkable in the official abstract; it uses no health statistics or individual conclusions from an unread full text.","Controlling another variable may change the target or introduce bias; more observations improve some estimation precision but do not automatically repair identification."],"questions":["If an invitation has a direct effect beyond encouraging uptake, must all results of the randomized invitation comparison be withdrawn?","Does analyzing only eventual recipients automatically preserve comparability of the original randomized groups?","Why cannot “the existence of an agreement” be entered directly as “everyday execution succeeded”?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-2-4/figures/section-2-4-1.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"Oregon’s 2008 lottery: selection offered an opportunity to apply","href":"https://www.nber.org/papers/w17190"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"Oregon’s 2008 lottery: selection offered an opportunity to apply","text":"The official abstract of the 2011 NBER working paper states that in 2008 a group of uninsured low-income Oregon adults obtained the opportunity to apply for Medicaid through a lottery. The design itself distinguishes winning an opportunity from actually becoming insured; the former cannot be renamed universal receipt of an identical intervention.","sourceLabel":"Finkelstein et al., The Oregon Health Insurance Experiment: Evidence from the First Year","sourceUrl":"https://www.nber.org/papers/w17190","sourceLocator":"Finkelstein et al., NBER Working Paper 17190, July 2011; first two sentences of the official page’s abstract (official text returned by search)","boundary":"The official abstract was obtained; direct full-text/PDF requests currently returned 403, and no full-text reading is claimed. Only the lottery and application-opportunity design facts are used, not a finding that all IV conditions, individual effects, or institutional-expansion effects hold.","href":"https://www.nber.org/papers/w17190"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"2.4.1","start_line":321,"end_line":328,"startLine":321,"endLine":328}],"outputId":"section-2-4-1","sourceHash":"fcf100a5c8688d1e0337c52750ce3ad21fb5dbcc250f97946602ce094345f825","argumentSteps":["Target definition: let ITT_Y=E[Y|Z=1]−E[Y|Z=0], the mean difference grouped by opportunity Z; the design must explain randomization, outcome comparability, attrition, and related conditions.","Additional conditions: if Z is randomized, actually changes D, affects Y only through D, has no defiers, and satisfies consistency and the corresponding no-interference conditions, then in the binary case ITT_Y／(E[D|Z=1]−E[D|Z=0]) can be interpreted as the local average effect for compliers.","Limitation: failure of conditions such as the exclusion restriction or monotonicity undermines this interpretation as a local effect of receipt. It does not automatically establish that the effect of the opportunity assigned by randomized Z is also absent.","Change of use: moving from a local effect of receipt to population-wide expansion, manipulation of a particular mediator, or individual prediction requires the corresponding additional assumptions and material; changing the title is insufficient.","Evidential responsibility: in this lesson the historical Oregon example supports only the design distinction that “randomly offering an opportunity to apply does not randomly guarantee enrollment.” The formula is a teaching explanation under explicit assumptions, not a substitute for all identification checks in the paper."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Mathematical teaching construction: one ratio, two layers of assumptions","text":"Suppose a randomly assigned opportunity increases the average outcome by 2 units and increases the receipt rate by 0.25, and additionally suppose the binary IV conditions above hold. The local average effect for compliers is then 2÷0.25=8 units. If the opportunity also directly affects Y, the numerator includes a path that does not run through D, so 8 loses that interpretation. If random assignment of the opportunity, measurement, and other relevant conditions still hold, 2 can still represent the opportunity effect.","assumptions":"2 and 0.25 are purely teaching assumptions, not observed values from the Oregon paper. Units and the time window are fixed.","boundary":"This calculation cannot establish the exclusion restriction or monotonicity in reality, nor assign 8 to every individual."},{"title":"Mathematical teaching construction: controlling a common outcome can create an association","text":"Let A and U be independent binary variables taking 0 and 1, and define S=A+U and Y=U. A does not affect Y in the population. Restricting the sample to S=1 leaves only (A,U)=(1,0) and (0,1), so those with A=1 have Y=0 and those with A=0 have Y=1: a negative association appears. Controlling the common outcome S changes the sample structure.","assumptions":"Independent, equally probable binary variables and deterministic functions are explicitly constructed assumptions.","boundary":"This counterexample rejects only “more controls necessarily mean greater reliability.” It does not imply that all controls are bad and is not an empirical estimate."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Teaching conditional counterexample: issuing an invitation itself provides additional consultation, affecting the outcome even when the main intervention is not received.","effect":"The exclusion restriction is undermined. One cannot simply use the invitation as an instrument and divide the entire outcome difference by the receipt difference. The overall effect of randomized invitation can still be studied independently.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Teaching conditional counterexample: service capacity becomes insufficient after system expansion, although it was sufficient in the original small trial.","effect":"The treatment and shared conditions have changed; the local trial effect cannot be generalized directly to full expansion without a bridge.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"If an invitation has a direct effect beyond encouraging uptake, must all results of the randomized invitation comparison be withdrawn?","answer":"Not necessarily. First withdraw the interpretation as an “effect of receipt” that depends on the exclusion restriction. If invitation randomization, measurement, and other design conditions remain credible, the opportunity effect may be retained.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Does analyzing only eventual recipients automatically preserve comparability of the original randomized groups?","answer":"No. Receipt is a post-assignment choice; selecting recipients may destroy comparability.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Why cannot “the existence of an agreement” be entered directly as “everyday execution succeeded”?","answer":"Text, verification, implementation, and actual use are distinct events. Subsequent nodes and their connections each require evidence.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-2-4/figures/section-2-4-1.png","alt":"Z: offering an opportunity, such as eligibility to apply through a lottery; D: actual receipt, involving eligibility/choice/execution; Y: the specified outcome, with object/unit/window. Opportunity effect: compare randomized Z. Local effect for compliers: additionally check IV and related assumptions. Effect of full institutional expansion: separately check supply/congestion/interactions. Estimand diagram based on 2.4.1; the Oregon abstract supports only the design fact concerning an application opportunity. The arrows are not a complete mechanism confirmed by this lesson.","caption":"Estimand diagram based on 2.4.1; the Oregon abstract supports only the design fact concerning an application opportunity. The arrows are not a complete mechanism confirmed by this lesson."}],"diagramBrief":"The target can be switched among Z, D, and overall expansion to display each target's assumptions and missing material. The same number cannot automatically be copied to all three targets.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"An actual lottery study directly illustrates the distinction between opportunity and receipt. Only design facts from the official abstract obtained are used here; formal derivation is separately labeled as teaching material."},"openQuestions":["Reviewing the study's complete IV setup and numerical results requires a readable formal paper, supplementary material, and specific analysis locations. This lesson does not exceed the scope of the abstract obtained."],"taskId":"S-2-4","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical research manuscript, complete Chinese source lines 293–314; exact section ranges in sourceRefs","claim_support":["2.4: terminology, contracts, argumentative responsibilities, and source boundaries; not certification of empirical facts","2.4.1: terminology, contracts, argumentative responsibilities, and source boundaries; not certification of empirical facts","2.4.2: terminology, contracts, argumentative responsibilities, and source boundaries; not certification of empirical facts"],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2.1.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"snow-table","title":"John Snow, On the Mode of Communication of Cholera","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/72894/pg72894-images.html","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"Second edition, 1855; digital transcription dated 2024-02-07","locator":"Second edition, 1855, printed pages 40, 49 (Table I), and 50–52","claim_support":["2.4: 143 fatal-case onsets on September 1, 28 on September 7; pump handle removed on September 8.","2.4: the author distinguishes onset and death times and discusses departure and decline before handle removal."],"limits":["Fatal cases are not all cases; onset times include estimates and unknowns.","Only the relevant original passages and table were checked; this is not a re-estimation of the net intervention effect."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"oregon-abstract","title":"Finkelstein et al., The Oregon Health Insurance Experiment: Evidence from the First Year","url":"https://www.nber.org/papers/w17190","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"2011-07，DOI 10.3386/w17190","locator":"NBER Working Paper 17190, first two sentences of the official abstract; official text returned by search","claim_support":["2.4.1: Oregon’s 2008 lottery offered uninsured low-income adults an opportunity to apply for Medicaid."],"limits":["Direct webpage and PDF access currently returned 403; only the official abstract returned by search was used, without claiming the full text had been read.","Supports only the design facts, not all identification conditions of this guide’s teaching formula in that study or particular health outcomes."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v2.1","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2.1, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2.1 / 2026-11-19","locator":"2.4.1 估计目标与机制责任; Chinese source lines 307–314; SHA-256 2128af7cfa4c63b58207521a375aa34c2aea230d47e1c04a5afec3334e290d28","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2.1; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-2-4/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-2-4/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-2-4/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-2-4/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v2.1; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"c4a0442cc7b6ec0d2afdc4078bd156c1a0f5c792ec943caf6c4bbb61c1632da2","source_sha256":"2128af7cfa4c63b58207521a375aa34c2aea230d47e1c04a5afec3334e290d28"}},"2.4.2":{"title":"2.4.2 Local effects, joint responses, and the whole chain","lead":"Two average effects can both be real and still produce no effect when connected: people changed by the first stage may be precisely those who do not respond to the second. Joint responses in the same objects are crucial.","explanation":["Source location: Chinese source lines 315–322; original anchor section-2-4-2. Short quotation: “Multiplying the two averages gives 1/4, which is not the effect of the chain as a whole.” See the corresponding section on this page for the current original; downloaded original research excerpts retain the historical draft.","Serial composition must preserve more than two averages. Does the first stage's output match the actual version of the second stage's input? Can the same objects and sequence be matched? Do the stages share a support range? Who responds to both? What is the terminal outcome? These joint conditions determine whether the chain connects. Different studies may indeed be incompatible, but even within the same population, losing the joint structure can still produce error.","The original gives a complete finite counterexample. s=0 and s=1 each have weight one half; treatment a and mediator m are binary; m(a)=a(1−s), y(a,m)=sm. Treatment changes the mediator for those with s=0, but they do not respond to the mediator. Those with s=1 respond to the mediator, but treatment does not change their mediator. The two stages' effects occur in different people.","The local average A→M is therefore 1/2, and the average M→Y under external assignment of M is also 1/2. Under the actual composition m(a), each type's terminal outcome is always 0, making the whole-chain effect 0. The product of averages, 1/4, differs from the average product of individual responses, 0. The given functions refute unconditional multiplication; they do not estimate the effect of any actual mechanism.","“Complete” is relative to a target, window, and dependency route. Identifying a bounded total effect need not require knowledge of every microscopic link. Manipulating a mediator, transferring across domains, or allocating responsibility requires the connections on which that use depends. Shared resources, counteracting paths, and feedback can change the composite outcome. Evidence for each node separately cannot be recast as an established whole chain within the same case."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Average edge effect","leftText":"The effect of each stage averaged separately across the population.","rightTitle":"Whole-chain effect","rightText":"The terminal difference arising from actual sequential connection within the same object."},{"leftTitle":"Evidence for individual nodes","leftText":"Each local fact has corresponding grounds.","rightTitle":"Evidence for joint connections","rightText":"Versions, identities, timing, and joint responses are compatible in this case."},{"leftTitle":"A complete bounded chain","leftText":"Covers the connections on which the current use actually depends.","rightTitle":"Exhausting every mechanism","rightText":"This is not required to identify every bounded total effect."}],"boundaries":["This counterexample is calculated exactly from the original text's specified functions. It cannot establish that all real mediation analyses are invalid, nor negate a total effect identified through another qualified design.","Multiplication may hold if both stage responses are constant across people, or if sufficient structural conditions make the desired whole-chain quantity exactly equal the product of means. These are additional conditions, not consequences of the two means themselves.","Changing the treatment version, permitted mediator manipulation, or terminal target requires rewriting the calculation. Equal numerical values do not mean equal conditions."],"questions":["Why does “the same population” still not guarantee that the two stage averages can be multiplied?","Is the second stage's 1/2 in this example an average outcome observed under actual m(a)?","Must finding this kind of composition problem revoke a bounded total effect from an independent randomized trial?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-2-4/figures/section-2-4-2.png","caseLinks":[],"caseNotes":[],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"2.4.2","start_line":329,"end_line":336,"startLine":329,"endLine":336}],"outputId":"section-2-4-2","sourceHash":"fcf100a5c8688d1e0337c52750ce3ad21fb5dbcc250f97946602ce094345f825","argumentSteps":["Premise: P(s=0)=P(s=1)=1/2; m(a)=a(1−s), y(a,m)=sm; change a from 0 to 1.","First stage: Δm=m(1)−m(0)=1−s, equal to 1 when s=0 and 0 when s=1, so E[Δm]=1/2.","Second stage: hold a fixed and externally set m from 0 to 1. Then Δ_my=y(a,1)−y(a,0)=s, with mean E[Δ_my]=1/2.","Whole chain: y(a,m(a))=s·a(1−s)=0 because binary s satisfies s(1−s)=0. Hence y(1,m(1))−y(0,m(0)) is 0 for both types, and its mean remains 0.","Incorrect multiplication: E[1−s]E[s]=1/4, whereas E[(1−s)s]=0. Discarding the correspondence between the two stage responses changes the quantity being calculated.","Conclusion and responsibility: this counterexample suffices to refute the universal claim that “two average effects can always be multiplied.” Multiplication in another model requires separate proof of structure, homogeneity, or appropriate joint conditions. The example cannot negate a total effect identified by an independent qualified design."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Mathematical construction from the original: two nonoverlapping response types","text":"Complete two-type table: when s=0, m(0)=0, m(1)=1, and y(a,0)=y(a,1)=0; when s=1, m(0)=m(1)=0, y(a,0)=0, and y(a,1)=1. Each type has terminal outcome 0 under the corresponding composition. With equal weights, both edge averages are 0.5, while the actual whole-chain average is 0.","assumptions":"An exact binary domain, two equally weighted types, and deterministic functions specified in the original, without random error or sampling.","boundary":"This expands the original formal counterexample; it is not an observational study and supplies no proportions or effects for an actual population."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"In the original counterexample, one group responds only at the first stage and the other only at the second. Both edge averages are 1/2; the whole chain is 0.","effect":"Limits unconditional “multiplication of average edges”: joint responses must be retained. It does not affect a separately identified total effect.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Why does “the same population” still not guarantee that the two stage averages can be multiplied?","answer":"Responses may be heterogeneous even within the same population. One must know whether first- and second-stage responses overlap in the same objects.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Is the second stage's 1/2 in this example an average outcome observed under actual m(a)?","answer":"No. It is the average response to externally setting m from 0 to 1. Under actual composition, a does not change m for those with s=1.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Must finding this kind of composition problem revoke a bounded total effect from an independent randomized trial?","answer":"No. Failure of a compositional inference and the total effect from another qualified design have different grounds. Retain or downgrade each according to its own dependencies.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-2-4/figures/section-2-4-2.png","alt":"s=0, weight 1/2: a changes m, effect 1; within this type, m does not change y, second-stage effect 0; its whole chain is 1×0=0. s=1, weight 1/2: a does not change m, effect 0; within this type, m changes y, second-stage effect 1; its whole chain is 0×1=0. Mathematical counterexample from the original: both edge averages are 1/2, their product is 1/4, and the actual whole-chain average is 0. Based on 2.4.2, not real-world data.","caption":"Mathematical counterexample from the original: both edge averages are 1/2, their product is 1/4, and the actual whole-chain average is 0. Based on 2.4.2, not real-world data."}],"diagramBrief":"The two rows must retain type identity; arrows must not connect across rows. Toggling retention of the s strata can show how retaining only two averages loses joint responses. All values follow from the specified functions.","applicability":{"realCase":"not_applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"This section uses a complete finite counterexample to refute a universal inference; the mathematical construction directly completes that task. No forced real-world example is added to simulate empirical confirmation."},"openQuestions":[],"taskId":"S-2-4","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical research manuscript, complete Chinese source lines 293–314; exact section ranges in sourceRefs","claim_support":["2.4: terminology, contracts, argumentative responsibilities, and source boundaries; not certification of empirical facts","2.4.1: terminology, contracts, argumentative responsibilities, and source boundaries; not certification of empirical facts","2.4.2: terminology, contracts, argumentative responsibilities, and source boundaries; not certification of empirical facts"],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2.1.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"snow-table","title":"John Snow, On the Mode of Communication of Cholera","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/72894/pg72894-images.html","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"Second edition, 1855; digital transcription dated 2024-02-07","locator":"Second edition, 1855, printed pages 40, 49 (Table I), and 50–52","claim_support":["2.4: 143 fatal-case onsets on September 1, 28 on September 7; pump handle removed on September 8.","2.4: the author distinguishes onset and death times and discusses departure and decline before handle removal."],"limits":["Fatal cases are not all cases; onset times include estimates and unknowns.","Only the relevant original passages and table were checked; this is not a re-estimation of the net intervention effect."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"oregon-abstract","title":"Finkelstein et al., The Oregon Health Insurance Experiment: Evidence from the First Year","url":"https://www.nber.org/papers/w17190","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"2011-07，DOI 10.3386/w17190","locator":"NBER Working Paper 17190, first two sentences of the official abstract; official text returned by search","claim_support":["2.4.1: Oregon’s 2008 lottery offered uninsured low-income adults an opportunity to apply for Medicaid."],"limits":["Direct webpage and PDF access currently returned 403; only the official abstract returned by search was used, without claiming the full text had been read.","Supports only the design facts, not all identification conditions of this guide’s teaching formula in that study or particular health outcomes."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v2.1","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2.1, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2.1 / 2026-11-19","locator":"2.4.2　局部作用、联合响应与整链; Chinese source lines 315–322; SHA-256 2128af7cfa4c63b58207521a375aa34c2aea230d47e1c04a5afec3334e290d28","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2.1; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-2-4/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-2-4/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-2-4/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-2-4/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v2.1; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"c7803d89a24d31f48ead190b38a4f0b96b5087a5711dc58eff76d8e8cb55f6c3","source_sha256":"2128af7cfa4c63b58207521a375aa34c2aea230d47e1c04a5afec3334e290d28"}},"2.4.3":{"title":"2.4.3 Constitutive and Causal Relations","lead":"Whether a right or eligibility is established and whether it is realized are two questions requiring separate answers.","explanation":["A constitutive relation asks whether, under applicable rules and valid conditions, this act constitutes appointment, commitment, a right, or a decision. First check the rule's source, the authorized subject, required form, and specific applicability. Recognition of status need not first identify a causal effect changing behavior.","A causal relation then asks whether established status changes actions, resources, or outcomes through a channel. Constitutive evidence can help establish who has which status, but cannot prove actual fulfillment. Effects that have occurred cannot retroactively repair an invalid procedure either.","Keep four columns separate when reading: constitutive conditions, participants' understandings, actual implementation or realization, and normative justification. They can yield different answers. A right may be validly established but not yet realized; an actual arrangement may be effective while lacking legal status or justification.","This is not an ontological claim that all objects arise from convention. It explains the division of evidential responsibilities and should be read alongside §5.5.5: the connection here concerns legal validity, which cannot be absorbed into the question of an institution's factual existence."],"keyTerms":[{"term":"Constitutive relation","text":"Explains how a status is established under applicable rules."},{"term":"Causal relation","text":"Explains how a change affects subsequent outcomes through a channel."}],"sourceQuotes":[{"text":"The two kinds of question are evidenced separately, and neither can substitute for the other.","ref":"§2.4.3 · author's English reference edition","href":"/en/theory/part-02#section-2-4-3"}],"argumentSteps":["A constitutive relation asks whether, under applicable rules and valid conditions, this act constitutes appointment, commitment, a right, or a decision. First check the rule's source, the authorized subject, required form, and specific applicability. Recognition of status need not first identify a causal effect changing behavior.","A causal relation then asks whether established status changes actions, resources, or outcomes through a channel. Constitutive evidence can help establish who has which status, but cannot prove actual fulfillment. Effects that have occurred cannot retroactively repair an invalid procedure either.","Keep four columns separate when reading: constitutive conditions, participants' understandings, actual implementation or realization, and normative justification. They can yield different answers. A right may be validly established but not yet realized; an actual arrangement may be effective while lacking legal status or justification."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Established status","leftText":"Judged by the rule's source, authority, form, and applicability conditions.","rightTitle":"An effect occurs","rightText":"Judged by implementation records, outcomes, and proportionate causal comparisons."}],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Constructed teaching scenario","text":"Suppose a valid procedure has granted eligibility for a service, but the applicant has not received it. Eligibility can be confirmed while the realization gap is recorded. Nonreceipt does not mean eligibility never existed, and eligibility alone does not prove delivery.","boundary":"Illustrates conditions and counterexamples only; it reports no real event or completed mechanism test."}],"caseNotes":[],"caseLinks":[],"boundaries":["Status, understanding, realization, and justification cannot prove one another.","Actual causal effects cannot manufacture legal authority; lawful status does not guarantee beneficial outcomes."],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"Constructed conditional counterexample","text":"Suppose a valid procedure has granted eligibility for a service, but the applicant has not received it. Eligibility can be confirmed while the realization gap is recorded. Nonreceipt does not mean eligibility never existed, and eligibility alone does not prove delivery.","effect":"Status, understanding, realization, and justification cannot prove one another.","sourceIds":["theory-v2"]}],"questions":["If no causal effect of a right is measured, can one say the right was not established?"],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"If no causal effect of a right is measured, can one say the right was not established?","answer":"Not directly. First check constitutive conditions. An unidentified causal effect limits the effect claim; realization, understanding, and justification remain separate judgments.","basis":"v2 §2.4.3"}],"openQuestions":[],"summary":"Whether a right or eligibility is established and whether it is realized are two questions requiring separate answers.","sourceRefs":[{"section":"2.4.3","start_line":337,"end_line":344,"startLine":337,"endLine":344}],"sourceHash":"fcf100a5c8688d1e0337c52750ce3ad21fb5dbcc250f97946602ce094345f825","sourceSliceHash":"2166cf30eb31e1c04e8da8b65af818f0611643473c93d628277a6fb24575d773","outputId":"section-2-4-3","taskId":"S-2-4","sources":[{"id":"theory-v2.1","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2.1, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2.1 / 2026-11-19","locator":"2.4.3　构成关系与因果关系; Chinese source lines 323–330; SHA-256 2128af7cfa4c63b58207521a375aa34c2aea230d47e1c04a5afec3334e290d28","claim_support":["This section's definitions, argumentative boundaries, and explicitly labeled constructed teaching explanations."],"limits":["No new external empirical test was added; examples do not serve as real-world evidence."],"access_status":"read"}],"applicability":{"realCase":"not_applicable","diagram":"not_applicable","reason":"This section explains the new v2 argument through conceptual distinctions, explicitly labeled teaching scenarios, and contrary conditions, without adding external empirical research."},"lecturePath":"","sourcesPath":"","sourceSlicePath":"","diagramsPath":"","supplementaryFiles":[],"figures":[],"diagramBrief":"Item-by-item comparison preserves conditions without adding a figure that would portray the process as a mandatory sequence.","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v2.1; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"18424cc9037547c15a6a76f48ee7888bc12d586132f5e3a08cff5810beb5d4c3","source_sha256":"2128af7cfa4c63b58207521a375aa34c2aea230d47e1c04a5afec3334e290d28"}},"2.5":{"title":"2.5 Conditions for evidence","lead":"“Having a source” answers only where material comes from, not what it establishes. Evidence is a support relation directed at a specific claim, requiring an account of why every step connects.","explanation":["Source location: Chinese source lines 331–336; original anchor section-2-5. Short quotation: “A material's having a source does not permit omitting the support relation between that material and the current claim.” See the corresponding section on this page for the current original; downloaded original research excerpts retain the historical draft.","A source is a person, archive, instrument, or study; material is the text, signal, or data left behind; an observation record is a result organized by rules; evidence is those records' support for a claim. A counterexample challenges a particular claim; judgment combines grounds and uncertainty; authorization for action separately determines what may be done on that basis. One cannot leap from “a reliable institution exists” at the beginning to “I have authority to intervene” at the end.","Record, as appropriate to the task, the object, material-generation process, research design, measurement and selection, uncertainty, competing explanations, and extrapolation boundaries. A material's use may change its support relation: a notice can directly establish what its text says, but cannot alone show that the public understood, personnel implemented it, or outcomes improved. Formal tests and foresight must also state null models, thresholds, and evaluation under their respective rules. Do not fabricate a pre-registration of a formal test for an ordinary historical text merely to make the form look complete.","Absence and silence have no default direction. An event's absence from a log may mean it did not occur, was not recorded, its record was lost, the record is inaccessible, or disclosure is prohibited. Corresponding negative information arises only when the event should demonstrably have left a detectable record. Evidential force against occurrence varies with recording and retrieval capability; the words “not found” do not automatically make it strong.","Protective nondisclosure is not an unconditional exemption from review. State the protective grounds, minimum scope, duration, and credible reviewer, and reduce the strength of conclusions that cannot be publicly supported. If no credible review is possible at all, privacy cannot become a pass for strong factual claims. Nor should unnecessary sensitive disclosure be compelled merely to complete an item.","When counterexamples and residuals appear after the evidence cutoff, preserve the original record and downgrade, suspend, or withdraw as agreed in advance. Objects, subtypes, thresholds, and success criteria cannot be rewritten afterward as “we really intended a different question.” A new question may receive a separate version; the old failure remains under its original arrangement."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Locatable provenance","leftText":"The material and version can be found.","rightTitle":"Explicit support relation","rightText":"One can explain how the material supports the specific claim and what it still cannot exclude."},{"leftTitle":"No record","leftText":"Nothing was found within the specified material and search scope.","rightTitle":"Records establish absence","rightText":"Requires a sufficiently reliable mechanism by which traces should be left and detected."},{"leftTitle":"Protective nondisclosure","leftText":"Has explicit protective grounds, narrow scope, duration, and credible review.","rightTitle":"A claim exempt from review","rightText":"No such exemption exists; inability to review requires downgrading."}],"boundaries":["Snow's record of unavailable information differs from respondents' reports of not drinking that water. Neither may be rewritten in this lesson as an error-free ground truth of exposure.","Counterexamples must target specific claims. One inconsistent number may affect a precise estimate without overturning all independent material; failure of a shared source or object boundary may affect more conclusions.","Material not set down beforehand may honestly support exploration or historical explanation. If nothing was set down in advance, this cannot be repaired by fabricating time records."],"questions":["Can a formal announcement directly establish that every on-site worker followed it?","If individual data cannot be disclosed for privacy reasons, may the public conclusion retain the highest certainty?","After a counterexample appears following the evidence cutoff, may the original indicator be deleted and replaced by a favorable one?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-2-5/figures/section-2-5.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"Two different kinds of “did not drink” in Snow's investigation","href":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/72894/pg72894-images.html"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"Two different kinds of “did not drink” in Snow's investigation","text":"Page 40 of Snow's 1855 original separately records six cases in which information could not be obtained because those concerned had died or left, and another six in which respondents reported that patients had not drunk Broad Street pump water before becoming ill. This section uses only that distinction: inability to know and obtaining a negative report are different statuses of material.","sourceLabel":"Snow, 1855 second edition, p.40","sourceUrl":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/72894/pg72894-images.html","sourceLocator":"Printed page 40, paragraph “With regard to the deaths occurring in the locality belonging to the pump”","boundary":"The author's interview records are not omniscient exposure measurements. Unknowns are not filled in as negatives, and reports are not treated as having excluded indirect water consumption.","href":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/72894/pg72894-images.html"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"2.5","start_line":345,"end_line":350,"startLine":345,"endLine":350}],"outputId":"section-2-5","sourceHash":"fcf100a5c8688d1e0337c52750ce3ad21fb5dbcc250f97946602ce094345f825","argumentSteps":["Premise: material generation and visibility determine which possible situations it can distinguish. Authentic provenance does not ensure that measurement fits the target.","Connection: material becomes bounded evidence only by stating, item by item, “why this record supports this claim, and which alternative explanations it remains compatible with.”","Reverse check: if the same absence could result either from nonoccurrence or from nonrecording, absence cannot uniquely support nonoccurrence.","Conclusion: state evidence strength, competing explanations, and failures by claim. Opacity and missingness limit the corresponding conclusions rather than automatically eliminating all existing material.","Authorization responsibility: moving from judgment to action still requires norms, lawful authorization, an action ceiling, and stopping conditions. A chain of sources does not itself create authority."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: what does a blank log actually exclude?","text":"Suppose an event always causes the system to generate and retain a retrievable record, and the query scope is complete. Under these conditions, “no record” can count against occurrence. Now suppose the recorder operates only when connected, and the target period may include a network outage. The same blank cannot distinguish nonoccurrence from occurrence unrecorded during an outage. What changed is the material-generation condition, not the researcher's preferred answer.","assumptions":"Both logging systems are teaching assumptions; no claim is made that actual logs are 100% reliable.","boundary":"Actual evidential force against occurrence requires material on recording rates, retention policies, permissions, and search coverage. It cannot inherit certainty from this construction."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"real_counterevidence","text":"In the historical record, Snow separately records six cases with unavailable drinking-water information and six reporting no consumption of pump water.","effect":"“Unknown” must not be merged into “no”; interview reports themselves must still acknowledge limitations from recollection and omitted exposures.","sourceIds":["snow-missing"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Teaching boundary: after discovering failure, changing the original target from “reduce total waiting time” to “allow more people into the queue,” then claiming the original plan succeeded.","effect":"Different targets require a new, separate record. Rewriting success criteria cannot erase the original failure.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Can a formal announcement directly establish that every on-site worker followed it?","answer":"No. An announcement is textual material; execution is a separate event requiring corresponding records and connection evidence.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"If individual data cannot be disclosed for privacy reasons, may the public conclusion retain the highest certainty?","answer":"Not automatically. Provide protective grounds, minimum scope, duration, and credible review, and limit public conclusions according to what can actually be verified.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"After a counterexample appears following the evidence cutoff, may the original indicator be deleted and replaced by a favorable one?","answer":"The original result cannot be erased. Preserve the failure, downgrade or suspend as agreed in advance, and create a separate record for the new target.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-2-5/figures/section-2-5.png","alt":"Source: who/instrument/archive; materials and observation records: generation/organization/selection; evidence and counterexamples: for which claim; action authorization: norms/permissions/stopping; bounded judgment: support and unresolved matters coexist; new counterexamples or residuals: preserve the original failure. Conceptual diagram based on §2.5; arrows indicate argumentative and procedural conversions, not materials naturally becoming authorization.","caption":"Conceptual diagram based on §2.5; arrows indicate argumentative and procedural conversions, not materials naturally becoming authorization."}],"diagramBrief":"Clicking any conversion should display its grounds and ceiling of support. Switching among “no record,” “explicit negative record” and “non-disclosure for protection” should preserve these three statuses without inventing data.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"A locatable primary historical passage directly demonstrates the difference between unknowns and negative reports, making it suitable for the evidence-chain and missingness questions."},"openQuestions":[],"taskId":"S-2-5","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical research manuscript, Chinese text lines 315–328; see sourceRefs for each section's exact range","claim_support":["2.5: terminology, contracts, burdens of argument and boundaries in the source; does not certify empirical facts","2.5.1: terminology, contracts, burdens of argument and boundaries in the source; does not certify empirical facts"],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2.1.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"snow-missing","title":"John Snow, On the Mode of Communication of Cholera","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/72894/pg72894-images.html","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"Second edition, 1855; digital transcription dated 2024-02-07","locator":"Second edition, 1855, p. 40, drinking-water investigation passage","claim_support":["2.5: six cases for which information could not be obtained were recorded separately from six other cases reporting no consumption of pump water."],"limits":["These are historical interview records, not infallible classifications of actual exposure."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"obermeyer-measure","title":"Obermeyer et al., Dissecting racial bias in an algorithm used to manage the health of populations","url":"https://www.ftc.gov/system/files/documents/public_events/1548288/privacycon-2020-ziad_obermeyer.pdf","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"2019-10-25，Science366:447—453","locator":"Science 366 (2019), 447–453; pp. 447–449, Data and analytic strategy, Health disparities conditional on risk score, Mechanism of bias; PDF pp. 1–3","claim_support":["2.5.1: data came from a large academic hospital during 2013–2015.","2.5.1: the algorithm used healthcare costs as its prediction label; at the same score, the two groups had similar costs in the following year but different health burdens. This does not establish individual or aggregate prediction accuracy."],"limits":["The cited main text and relevant figure captions were read; individual-level data were not obtained for recalculation.","Do not convert group comparisons into individual diagnoses, equate cost accuracy with accuracy about health needs, or extrapolate to all models."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v2.1","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2.1, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2.1 / 2026-11-19","locator":"2.5　证据的条件; Chinese source lines 331–336; SHA-256 2128af7cfa4c63b58207521a375aa34c2aea230d47e1c04a5afec3334e290d28","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2.1; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-2-5/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-2-5/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-2-5/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-2-5/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v2.1; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"21fca5a5bf35657f509f2da802b3ae573ddcc69bca3256242dfdfde5f3053303","source_sha256":"2128af7cfa4c63b58207521a375aa34c2aea230d47e1c04a5afec3334e290d28"}},"2.5.1":{"title":"2.5.1 Measurement Objects and Data Generation","lead":"A score can be calculated very accurately without measuring what you actually care about. Measurement asks first about the target and data generation, then about error magnitude and comparability.","explanation":["Source location: Chinese source lines 337–344; source anchor section-2-5-1; short quotation: “First state what is to be measured and how the materials are generated.” See the corresponding section on this page for the current source; the downloaded original research excerpt preserves the historical manuscript.","The source distinguishes five kinds of object. Directly recorded quantities, such as counts under an established protocol, still entail responsibility for missed records and event definitions. Definitional composite indicators combine components by rule; correct calculation does not establish a latent attribute that the indicator reflects. Latent-attribute measurement must explain the model relating observable indicators to an attribute not directly visible, alternatives and validation. Formative conditions concern conditions that jointly constitute or affect the target; they need not rise and fall together as reflective indicators of one attribute would. A predictive proxy only needs predictive capability for a future target; it does not thereby become a direct measure of another target.","Thus a test score can reliably record performance in the current item domain without fully representing long-term capability. Declining service use may indicate unmet needs, and increasing diagnoses may result from easier detection rather than more disease. These are different data-generation paths; observing one direction must not automatically select the most appealing story.","Comparisons across groups or periods must fit the purpose. Estimating latent means requires interpretable correspondence in scale units and origins; making person-level threshold decisions also requires analysis of individual error and consequences. Using the same scoring formula in two groups does not establish identical item meanings, response strategies or measurement relations. If shared anchors drift, larger samples may only estimate the same unidentified problem more precisely; seek external anchors or perform sensitivity analysis.","Improvement in an indicator may have at least four causes: genuine improvement in the target; behavior that adapts to a narrow metric without improving the broader target; changes in sample or reporting composition; or fabricated records. These mechanisms can coexist, but indicator change alone must not trigger accusations of fabrication. Two valid indicators can also move in opposite directions while both being correct if one measures a total and the other a subgroup.","The actual algorithm case illustrates proxy mismatch: costs were the predicted label, and the study compared costs and health burdens across groups at the same risk score. Similar cross-group cost calibration does not mean small individual prediction errors or good aggregate prediction accuracy. When identifying care needs, the relationship between cost and need must also be validated as comparable in the target groups. Reconsidering the label does not declare all proxies invalid; it requires evidence of fit for the current purpose."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Predicting a label accurately","leftText":"The model predicts the explicitly specified target well.","rightTitle":"Measuring the attribute actually of concern","rightText":"The label's relationship to the target attribute and intended use has been checked."},{"leftTitle":"Definitional composite indicator","leftText":"The components and weights define the score's composition.","rightTitle":"Latent-attribute measurement","rightText":"Requires an argument for how observed indicators reflect the latent attribute and for their comparability."},{"leftTitle":"Formative conditions","leftText":"The conditions may move in different directions while jointly affecting or constituting the target.","rightTitle":"Reflective indicators","rightText":"Represent manifestations of a latent attribute within the model and require a different testing logic."},{"leftTitle":"Indicators move in opposite directions","leftText":"This may result from different objects, denominators, weights or tasks.","rightTitle":"The data must be fabricated","rightText":"Requires independent evidence; different directions do not directly imply fabrication."}],"boundaries":["The Obermeyer study evaluates an algorithm in a particular hospital and a 2013–2015 sample; it must not be used to judge unexamined individuals or all current models.","Observed differences in group calibration do not mean every social mechanism has been disentangled; changing a label also does not automatically guarantee long-term health benefits or fulfillment of all fairness norms.","High internal consistency, an identical formula or a larger sample cannot alone guarantee a latent-attribute measure's construct validity and cross-group fit."],"questions":["If a test is very stable on retesting, can one immediately conclude it measures long-term capability?","Can falling service use directly establish falling need?","If the total number of overdue cases rises while the overdue rate falls, must the data be fabricated?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-2-5/figures/section-2-5-1.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"The 2019 study of a cost-prediction algorithm: proxy and need are different targets","href":"https://www.ftc.gov/system/files/documents/public_events/1548288/privacycon-2020-ziad_obermeyer.pdf"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"The 2019 study of a cost-prediction algorithm: proxy and need are different targets","text":"Obermeyer et al. analyzed data from a large academic hospital during 2013–2015. The audited algorithm predicted healthcare costs. At the same risk score, the study found similar costs in the following year for the two groups but a heavier health burden among Black patients. This indicates more consistent cross-group cost calibration, not necessarily high aggregate or individual prediction accuracy; calibration also cannot replace a test of fitness for care needs.","sourceLabel":"Obermeyer et al., Dissecting racial bias in an algorithm used to manage the health of populations","sourceUrl":"https://www.ftc.gov/system/files/documents/public_events/1548288/privacycon-2020-ziad_obermeyer.pdf","sourceLocator":"Science 366 (2019), 447–453; printed pp. 447–449, Data and analytic strategy, Health disparities conditional on risk score, Mechanism of bias; PDF pp. 1–3","boundary":"Interpret the paper within its specific sample, indicators and purpose. This guide has not recalculated individual data, makes no individual medical judgment and does not claim that changing the label has improved actual long-term outcomes.","href":"https://www.ftc.gov/system/files/documents/public_events/1548288/privacycon-2020-ziad_obermeyer.pdf"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"2.5.1","start_line":351,"end_line":358,"startLine":351,"endLine":358}],"outputId":"section-2-5-1","sourceHash":"fcf100a5c8688d1e0337c52750ce3ad21fb5dbcc250f97946602ce094345f825","argumentSteps":["Premise: observed scores are jointly generated by the target attribute, measurement rules, opportunities for contact, selection and recording processes; different generation processes can produce the same score.","Classification: first explain whether the measurement concerns a direct quantity, definitional composite, latent attribute, formative condition or predictive proxy, and identify which errors affect its current use.","Connection: accurate prediction of a label directly supports only that label's task; using it to judge need, capability or value requires separate evidence connecting measurement and purpose.","Comparison conditions: when groups or times change, check units, origins, anchors, individual errors and sample composition; an identical formula does not imply identical psychological processes.","Conclusion: when reporting indicator changes, retain competing generation mechanisms and propose discriminating checks; without sufficient materials, do not promote one explanation into fact."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Mathematical teaching construction: a changing mean or a drifting scale origin","text":"Assume an observed score S=θ+b, where θ is the attribute to be measured and b the scale origin. An observed mean rising from 4 to 6 over two periods could reflect θ increasing by 2 with b unchanged, or unchanged θ with b increasing by 2. Observing S alone cannot distinguish them. Even infinitely large samples and error-free observed means in each period still require external anchoring or testable constraints to interpret the change.","assumptions":"An additive measurement model, common units and no other error; used only to illustrate identifiability.","boundary":"Not measured facts about any scale or population; a particular measurement relation may be nonlinear and require a different model."},{"title":"Teaching construction: two correct indicators can move in opposite directions","text":"A service handles 10 cases in the first period, of which 2 are overdue, then 30 in the second, of which 3 are overdue. The total overdue count rises from 2 to 3, while the rate falls from 20% to 10%. Both calculations are correct: one answers total burden, the other the proportion of cases. Opposite directions alone cannot establish fabrication by either side.","assumptions":"Case counts are stipulated for teaching, and event definitions and windows are comparable; there is no sampling or underreporting.","boundary":"This does not establish improvement in a real service; case difficulty, demand and people not served in the second period may still affect evaluation."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"real_counterevidence","text":"The study shows similar next-year costs in the two groups at the same risk score, indicating relatively consistent cross-group cost calibration, while Black patients have a heavier health burden.","effect":"This limits the inference that “consistent cross-group cost calibration fairly identifies health needs”; it does not establish high individual cost-prediction accuracy or invalidate the cost-prediction task itself.","sourceIds":["obermeyer-measure"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"In the teaching construction, scale-origin drift and genuine attribute change produce the same observed change in means.","effect":"More observations of the same kind cannot alone identify the two mechanisms; add anchoring or make sensitivity explicit.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"If a test is very stable on retesting, can one immediately conclude it measures long-term capability?","answer":"No. Stability is one property; construct content, generalization beyond the item domain, measurement relations and fitness for use still require argument.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Can falling service use directly establish falling need?","answer":"No. Accessibility, price, supply, sampling and recording changes can all alter use; discriminating materials are required.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"If the total number of overdue cases rises while the overdue rate falls, must the data be fabricated?","answer":"No. A larger denominator can produce this result. First check the objects, denominators, windows and calculations, then investigate specific anomalies.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-2-5/figures/section-2-5-1.png","alt":"Data generation: service accessibility/recording; observed label: costs, assuming accurate prediction of that label; evaluation of the cost task: answers only cost prediction; the target actually of interest: care needs; the bridge between needs and costs: groups/time/errors; evaluation of use for needs: separate evidence is required and must not be skipped. Conceptual diagram based on §2.5.1; for cost–need mismatch, see Obermeyer et al. 2019, pp. 447–449. No invented individual values or effect curves.","caption":"Conceptual diagram based on §2.5.1; for cost–need mismatch, see Obermeyer et al. 2019, pp. 447–449. No invented individual values or effect curves."}],"diagramBrief":"The upper path checks label prediction; the lower checks the intended target use. An interactive switch between evaluation labels “costs/health burden” should replace the corresponding evidence and indicators, not merely rename an axis.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"The primary paper directly examines mismatch between prediction labels and health needs, fitting this section's distinction between measurement objects and proxy uses."},"openQuestions":["Transferring this conclusion to another institution or current algorithm requires that version's labels, data-generation process, group comparability and actual outcome materials."],"taskId":"S-2-5","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical research manuscript, Chinese text lines 315–328; see sourceRefs for each section's exact range","claim_support":["2.5: terminology, contracts, burdens of argument and boundaries in the source; does not certify empirical facts","2.5.1: terminology, contracts, burdens of argument and boundaries in the source; does not certify empirical facts"],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2.1.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"snow-missing","title":"John Snow, On the Mode of Communication of Cholera","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/72894/pg72894-images.html","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"Second edition, 1855; digital transcription dated 2024-02-07","locator":"Second edition, 1855, p. 40, drinking-water investigation passage","claim_support":["2.5: six cases for which information could not be obtained were recorded separately from six other cases reporting no consumption of pump water."],"limits":["These are historical interview records, not infallible classifications of actual exposure."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"obermeyer-measure","title":"Obermeyer et al., Dissecting racial bias in an algorithm used to manage the health of populations","url":"https://www.ftc.gov/system/files/documents/public_events/1548288/privacycon-2020-ziad_obermeyer.pdf","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"2019-10-25，Science366:447—453","locator":"Science 366 (2019), 447–453; pp. 447–449, Data and analytic strategy, Health disparities conditional on risk score, Mechanism of bias; PDF pp. 1–3","claim_support":["2.5.1: data came from a large academic hospital during 2013–2015.","2.5.1: the algorithm used healthcare costs as its prediction label; at the same score, the two groups had similar costs in the following year but different health burdens. This does not establish individual or aggregate prediction accuracy."],"limits":["The cited main text and relevant figure captions were read; individual-level data were not obtained for recalculation.","Do not convert group comparisons into individual diagnoses, equate cost accuracy with accuracy about health needs, or extrapolate to all models."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v2.1","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2.1, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2.1 / 2026-11-19","locator":"2.5.1 测量对象与资料生成; Chinese source lines 337–344; SHA-256 2128af7cfa4c63b58207521a375aa34c2aea230d47e1c04a5afec3334e290d28","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2.1; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-2-5/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-2-5/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-2-5/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-2-5/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v2.1; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"6d1256b83f698b7f2ba960ea331c0904d2639317f7da7f5c37e145a4152db770","source_sha256":"2128af7cfa4c63b58207521a375aa34c2aea230d47e1c04a5afec3334e290d28"}},"2.6":{"title":"2.6 Conditions for Analogy","lead":"The most useful role of “this resembles something else” is to suggest what to investigate next; it cannot directly import that other thing's causes, evaluations and solutions.","explanation":["Source location: Chinese source lines 345–350; source anchor section-2-6; short quotation: “Analogy is only a tool for generating questions.” See the corresponding section on this page for the current source; the downloaded original research excerpt preserves the historical manuscript.","An adequate analogy lists at least the source domain, target domain, shared structure, key differences, nontransferable conditions, variable mapping and discriminating tests, and explicitly calls itself an analogy in the main text. Comparing a queue to stored water, for example, may share an “inflow, stock, outflow” structure. But people can leave, change choices or respond strategically, and water volume and numbers of people must not be combined without specifying units and processes.","A variable mapping turns “similarity” from rhetoric into a checkable proposal: which source-domain quantity corresponds to which target-domain quantity, under which operations the relationship persists, and when it fails. Similar diagrams, identical words, appealing stories and similar outcomes do not complete this mapping. Treating “organizational immunity” or “social temperature” as measured mechanisms exemplifies the overreach to avoid.","An analogy can generate a checklist: which source-domain channels, boundaries or competing explanations merit investigation in the target domain? But empirical causal relations in the source do not automatically become target-domain facts. Corresponding operations, measurements and alternative explanations still need testing in the target, and analogy cannot replace the requirements of a formal test.","Contrary conditions must actually constrain the claim. If researchers say “it still looks similar” regardless of what they observe, the analogy cannot eliminate mistaken candidates. A reasonable formulation states that particular target-domain behavior, object identities or resource constraints invalidate the mapping; if discriminating tests are incomplete, retain only a heuristic candidate. Normative evaluations and action plans particularly must not transfer directly on resemblance alone."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Structural similarity","leftText":"Some relations or indicators can correspond.","rightTitle":"Identical mechanism","rightText":"Identical causal processes and operating conditions require new discriminating evidence."},{"leftTitle":"Generating questions","leftText":"Deciding what to investigate first.","rightTitle":"Confirming answers","rightText":"Which relations the materials can already support has a different evidential force."},{"leftTitle":"Evaluation within an analogy","leftText":"A judgment reached under the source domain's value criteria.","rightTitle":"Justification in the target domain","rightText":"Targets, rights, risks and authorization must be made explicit anew."}],"boundaries":["In actual research, similar statistics can result from different formation processes; two similar numbers cannot establish identical social institutions.","An analogy need not match in every respect to be useful, but the part claimed to transfer must be explicit; stop using it or narrow it when differences affect key premises.","This guide does not use biological analogies to assign standing, personality or governance arrangements to actual individuals or groups."],"questions":["If two systems both have hub-and-spoke diagrams, can one directly conclude that the central nodes control all resources?","When comparing human behavior to water flow, what should be added first?","If an analogy faces counterevidence but can still inspire a question, must it be discarded entirely?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-2-6/figures/section-2-6.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"A 2022 ancient-DNA study's explicit limits on resemblance to great apes","href":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9581778/"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"A 2022 ancient-DNA study's explicit limits on resemblance to great apes","text":"Skov et al. compared patterns of genetic diversity when studying Neanderthal communities, but explicitly warned in “Inferring social organization” that similar ratios of Y-chromosome to mitochondrial coalescence times do not necessarily imply the same social organization; multiple scenarios can produce similar statistics.","sourceLabel":"Skov et al., Genetic insights into the social organization of Neanderthals","sourceUrl":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9581778/","sourceLocator":"Nature 610 (2022), 519–525; PMC main text, Inferring social organization, the cautionary paragraph following the comparison of 47 human populations and 10 great-ape subspecies (this guide does not use its specific comparative values)","boundary":"Only the authors' limitation on analogy is used; ancient DNA is not used to evaluate modern groups, and their demographic-history model is not recalculated.","href":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9581778/"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"2.6","start_line":359,"end_line":364,"startLine":359,"endLine":364}],"outputId":"section-2-6","sourceHash":"fcf100a5c8688d1e0337c52750ce3ad21fb5dbcc250f97946602ce094345f825","argumentSteps":["Premise: known relations in the source domain depend on its particular objects, variables and operating conditions; similarity covers only some of these.","Connection: writing down mappings and differences first permits judgment of which source-domain questions can be target-domain candidates and which observations would invalidate the mapping.","Conclusion: analogy permits asking questions and generating relations for testing, not directly declaring that target-domain mechanisms, value judgments or actions are established.","Evidential responsibility: target-domain support must come from its own materials and bridges; even reliable source-domain research cannot fill in unobserved conditions in the target.","Responsibility for failure: retain dissimilarities and counterevidence; mapping failures must not be explained away as target-domain “anomalies” while the analogy is never revised."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching analogy: when a queue stops resembling stored water","text":"The source domain is a water tank and the target a service queue. Mapping inflow to new arrivals, water level to queue length and outflow to completed service can generate the question to investigate: “Does the stock accumulate when arrivals exceed processing capacity?” But if people can leave midway, obtain duplicate queue numbers or decide not to arrive after seeing a long queue, additional channels are needed. Record arrivals, completions, exits and duplicate counts to test which conservation equation applies, rather than directly substituting tank parameters for a population.","assumptions":"This is an explicitly labeled teaching analogy; no real queue has been observed. The target domain must declare its own units and event rules.","boundary":"The analogy does not establish service effectiveness, still less that people should be controlled like water; empirical and normative conditions in the target domain require separate checks."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"real_counterevidence","text":"Skov et al.'s 2022 study explicitly warns that similar Y-chromosome/mitochondrial coalescence-time ratios in Neanderthals and great apes do not necessarily imply identical social organization; multiple scenarios can produce similar ratios.","effect":"A real study itself distinguishes statistical resemblance from social explanation; similar indicators must not be used to bypass generation mechanisms.","sourceIds":["skov-analogy"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"If two systems both have hub-and-spoke diagrams, can one directly conclude that the central nodes control all resources?","answer":"No. Similar-looking diagrams do not reveal the meaning of edges, actual flows, permissions, or alternative channels. Materials from the target domain must be checked.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"When comparing human behavior to water flow, what should be added first?","answer":"Specify mappings of variables and units, crucial differences such as people's exit and strategic responses, conditions that cannot transfer, and target-domain records capable of distinguishing these processes.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"If an analogy faces counterevidence but can still inspire a question, must it be discarded entirely?","answer":"Not necessarily. Restrict or remove the failed part while retaining insights that clearly remain applicable. The original strong mechanism conclusion must not be retained.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-2-6/figures/section-2-6.png","alt":"Source domain, existing relations and conditions; explicit mapping, shared structure plus crucial differences; candidate questions in the target domain, still requiring testing; causal/normative conclusions from the source domain, which must not transfer directly; conditions that do not transfer, counter-signals/failure modes; independent target-domain materials, testing operations and alternative explanations. A conceptual diagram based on §2.6; for the cautious interpretation in the ancient-DNA example, see Skov et al. 2022. No data curves or automatic transfer conclusions.","caption":"A conceptual diagram based on §2.6; for the cautious interpretation in the ancient-DNA example, see Skov et al. 2022. No data curves or automatic transfer conclusions."}],"diagramBrief":"An interaction could reveal a crucial difference and show which mapping it blocks. It must not provide an automatic button that permits action because similarity is high.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"The primary study explicitly states that similar indicators cannot replace evidence of social mechanisms, directly illustrating the ceiling on analogy's applicability."},"openQuestions":[],"taskId":"S-2-6","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical Chinese research manuscript, full-text lines 329–340; see sourceRefs for each section's exact range","claim_support":["2.6: terms, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; this does not certify empirical facts","2.6.1: terms, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; this does not certify empirical facts"],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2.1.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"skov-analogy","title":"Skov et al., Genetic insights into the social organization of Neanderthals","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9581778/","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"2022-10-19，DOI10.1038/s41586-022-05283-y","locator":"Nature 610:519–525; the paragraphs in Inferring social organization discussing the Y/mtDNA coalescence-time ratio and multiple scenarios","claim_support":["2.6: the authors explicitly caution that similar genetic ratios need not represent the same social organization, and that several processes could produce them."],"limits":["The main-text passages used in this lesson have been read; genetic data and supplementary models have not been recalculated.","Used only to illustrate the ceiling on support from analogy."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"skov-kinship","title":"Skov et al. 2022, kinship analysis (the same study)","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9581778/","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"2022-10-19; a different supporting passage in the same paper","locator":"Genetic relationships, the Chagyrskaya D/H paragraph; Table 1","claim_support":["2.6.1: the original study identifies D and H as father and daughter on the basis of first-degree relatedness and different mitochondrial genomes."],"limits":["The same paper as skov-analogy; these are not counted as two independent studies.","Kinship does not automatically establish ruling status. This limit on use is the lesson's analysis, not an institutional conclusion attributed to the original authors."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v2.1","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2.1, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2.1 / 2026-11-19","locator":"2.6　类比的条件; Chinese source lines 345–350; SHA-256 2128af7cfa4c63b58207521a375aa34c2aea230d47e1c04a5afec3334e290d28","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2.1; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-2-6/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-2-6/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-2-6/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-2-6/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v2.1; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"0aab7cfe5f677be02c6e8d89d6aa68a61ceba34934f7295ff79dbf8962ab6c35","source_sha256":"2128af7cfa4c63b58207521a375aa34c2aea230d47e1c04a5afec3334e290d28"}},"2.6.1":{"title":"2.6.1 Analogy, Formal Instances, and Tested Comparisons","lead":"Resembling something, belonging to the same class within a model, and being supported in the target world are three different statuses. Keeping them separate neither devalues analogy nor lends it evidential force it has not acquired.","explanation":["Source location: Chinese source, lines 351–356; original anchor section-2-6-1. Short quotation: “A heuristic analogy, a formal instance satisfying explicit mathematical premises, and a target-domain relation tested against independent materials are three different states.” See the corresponding section on this page for the current source; the original research extract available for download preserves the historical manuscript.","The first is heuristic analogy: explain similarities and differences and propose a relation worth testing. The second is a formal instance: variables and operations satisfy all premises of a mathematical proposition, so the conclusion follows by proof within that model, irrespective of whether the stories seem alike. The third is a tested target-domain relation: independent materials together with bridges support the specific relation, and the support comes from those materials, not from calling it similar. The three can work together in one study, but must not automatically be upgraded from one status to another.","For example, the existence of a path in an abstract graph is a formal property. Mapping an actual link to the ability to send information requires checking that the corresponding channel is available. Claiming that information actually traveled through it and changed an outcome requires further evidence of events and effects. Even with isomorphic graphs, edges representing cooperation, power supply, or kinship have entirely different meanings for real-world operations.","Historical and archaeological materials require even clearer separation of layers: how traces physically formed is one layer; which activity or function they correspond to is another; the social relations and institutions involving participants are a third. Similar tool marks may raise questions about activities, but do not automatically identify the same power regime. Kinship identification can support a biological relationship without by itself showing who ruled whom. More accurate dating helps establish sequence, but does not by itself answer what an event meant.","Respecting different sources of knowledge does not mean giving them equal weight for every question. Dating measurements, genetic relations, local knowledge, oral accounts, and textual context may each answer non-interchangeable questions. Material unsuited to one claim is not thereby useless for every question. Conversely, the importance of a form of knowledge must not exempt it from criticism required for the proposed use.","This section continues using the ancient-DNA study but adds a new focus: the scope of support for identifying a relation. Kinship conclusions depend on genetic materials and interpretation, whereas conclusions about rule or normative standing need separate social evidence. This limit is the lesson's analysis of how the material may be used; it does not invent political conclusions that the study's authors never proposed and then refute them."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Formal instance","leftText":"The object and mapping satisfy specified mathematical premises.","rightTitle":"Evidence for a real-world case","rightText":"The target-domain mapping and premises have appropriate observational and design support."},{"leftTitle":"Genetic kinship","leftText":"An evidential judgment about biological relatedness.","rightTitle":"Social power","rightText":"Roles, rules, domination, resources, and institutional relations; these must not be inferred automatically from kinship."},{"leftTitle":"Similar traces","leftText":"Clues to possible formation processes.","rightTitle":"The same institution","rightText":"Requires additional grounds concerning activities and social relations."}],"boundaries":["The father–daughter conclusion in the ancient-DNA study does not by itself establish male rule, inheritance rules, or the community's full institutional arrangements. This lesson does not claim that such institutions were necessarily absent; this material is simply insufficient to decide.","Formal isomorphism preserves the stated structural properties, not every everyday meaning and causal parameter. More accurate dating likewise does not automatically recover an event's entire meaning.","Comparative research need not always be merely suggestive. After testing with independent target-domain materials, it may take on limited argumentative responsibilities according to its actual design."],"questions":["Are two perfectly isomorphic organizational charts enough to establish identical actual decision mechanisms in the organizations?","Does identifying two ancient individuals as father and daughter directly justify writing that the father ruled the community?","After an analogy gains independent target-domain materials, does its support come from the analogy itself?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-2-6/figures/section-2-6-1.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"The father–daughter relationship at Chagyrskaya: which relation do the materials support?","href":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9581778/"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"The father–daughter relationship at Chagyrskaya: which relation do the materials support?","text":"In Genetic relationships, Skov et al.'s 2022 paper combines first-degree relatedness with different mitochondrial genomes to identify Chagyrskaya H as D's daughter. This lesson distinguishes the scope of that finding: it is a kinship judgment supported by genetic materials. Ruling status or institutions must not be added solely on the basis of that relation.","sourceLabel":"Skov et al. 2022, main-text kinship analysis","sourceUrl":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9581778/","sourceLocator":"Nature 610 (2022), 519–525; the paragraph beginning “One adult male individual, Chagyrskaya D” in Genetic relationships in the PMC text, and Table 1","boundary":"The statement that kinship does not automatically establish rule is this lesson's limitation on use. It does not claim that the paper's authors proposed or rejected a particular ruling system, or generalize from this site to all Neanderthal societies.","href":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9581778/"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"2.6.1","start_line":365,"end_line":370,"startLine":365,"endLine":370}],"outputId":"section-2-6-1","sourceHash":"fcf100a5c8688d1e0337c52750ce3ad21fb5dbcc250f97946602ce094345f825","argumentSteps":["Classification premise: specify whether the item is a proposal of similarity, a formal instance satisfying mathematical premises, or an empirical comparison supported by target-domain materials.","Formal connection: if graph isomorphism or a formal instance of a proposition is claimed, verify one by one the objects, relations, and operations preserved by the mapping. Only preserved properties may transfer through the proof.","Empirical connection: real-world relations require examination of material generation, operations, timing, identities, and alternative explanations. Formal isomorphism does not directly establish actual use or causal effects.","Historical connection: moving from traces to functions and then to social relations requires listing new assumptions and discriminating materials at each step. Evidence at other layers must not manufacture truth for a missing layer.","Conclusion: tested comparison may enter an argument, but must precisely identify where support comes from and retain relations that have not been tested as such."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching mathematical construction: isomorphic graphs can transfer reachability, not power","text":"Let G₁ have nodes {a,b,c} and edges {a→b,b→c}, and G₂ have nodes {1,2,3} and edges {1→2,2→3}. The mapping a↦1, b↦2, c↦3 preserves each edge, so if a path exists from a to c in G₁, a path exists from 1 to 3 in G₂. If one graph is interpreted as an authorization chain and the other as contact among colleagues, graph isomorphism does not also prove the authorization semantics.","assumptions":"Both graphs and all edges are stipulated for teaching. Authorization chain/contact among colleagues are merely two hypothetical interpretations.","boundary":"The proof covers only reachability in the graphs. It does not establish that the actual relations exist, that information is transmitted, or who has authority to act."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Teaching boundary: tools from two burials show very similar wear, and a researcher directly concludes that the two places had the same ruling institutions.","effect":"This skips at least the several bridges between trace formation, activity functions, and social relations. Return to candidate status and examine the relevant materials.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"unresolved_boundary","text":"Kinship evidence can support a father–daughter relationship, but by itself cannot distinguish multiple possible arrangements of social power.","effect":"Leave power relations unresolved rather than withdrawing the grounded kinship result.","sourceIds":["skov-kinship"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Are two perfectly isomorphic organizational charts enough to establish identical actual decision mechanisms in the organizations?","answer":"No. Isomorphism preserves only the relational structure defined in the graph. Actual edge meanings, processes, resources, operations, and outcomes require separate examination.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Does identifying two ancient individuals as father and daughter directly justify writing that the father ruled the community?","answer":"No. Kinship and rule are different relations; the latter requires social and institutional evidence. Uncertainty about rule does not cancel the kinship evidence.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"After an analogy gains independent target-domain materials, does its support come from the analogy itself?","answer":"It comes from those materials, designs, and bridges. Update the material's status and the conclusion's scope instead of treating resemblance as additional evidence.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-2-6/figures/section-2-6-1.png","alt":"Heuristic analogy, similarities plus differences; formal instance, complete premises plus mapping; tested target-domain relation, independent materials plus bridges; how traces formed, physical process; which activities they correspond to, functional explanation; which social relations, with separate evidence for institutions/power. The upper row shows three statuses of materials and arguments, not an automatic upgrading pipeline; the lower row separates layers of archaeological interpretation. Based on §2.6.1 and the limited kinship example in Skov et al. 2022.","caption":"The upper row shows three statuses of materials and arguments, not an automatic upgrading pipeline; the lower row separates layers of archaeological interpretation. Based on §2.6.1 and the limited kinship example in Skov et al. 2022."}],"diagramBrief":"Do not draw automatic upgrading arrows in the upper row. Each transition between layers in the lower row may open supporting materials and competing explanations. If materials are missing, stop at the supported layer instead of filling the remaining layers with supposed facts.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"This actual study clearly illustrates the kind of relation supported by materials and the additional responsibilities of social interpretation. It reuses the preceding section's case without repeating its analogy narrative."},"openQuestions":["Questions about rule, rights, and institutions require additional materials on activities, resources, and social relations. This lesson has not obtained evidence capable of deciding these questions."],"taskId":"S-2-6","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical Chinese research manuscript, full-text lines 329–340; see sourceRefs for each section's exact range","claim_support":["2.6: terms, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; this does not certify empirical facts","2.6.1: terms, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; this does not certify empirical facts"],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2.1.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"skov-analogy","title":"Skov et al., Genetic insights into the social organization of Neanderthals","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9581778/","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"2022-10-19，DOI10.1038/s41586-022-05283-y","locator":"Nature 610:519–525; the paragraphs in Inferring social organization discussing the Y/mtDNA coalescence-time ratio and multiple scenarios","claim_support":["2.6: the authors explicitly caution that similar genetic ratios need not represent the same social organization, and that several processes could produce them."],"limits":["The main-text passages used in this lesson have been read; genetic data and supplementary models have not been recalculated.","Used only to illustrate the ceiling on support from analogy."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"skov-kinship","title":"Skov et al. 2022, kinship analysis (the same study)","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9581778/","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"2022-10-19; a different supporting passage in the same paper","locator":"Genetic relationships, the Chagyrskaya D/H paragraph; Table 1","claim_support":["2.6.1: the original study identifies D and H as father and daughter on the basis of first-degree relatedness and different mitochondrial genomes."],"limits":["The same paper as skov-analogy; these are not counted as two independent studies.","Kinship does not automatically establish ruling status. This limit on use is the lesson's analysis, not an institutional conclusion attributed to the original authors."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v2.1","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2.1, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2.1 / 2026-11-19","locator":"2.6.1 类比 形式实例与受检比较; Chinese source lines 351–356; SHA-256 2128af7cfa4c63b58207521a375aa34c2aea230d47e1c04a5afec3334e290d28","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2.1; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-2-6/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-2-6/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-2-6/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-2-6/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v2.1; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"e3a638e86113e48230652cd77c3a45ee4e1738ee68ef4cc59147fbb490e890e4","source_sha256":"2128af7cfa4c63b58207521a375aa34c2aea230d47e1c04a5afec3334e290d28"}},"2.7":{"title":"2.7 Conditions for Sources","lead":"A source list is not better merely because it is longer. Each judgment should lead back to the precise location that supports it. Citing theory to explain how to test something does not mean that real-world test results have been obtained.","explanation":["Source location: Chinese source, lines 357–364; original anchor section-2-7. Short quotation: “a method definition is not the same as empirical support.” See the corresponding section on this page for the current source; the original research extract available for download preserves the historical manuscript.","For each source, record the domain, case type, time and region, scope of support, limits of extrapolation, and relevant counterexamples. Separate original materials, secondary interpretations, and inferences made within this framework, so readers can trace a judgment to its original location and see which limited claims a given material is being used to support. Bidirectional links serve to check whether inference exceeds its bounds, not to label sources as authoritative.","Concept definitions, correspondences between elements, and method explanations determine what to ask, how to record it, and where to stop. They must not count as records of empirical evidence or serve as object facts or support for mechanisms. For example, the book's specification of how a channel-effect test (§4.4) tests a specified channel does not establish that the channel exists in an actual object. Its account of how C combines results does not mean that actual materials already support every combined link.","Applications for formal empirical support require independently recorded case materials: object and scale, time window, research design, null model, results, uncertainty, review or replication, and evidential level. This follows the two-stage admission in §2.1; it does not force every archive into a modern experimental format. Ordinary domain evidence retains its original design, while the support of a formal test is obtained under the corresponding pre-registration.","C serves different purposes, including determining results, specifying mechanism conditions, and normative bridging. It must not uniformly be treated as additional causal facts. Judgments about actual mechanisms under the conditional mechanisms still require corresponding case evidence. Question catalogues, conclusion rules, and explanations of the framework itself can at most specify testing responsibilities. Repeated citation will not fill gaps in external materials.","Sources closer to the original generation of information generally make specific details easier to check, but original materials may also contain errors, omissions, or interpretation. Examine version, generation process, precise location, and correspondence to claims separately; primary must not mean beyond criticism. The next section addresses source relations and reading scope, and the following section addresses independence and how errors propagate."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Method source","leftText":"Provides terms, design, or recording rules.","rightTitle":"Empirical source","rightText":"Provides objects, observations, or case results whose scope of support must be assessed."},{"leftTitle":"Primary record","leftText":"Close to the actual generation process, with specific locations that can be checked.","rightTitle":"Unconditional truth","rightText":"No source has this privilege; original records still have limitations."},{"leftTitle":"Source support","leftText":"Specifically supports a bounded claim.","rightTitle":"Authoritative endorsement","rightText":"Using prestige to bypass the support relation cannot substitute for evidence."}],"boundaries":["Neither the source text nor the skill can independently certify its empirical assertions about the real world. This lesson cites the theory only to explain what the author says and under which conditions.","Numerous references and complete bibliographic information only make checking easier; they do not replace the support relation between claims and facts.","Existing historical evidence need not be discarded because it has not become a formal test, but its historical standing does not automatically confer eligibility for formal tests either."],"questions":["Can citing this book's definition of channel effects count as evidence that a specified channel has received empirical support?","May an author's speculation in a primary paper be recorded as an original fact?","If the current claim changes from a measure was announced to a measure actually changed the outcome, is the original announcement source sufficient?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-2-7/figures/section-2-7.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"One original work, different scopes of support for different sentences","href":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/72894/pg72894-images.html"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"One original work, different scopes of support for different sentences","text":"Page 40 of Snow's 1855 original work supports his account of the September 7 meeting and removal of the pump handle the following day. Pages 51–52 also limit an interpretation attributing the subsequent decline directly to that action. This section links these locations separately to event dates and limits on causal attribution.","sourceLabel":"Snow, second edition, 1855; the corresponding event and limitation passages","sourceUrl":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/72894/pg72894-images.html","sourceLocator":"Printed p.40 and pp.51–52","boundary":"Date records are not evidence of a net effect. Expressions such as I learned or I judged in the original must retain their status as the author's account, rather than being merged with independent confirmation by later researchers.","href":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/72894/pg72894-images.html"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"2.7","start_line":371,"end_line":378,"startLine":371,"endLine":378}],"outputId":"section-2-7","sourceHash":"fcf100a5c8688d1e0337c52750ce3ad21fb5dbcc250f97946602ce094345f825","argumentSteps":["Premise: a document may contain definitions, facts, the author's interpretations, and unresolved speculation; different passages support different objects of inquiry.","Connection: state each claim's source location and support ceiling, separating original material, others' interpretations, and this lesson's inferences, so that the appropriateness of borrowing can be checked.","Exclusion: a method requirement alone contains no case results. It must not be inserted into empirical records to support the existence of a mechanism.","Conclusion: strong claims concerning formal tests and conditional mechanisms require corresponding external case materials. Existing domain materials retain their actual design; numbers or citation counts must not upgrade them.","Review responsibility: when a claim changes, recheck the support relation. The same document may support a date fact without supporting a net causal narrative beginning on that date."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: two usable kinds of content in one manual","text":"Suppose a manual's first chapter defines an acceptable reading as having error no greater than e, while an appendix records the error in one actual measurement. The definition supports the term's meaning, not the acceptability of that measurement. Only if measurement and calculation are reliable might the appendix data support the judgment about that case. If the appendix is merely a worked example, it still cannot become a real-world measurement.","assumptions":"The manual, threshold, and actual measurement are all teaching assumptions; no real publication is identified.","boundary":"The example illustrates source roles; it does not certify any actual equipment or case."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"real_counterevidence","text":"Snow recounts the September 7 interview and removal of the handle the following day, while later retaining the qualification that the decline had begun before the pump was disabled.","effect":"The same source supports the event date, but the existence of that date does not justify jumping to the claim that removing the handle caused the entire decline.","sourceIds":["snow-use"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Can citing this book's definition of channel effects count as evidence that a specified channel has received empirical support?","answer":"No. A definition specifies the testing task and requirements; it does not provide case materials for the target object.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"May an author's speculation in a primary paper be recorded as an original fact?","answer":"No. Distinguish records, analysis, and speculation, and mark the uncertainty expressed in the original.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"If the current claim changes from a measure was announced to a measure actually changed the outcome, is the original announcement source sufficient?","answer":"No. The event and causal target of the claim have changed. Execution, outcomes, and counterfactual comparisons require separate checking.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-2-7/figures/section-2-7.png","alt":"Original material, version/time/location; actual content, separating records from the author's interpretation; this lesson's bounded judgment, scope of support/counterexamples; framework definitions and methods, determining what to ask and how to check; external case evidence, object/design/results; application for formal empirical support, checking the corresponding conditions. A source-relations diagram based on §2.7; method definitions are not drawn as an evidential arrow leading to empirical support.","caption":"Source-relationship diagram based on 2.7; methodological definitions are not drawn as evidential arrows leading to empirical support."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static figure; interaction has not been implemented. References below to selecting, switching, or clicking are ideas for later design only. Two-way lookup: select a judgment to display the location of its material; select material to display the claims for which it is used. Methodological sources and empirical records retain distinct labels, with no score accumulated by counting publications.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"Two passages in the same original work can support judgments of different scope, directly demonstrating claim-by-claim source records."},"openQuestions":[],"taskId":"S-2-7","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical research manuscript, Chinese full-text lines 341–362; see sourceRefs for each section's exact range","claim_support":["2.7: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","2.7.1: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","2.7.2: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts."],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2.1.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"snow-use","title":"John Snow 1855: events and causal limitations","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/72894/pg72894-images.html","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"Second edition, 1855; digital transcription dated 2024-02-07","locator":"p.40；pp.51—52","claim_support":["2.7: accounts of the meeting and handle-removal dates; the limitation that the decline had begun before closure."],"limits":["Date facts do not directly support a net effect."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"snow-version","title":"Bibliographic information for John Snow's work and its Gutenberg digital edition","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/72894/pg72894-images.html","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"Second edition, 1855; Gutenberg release 2024-02-07","locator":"Release date at the top of the page; second edition and London 1855 on the title page","claim_support":["2.7.1: the 1855 second edition of the historical work and the 2024 digital release are different carriers of the same work."],"limits":["The digital transcription has not been collated word for word with the print edition; a carrier does not constitute an independent investigation."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"snow-trace","title":"John Snow 1855: Table I and its explanation","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/72894/pg72894-images.html","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"Second edition, 1855; digital transcription dated 2024-02-07","locator":"pp.49–51, dated rows, Date unknown row, and total in Table I; the description of daily decline after the table","claim_support":["2.7.2: 43 dated rows sum to 571; unknown dates account for 45; total 616 (the sum is derived in this lesson).","2.7.2: 20 on September 6 and 28 on September 7 limit a strictly day-by-day monotonic reading."],"limits":["The original table has two columns, onset date and death date for cases ultimately fatal; this lesson calculates only the former.","These are different locations in the same work as snow-use and snow-version, adding no independent studies; an anomaly does not reveal its cause."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v2.1","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2.1, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2.1 / 2026-11-19","locator":"2.7　来源的条件; Chinese source lines 357–364; SHA-256 2128af7cfa4c63b58207521a375aa34c2aea230d47e1c04a5afec3334e290d28","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2.1; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-2-7/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-2-7/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-2-7/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-2-7/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v2.1; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"05b7a36ae3c3059df57f09a30176e6d53141eb804a748f87f43d82997367f9d0","source_sha256":"2128af7cfa4c63b58207521a375aa34c2aea230d47e1c04a5afec3334e290d28"}},"2.7.1":{"title":"2.7.1 Relationships among sources and actual scope of use","lead":"Sources have genealogies, and reading has a scope. Transcription, republication, or revision of a file does not automatically add independent evidence by multiplying URLs.","explanation":["Source location: Chinese source lines 365–372; source anchor section-2-7-1; brief quotation: “Finding a link, completing a download, and understanding an argument are also different actions.” See the corresponding section on this page for the current source; the downloadable original research extract preserves the historical draft.","Record separately whether only the contents list, the abstract, relevant chapters, or the full text were read. Finding a link establishes discovery of an entry point; downloading establishes acquisition of a file; understanding a passage's argument still requires actually examining its premises and material. Reading only relevant passages can honestly support bounded facts within them; do not enlarge that reading scope into a claim of full-text review.","Preserve relationships among different analyses of the same data, preprints and final versions, and original reports and institutional retellings. Another algorithm may expose errors in the original analysis without automatically generating new observations; a final version may also change the sample, outcomes, or definitions. List changes before comparing: similar titles alone justify neither merging them nor treating them as replications.","The history of a theory also requires distinguishing actual adoption, mere mention, explicit limitation or rejection, compatibility with the current text, and a new connection proposed by today's analyst. Each historical claim needs its own record. “Compatible as seen today” does not establish that the author was influenced by it then; failure to find evidence of adoption is insufficient to say “the author never considered it.”","Absence from an archive follows multiple filters: whether an event generated a record, whether it was preserved or destroyed, whether it was opened to access, whether retrieval can find it, and whether the narrator ultimately selected it. Absence has corresponding counterevidential force only when events of that kind should have left discoverable traces. Failure to obtain a file within one research window does not mean other researchers or the entire project lack it.","Date errors in oral accounts should still be corrected, while the manner of narration may supply material about memory, experience, and meaning. One chronological error does not make the whole oral account worthless; the account's value does not justify refusing to correct factual misplacement either. Evaluate different uses separately."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"The full text exists","leftText":"An opportunity to obtain the complete text may exist.","rightTitle":"The full text was read in this inquiry","rightText":"The whole text was actually accessed and examined; existence or downloading cannot substitute for this."},{"leftTitle":"Multiple versions of a shared source","leftText":"May contain valuable revisions and reanalyses.","rightTitle":"Independent replication","rightText":"Requires an account of independent observations or design; the number of versions does not determine it."},{"leftTitle":"Not found in the current search","leftText":"The status of material within the current search window.","rightTitle":"Never existed historically","rightText":"A stronger claim over all relevant times and places, carrying far greater evidential responsibility."},{"leftTitle":"Factual date accuracy","leftText":"Used for ordering events and checking facts.","rightTitle":"Meaningfulness of expressed memory","rightText":"Used to understand narration and experience; both evaluations can proceed together."}],"boundaries":["The Snow case here relies only on the digital transcription of relevant passages in his 1855 original; the transcription is not treated as another historical investigation.","Without personally reading the original registers, do not claim to have checked them; the original work's statement that registers were consulted is an account of the author's investigative process.","A new interpretation can be valuable, but should be recorded as an analytical connection made today, not disguised as historical adoption."],"questions":["On finding both a final paper and its preprint, what should be compared first?","If only two relevant sections were read, may the source record say “full text read”?","How should the remaining content of an oral account be handled when one date is wrong?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-2-7/figures/section-2-7-1.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"The 1855 text and its digital edition: two carriers of one historical work","href":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/72894/pg72894-images.html"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"The 1855 text and its digital edition: two carriers of one historical work","text":"The Gutenberg page used in this lesson explicitly preserves the title and publication information of the 1855 second edition of Snow's work, while listing 2024 for its digital release. The digital text makes the original passages searchable; it does not mean a new Broad Street investigation was conducted in 2024.","sourceLabel":"Gutenberg digital transcription of Snow's work","sourceUrl":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/72894/pg72894-images.html","sourceLocator":"Release date at the top of the Gutenberg page and “SECOND EDITION, MUCH ENLARGED” and “LONDON…1855” on the title page; historical passages used here: pp.39–52","boundary":"Only bibliographic information and cited passages were checked; no word-for-word collation of the entire print edition is claimed. The digital transcription and the 1855 work do not count as two independent empirical sources.","href":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/72894/pg72894-images.html"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"2.7.1","start_line":379,"end_line":386,"startLine":379,"endLine":386}],"outputId":"section-2-7-1","sourceHash":"fcf100a5c8688d1e0337c52750ce3ad21fb5dbcc250f97946602ce094345f825","argumentSteps":["Premise: number of source files, number of underlying datasets, and scope of reading are three different objects of measurement.","Connection: source relationships must be established before distinguishing new material, reanalysis, transcription, version changes, and repeated retellings; reading status determines what can currently be personally verified.","Historical inference: upgrading “not found” to “never happened” requires additional support that traces should exist and could be retrieved; otherwise report only that material was not obtained within the current scope.","Conclusion: cite within the scope actually read and evaluate sources by information actually added; do not replace independence with counts of files, citations, or URLs.","Responsibility: remain accountable for correctable content such as dates and numbers, while preserving the distinctive contributions of different materials to different questions."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: which conclusions are affected by an incorrect date in an oral account?","text":"Suppose an interview dates an event to 2010, while a verifiable document dates it to 2012. Correct the event date and chronological inferences that depend on it. But if the question concerns how the interviewee connects the event to another life stage, the narrative itself can still serve as material on how memory is organized. This does not warrant inferring that the interviewee is lying, nor retaining the wrong year as a fact.","assumptions":"The interview and dates are teaching stipulations, referring to no real person or actual archive.","boundary":"Illustrates evaluation by use, without psychological diagnosis or endorsement of any real oral account."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Teaching boundary: a paper, a press release, and three news reports all recount the same data but are treated as five independent replications.","effect":"There is a communication chain, not five underlying datasets; preserve their separate uses and common source.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Teaching boundary: because a project's public repository lacks a contract, someone asserts that none of its participants ever held one.","effect":"The scope of a public-repository search cannot cover private holdings and other archives; the conclusion should stop at “not found in the current search.”","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"On finding both a final paper and its preprint, what should be compared first?","answer":"Check changes in version, sample, outcomes, analysis, and definitions, and identify shared underlying material; do not immediately count two replications.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"If only two relevant sections were read, may the source record say “full text read”?","answer":"No. State that the relevant chapters were read and limit the scope of support to what was actually checked.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"How should the remaining content of an oral account be handled when one date is wrong?","answer":"Correct the date and inspect inferences that depend on it; evaluate other content by its independent evidence and use, neither rejecting nor accepting everything wholesale.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-2-7/figures/section-2-7-1.png","alt":"The 1855 work: contemporary investigation and the author's interpretation; the 2024 digital transcription: a new carrier of the same work; this lesson's citations: only relevant passages actually checked; independent underlying material?: source relationships require separate support; full text/chapters/abstract: state the actual reading scope; not found now does not mean never existed historically. Version diagram based on the Gutenberg page header and the 1855 title page; two carriers are not two independent investigations.","caption":"Version diagram based on the Gutenberg page header and the 1855 title page; two carriers are not two independent investigations."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static figure; interaction has not been implemented. References below to selecting, switching, or clicking are ideas for later design only. Selecting a carrier could display its date, version relationship, and reading scope. Word-for-word collation with the print edition remains unchecked; having a digital file does not automatically complete it.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"The version relationship between an actual digital carrier and the historical work is verifiable, making it suitable for demonstrating source relationships and reading scope."},"openQuestions":[],"taskId":"S-2-7","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical research manuscript, Chinese full-text lines 341–362; see sourceRefs for each section's exact range","claim_support":["2.7: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","2.7.1: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","2.7.2: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts."],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2.1.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"snow-use","title":"John Snow 1855: events and causal limitations","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/72894/pg72894-images.html","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"Second edition, 1855; digital transcription dated 2024-02-07","locator":"p.40；pp.51—52","claim_support":["2.7: accounts of the meeting and handle-removal dates; the limitation that the decline had begun before closure."],"limits":["Date facts do not directly support a net effect."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"snow-version","title":"Bibliographic information for John Snow's work and its Gutenberg digital edition","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/72894/pg72894-images.html","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"Second edition, 1855; Gutenberg release 2024-02-07","locator":"Release date at the top of the page; second edition and London 1855 on the title page","claim_support":["2.7.1: the 1855 second edition of the historical work and the 2024 digital release are different carriers of the same work."],"limits":["The digital transcription has not been collated word for word with the print edition; a carrier does not constitute an independent investigation."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"snow-trace","title":"John Snow 1855: Table I and its explanation","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/72894/pg72894-images.html","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"Second edition, 1855; digital transcription dated 2024-02-07","locator":"pp.49–51, dated rows, Date unknown row, and total in Table I; the description of daily decline after the table","claim_support":["2.7.2: 43 dated rows sum to 571; unknown dates account for 45; total 616 (the sum is derived in this lesson).","2.7.2: 20 on September 6 and 28 on September 7 limit a strictly day-by-day monotonic reading."],"limits":["The original table has two columns, onset date and death date for cases ultimately fatal; this lesson calculates only the former.","These are different locations in the same work as snow-use and snow-version, adding no independent studies; an anomaly does not reveal its cause."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v2.1","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2.1, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2.1 / 2026-11-19","locator":"2.7.1 来源的关系与实际使用范围; Chinese source lines 365–372; SHA-256 2128af7cfa4c63b58207521a375aa34c2aea230d47e1c04a5afec3334e290d28","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2.1; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-2-7/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-2-7/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-2-7/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-2-7/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v2.1; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"98e2d1ac1af17553667fbce66152990a2e45a44894b03064a8033ae09aae8fd8","source_sha256":"2128af7cfa4c63b58207521a375aa34c2aea230d47e1c04a5afec3334e290d28"}},"2.7.2":{"title":"2.7.2 Evidence independence and propagation of defects","lead":"A different analysis of the same material may indeed add knowledge, but does not add an independent sample. Finding a discrepancy should initially suspend only conclusions that actually depend on it.","explanation":["Source location: Chinese source lines 373–378; source anchor section-2-7-2; brief quotation: “Independence must be judged relative to the claim being supported.” See the corresponding section on this page for the current source; the downloadable original research extract preserves the historical draft.","Independence is not an unconditional yes/no label. Long-term follow-up of the same randomized allocation adds information on time and outcomes, but not another randomization; rechecking the same material with another algorithm adds implementation and interpretation checks, but not a new original cohort. Conversely, two studies with the same topic name but different underlying samples must not be forcibly merged based solely on their titles.","Source relationships should preserve shared data, common measurements, version inheritance, and new observations. Specify which stage is independent: data may be shared while analytical implementations are independent; measurement methods may be shared while samples of objects are independent. Evaluating a particular claim requires identifying its dependence on these stages, not converting file counts into evidence scores.","On finding a numerical discrepancy, first determine whether it is a conflict about the same object, a legitimate difference between different objects, or an apparent difference in display precision. Cases versus person-occurrences, cumulative versus new counts, onset date versus death date, and the whole population versus a threshold-defined subgroup need not be equal. If a genuine conflict remains unexplained, suspend precise conclusions depending on that number and trace the chain “source value → transcription → model input → derived quantity → main text.”","Agreement of sums is a check, but cannot by itself certify every component. One component increasing while another decreases can leave the total unchanged; conversely, an anomaly does not immediately identify transcription error, fabrication, or actual change as its cause. First report the locatable discrepancy and affected scope; do not fill in causes on behalf of the evidence.","Other judgments with independent grounds can continue to be used within their scope. Only failure of a common source, key premise, or object boundary blocks all related outputs. Proposition-by-proposition handling allows correction without concealing a problem or inflating a local issue into an unsupported wholesale rejection."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Independent data","leftText":"New underlying observations or cohorts.","rightTitle":"Independent reanalysis","rightText":"A different implementation on the same underlying sample; it may reveal errors but does not count another group of people."},{"leftTitle":"Genuine conflict","leftText":"The claims still cannot coexist after aligning object, unit, and time.","rightTitle":"Legitimate difference","rightText":"The objects, denominators, or indicators being compared were different to begin with."},{"leftTitle":"Agreement of the total","leftText":"The sum of components matches the total.","rightTitle":"Correct components","rightText":"Each component has correct provenance, classification, and definitions; further checking is required."},{"leftTitle":"Local anomaly","leftText":"Affects particular values and derived conclusions.","rightTitle":"Failure of a shared premise","rightText":"May block every output depending on it, with a broader scope."}],"boundaries":["Snow's Table I gives 20 onsets of ultimately fatal cases on September 6 and 28 on September 7. Thus, the text's “decreased every day” cannot mechanically be read as a strictly monotonic sequence. This local counterexample does not erase the overall decline, nor determine the cause of a difference involving transcription or the original wording.","This lesson checks only the aggregate and these two daily values in the current digital transcription; it does not claim word-for-word collation of the original death registers. The 43 dated rows are not 43 independent studies.","571+45=616 does not establish that the 45 unknown dates can be allocated arbitrarily, or that the complete number of infections is known."],"questions":["Can adding three years of follow-up to the same randomized trial be described as conducting a new randomized trial?","If a table's total equals the sum of its components, does that establish that every component is correct?","If a figure for one day is disputed, why should every judgment in the same book not immediately be withdrawn?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-2-7/figures/section-2-7-2.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"Snow's Table I: check both the sum and the strength of the wording","href":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/72894/pg72894-images.html"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"Snow's Table I: check both the sum and the strength of the wording","text":"Table I in the current transcription lists 45 cases with unknown onset dates; the other 43 dated rows sum to 571, for a total of 616. A further local check shows 20 on September 6 and 28 on September 7, so “decreased every day” cannot be used as a strictly monotonic numerical proposition. This section does not speculate about the cause of the discrepancy, but only limits the corresponding wording.","sourceLabel":"Snow, second edition, 1855: Table I and the explanation immediately following it","sourceUrl":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/72894/pg72894-images.html","sourceLocator":"Printed p.49, Table I (1854-08-19 to 09-30 and Date unknown); pp.50–51, explanation after the table","boundary":"The counts concern onset times of ultimately fatal cases, some inferred backward from illness duration. Agreement of the total does not certify daily values; non-monotonicity does not refute an overall decline, nor identify the effect of removing the handle.","href":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/72894/pg72894-images.html"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"2.7.2","start_line":387,"end_line":392,"startLine":387,"endLine":392}],"outputId":"section-2-7-2","sourceHash":"fcf100a5c8688d1e0337c52750ce3ad21fb5dbcc250f97946602ce094345f825","argumentSteps":["Premise one: new information can come from new samples, times, measurements, or analyses; these additions are not the same kind of independence.","Premise two: derived conclusions depend only on their actual inputs and premises, so error propagation has specific paths.","Connection: first align numbers by object, definition, unit, window, and precision, then determine whether there is a genuine conflict or a legitimate difference; for a genuine conflict, suspend conclusions along the dependency path.","Conclusion: state both independence and downgrading claim by claim; unaffected independent grounds are not invalidated together with a local anomaly.","Actual calculation: the 43 dated rows for known onset dates in Snow's Table I sum to 571; adding 45 with unknown onset dates gives the table's total of 616. This agreement checks only aggregation, not the correctness of the allocation across dates."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Mathematical teaching construction: the same total with both components wrong","text":"Suppose the true components are 40 and 60, totaling 100. If transcribed as 45 and 55, the total is still 100. Passing the total check does not guarantee correct components. Text using only an independently checked total may be unaffected by this allocation error; text using the two groups' proportions or difference requires suspending the relevant inference.","assumptions":"These numbers are purely a teaching construction, not Snow's data or any actual dataset.","boundary":"Demonstrates error propagation without accusing any real author of fabrication or identifying a particular transcription error."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"real_counterevidence","text":"The values 20 on September 6 and 28 on September 7 in Snow's Table I are incompatible with a strictly monotonic reading of the subsequent “decreased every day.”","effect":"Suspend the strict day-by-day monotonicity claim; a supported description of overall decline may remain, but cannot yield the net effect of handle removal.","sourceIds":["snow-trace"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Teaching construction: 40+60 and 45+55 both equal 100.","effect":"Agreement of the total cannot independently certify every component; trace the particular derived quantities.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Can adding three years of follow-up to the same randomized trial be described as conducting a new randomized trial?","answer":"No. Additional information about time and outcomes is worth retaining, but neither allocation nor the original cohort has thereby been replicated.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"If a table's total equals the sum of its components, does that establish that every component is correct?","answer":"No. Component errors can cancel each other; source values, units, classifications, and transcription still require checking.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"If a figure for one day is disputed, why should every judgment in the same book not immediately be withdrawn?","answer":"First determine which conclusions actually depend on the number; other judgments with independent material may remain. Expand the suspension only if a common source or boundary has failed.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-2-7/figures/section-2-7-2.png","alt":"Daily values in the source table: September 6, 20 / September 7, 28; local derived judgment: not a strict daily decline; limit the stronger wording while preserving the boundary of the overall trend; 43 known-date rows total 571; unknown dates, 45; 571+45=616; the total check passes but does not certify every component. Actual table values and this lesson's exact sum: Snow 1855, Table I, pp.49–51. The two checking routes answer different questions and do not infer the anomaly's cause.","caption":"Actual table values and this lesson's exact sum: Snow 1855, Table I, pp.49–51. The two checking routes answer different questions and do not infer the anomaly's cause."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static figure; interaction has not been implemented. References below to selecting, switching, or clicking are ideas for later design only. Selecting “total conclusion/daily proportions/strict monotonicity” could highlight their respective dependencies; simulated changes must be labeled teaching sensitivity analysis, without rewriting the source table or erasing old records.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"Aggregates and local changes within the same source can be checked exactly, directly demonstrating discrepancy classification and the narrowing of conclusions without fabricating causes for anomalies."},"openQuestions":[],"taskId":"S-2-7","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical research manuscript, Chinese full-text lines 341–362; see sourceRefs for each section's exact range","claim_support":["2.7: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","2.7.1: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","2.7.2: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts."],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2.1.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"snow-use","title":"John Snow 1855: events and causal limitations","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/72894/pg72894-images.html","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"Second edition, 1855; digital transcription dated 2024-02-07","locator":"p.40；pp.51—52","claim_support":["2.7: accounts of the meeting and handle-removal dates; the limitation that the decline had begun before closure."],"limits":["Date facts do not directly support a net effect."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"snow-version","title":"Bibliographic information for John Snow's work and its Gutenberg digital edition","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/72894/pg72894-images.html","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"Second edition, 1855; Gutenberg release 2024-02-07","locator":"Release date at the top of the page; second edition and London 1855 on the title page","claim_support":["2.7.1: the 1855 second edition of the historical work and the 2024 digital release are different carriers of the same work."],"limits":["The digital transcription has not been collated word for word with the print edition; a carrier does not constitute an independent investigation."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"snow-trace","title":"John Snow 1855: Table I and its explanation","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/72894/pg72894-images.html","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"Second edition, 1855; digital transcription dated 2024-02-07","locator":"pp.49–51, dated rows, Date unknown row, and total in Table I; the description of daily decline after the table","claim_support":["2.7.2: 43 dated rows sum to 571; unknown dates account for 45; total 616 (the sum is derived in this lesson).","2.7.2: 20 on September 6 and 28 on September 7 limit a strictly day-by-day monotonic reading."],"limits":["The original table has two columns, onset date and death date for cases ultimately fatal; this lesson calculates only the former.","These are different locations in the same work as snow-use and snow-version, adding no independent studies; an anomaly does not reveal its cause."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v2.1","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2.1, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2.1 / 2026-11-19","locator":"2.7.2　证据独立性与缺陷的传播; Chinese source lines 373–378; SHA-256 2128af7cfa4c63b58207521a375aa34c2aea230d47e1c04a5afec3334e290d28","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2.1; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-2-7/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-2-7/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-2-7/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-2-7/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v2.1; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"c702791cfd699a1e925bf5d86a25a9e9e5a1c686f0de9a5a6728025aafff1540","source_sha256":"2128af7cfa4c63b58207521a375aa34c2aea230d47e1c04a5afec3334e290d28"}},"2.8":{"title":"2.8 Core terminology lock","lead":"‘Cost’, ‘repair’, ‘choice’, and ‘responsibility’ are everyday words, but each carries different conditions for judgment. Fixing their meanings prevents a sentence from silently changing meaning during an inference.","explanation":["Chinese source location: lines 379–396; source anchor section-2-8; short quotation: ‘Their broad everyday meanings must not be substituted for those conditions’. The current source appears in the corresponding subsection on this page; the downloadable original research excerpt preserves the historical manuscript.","Cost is first a quantity of some type: its type, unit, time window, accounting boundary, and cost-bearing position must be stated. Cash, working hours, risk, and attention cannot simply be added; conversion requires an explicit valuation, measurement, or accounting mapping. Lower spending in one place does not mean a cost of the same type necessarily shifts elsewhere, much less establish a universal conservation of cost across domains. Occupation of resources is an observation; comparability with other quantities is a separate task.","Externality here involves two steps: first establish a cross-boundary causal channel and the positions across which effects are distributed, then specify which impact an economic model or actual institutional or accounting rule excludes from decision accounting. With only the first step, call it a cross-boundary spillover or impact; terminology must not assign responsibility in advance. The second step does not require prior determination of legal liability; inability to determine who is legally liable does not prevent recording supported cross-boundary harm.","Cross-boundary analysis also requires the right rule. The conclusion rule “Local Improvement and Cross-Boundary Spillover” (§4.12.4) addresses spillovers and distributional positions under its specified conditions, not every economic externality or transmission in general. Check scale transformation (§3.4) and “Testing Cross-Scale Transfer” (§4.8.5) separately only where scale actually changes; carry out the corresponding scale-closure test (§4.6) only when claiming formal support for closure failure or object or intervention conversion. The word ‘external’ alone must not trigger every scale procedure.","Repair requires disclosure, before outcomes, of the target identity criterion or target functional criterion: which object identity or function is to be recovered. Repair may allow a new implementation rather than reproduce the historical form; recovery of a specified function also does not equal an improvement in value. Whether the target is justified, who decides it, and who may implement it remain subject to review under normative premises and operational procedures. The target must not be changed after seeing results to declare any change a successful repair.","Choice has three meanings. System selection is a process of variation, differential retention, and reproduction; it need not involve intention. Choice by an acting subject involves a subject deciding under conditions and must not be equated with environmental selection. Governance choice additionally requires comparisons of options, normative premises, and authorization; observing that an outcome was retained does not mean someone justifiably chose it.","Responsibility and authorization separately state the uptake carrier, bearer of costs of the same type, beneficiaries, right to stop, and responsible party. Being the actual implementer, the most capable, the most affected, the most visible, or the best informed does not by itself make someone infinitely responsible. Expanded observation or confidence in explanation does not expand the scope of authorization either.","Reasons for obligations may include commitments, roles, causal contributions, benefits, capabilities, and applicable norms; disclose how they jointly support specific obligations and their boundaries. Bounded obligations do not turn anyone into a unilateral, unlimited uptake party with no exit; arrangements for stopping, handoff, duration, and remedy are themselves part of responsibility judgments."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Reduced spending","leftText":"A change in an amount of money within a specified boundary, unit, and window.","rightTitle":"Lower unified cost","rightText":"A stronger judgment across different cost bearers and quantity types, requiring grounds for conversion and allocation."},{"leftTitle":"Cross-boundary impact","leftText":"Has causation and distributional positions.","rightTitle":"Externality","rightText":"Additionally specifies the rule excluding the impact from decision accounting; this is not prior determination of legal liability."},{"leftTitle":"Repair","leftText":"Meets the target identity criterion or target functional criterion disclosed in advance.","rightTitle":"Improvement in value","rightText":"A justified target and acceptable consequences, requiring additional normative assessment."},{"leftTitle":"System selection","leftText":"A process of differential retention.","rightTitle":"Subject/governance choice","rightText":"The former requires a subject's decision; the latter additionally requires norms and permissions."},{"leftTitle":"Uptake party or beneficiary","leftText":"Records who actually bears or receives what.","rightTitle":"Responsible party","rightText":"Bounded obligations supported by multiple reasons and norms; not automatically the same party."}],"boundaries":["This section specifies specialized meanings and conditions of use; definitions do not establish a universal conservation of cost or a shared purpose in the world.","Failure to determine legal liability does not prevent recording harm; recording harm also does not establish burden shares or authority to act.","Observational capability and authorization are different scale axes. Even complete knowledge of the situation still requires checking permissions for the particular object, action, territory, validity period, and withdrawal conditions.","Repair targets must not be arbitrarily rewritten after results; necessary changes require a new version that preserves outcomes under the original target."],"questions":["If pollution is recorded as harming others across a boundary, must legal liability first be determined before its impacts can be reported?","Why does a function meeting its pre-specified target still not justify declaring ‘this is a good option and may be implemented immediately’?","If A benefits most and B performs most of the implementation, may that alone identify one sole responsible party?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-2-8/figures/section-2-8.png","caseLinks":[],"caseNotes":[],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"2.8","start_line":393,"end_line":410,"startLine":393,"endLine":410}],"outputId":"section-2-8","sourceHash":"fcf100a5c8688d1e0337c52750ce3ad21fb5dbcc250f97946602ce094345f825","argumentSteps":["Premise: an everyday word often simultaneously carries descriptive, evaluative, and action-related meanings; mixing them allows a later step to borrow grounds the earlier step never supplied.","First connection: costs first require the same type and unit or a valid conversion; externalities first require causation and exclusion rules; repair first requires the target identity criterion or target functional criterion; choice first requires specifying the system, subject, or governance meaning.","Second connection: where responsibility is involved, separately check roles, causation, commitments, benefits, capabilities, and norms instead of deriving total responsibility directly from one visible identity.","Conclusion: locking meanings makes each step's missing conditions visible; ‘successful repair’ must not bypass value review, and ‘I understand’ must not bypass authorization.","Evidential and normative responsibility: cross-boundary effects and cost-bearing positions require materials; exclusion rules require a basis in models or institutions; obligations and action ceilings require norms and valid authorization."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: budget savings and time burdens cannot simply be conserved","text":"Suppose an option reduces an institution's monthly spending by 100 yuan but makes a user wait an additional 3 hours. First record yuan/month and hours/person-visit separately, along with their bearers; do not write ‘total cost fell by 97’ or ‘100 yuan fully converted into 3 hours’. Only with an explicit valuation of v yuan per hour may 100−3v be written for a specified number of people and window. This v includes choices of value and measurement and must not be disguised as a universal constant of the world.","assumptions":"100 yuan and 3 hours are teaching assumptions; an illustrative conversion is possible only after explicitly specifying conditions such as a single user and the same window.","boundary":"Does not estimate the net welfare of an actual option or allocate legal liability from these numbers."},{"title":"Teaching construction: functional repair does not mean everything returns to its old form","text":"Suppose the target functional criterion F* disclosed in advance is ‘reading error no greater than e under a given load’. If valid measurement after component replacement shows this criterion is met, this may count as functional recovery within that arrangement; there is no need to claim that every original component has been restored. If the new component introduces additional safety risks or infringes others' rights, meeting the function criterion does not by itself justify the option.","assumptions":"The equipment, load, error bound, and outcome are all conditional teaching assumptions, not a measurement report.","boundary":"Qualification as repair is limited to the specified function; justification, actual evidence, and implementation permissions are checked separately."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Teaching boundary: the person who understands the system best is required to bear all maintenance costs permanently solely because ‘only you are capable’.","effect":"Capability may be one reason for responsibility, but does not by itself imply an unlimited obligation with no exit.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Teaching boundary: an organizational form survives competition, which is described as ‘the system intentionally selected the most justified institution’.","effect":"Differential retention has been replaced by a subject's intention and normative superiority; both additional claims lack grounds.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"If pollution is recorded as harming others across a boundary, must legal liability first be determined before its impacts can be reported?","answer":"No. Recording causal harm, identifying accounting-exclusion rules, and determining legal liability are different tasks; supported impacts can be retained first.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Why does a function meeting its pre-specified target still not justify declaring ‘this is a good option and may be implemented immediately’?","answer":"Functional repair does not settle values, risks, permissions, action ceilings, or stopping conditions; normative premises and operational procedures require separate checking.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"If A benefits most and B performs most of the implementation, may that alone identify one sole responsible party?","answer":"No. Disclose the relations among commitments, roles, causal contributions, benefits, capabilities, and applicable norms, and bound the obligations.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-2-8/figures/section-2-8.png","alt":"Cost: type/unit/window/bearing; externality: cross-boundary causation plus accounting exclusion; repair: target identity criterion or target functional criterion disclosed before outcomes; choice: separate selection/subject/governance; responsibility: multiple reasons plus bounded obligations; authorization: object/action/validity period/withdrawal. A diagram of terminological conditions based on 2.8; boxes do not substitute for one another and imply neither conservation of cost, a hierarchy of purposes, nor automatic transfer of responsibility.","caption":"A diagram of terminological conditions based on 2.8; boxes do not substitute for one another and imply neither conservation of cost, a hierarchy of purposes, nor automatic transfer of responsibility."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static diagram; interaction has not been implemented, and the following references to selecting, switching, or clicking describe future design ideas only. Clicking a term could show the conclusion ceiling when an item is missing. Changing an accounting boundary, repair target, or authorized action requires creating or revising the corresponding conditions, without automatically expanding the other meanings.","applicability":{"realCase":"not_applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"This section fixes meanings and conditions of use; teaching counterexamples directly expose substitutions of units, targets, intentions, and responsibilities. Historical facts are not forced into the definitions."},"openQuestions":[],"taskId":"S-2-8","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Lines 363–380 of the complete historical Chinese research manuscript; see sourceRefs for each section's precise range","claim_support":["2.8: Terms, contracts, burdens of argument, and source boundaries; not a basis for certifying empirical facts"],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2.1.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"theory-v2.1","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2.1, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2.1 / 2026-11-19","locator":"2.8　核心词语锁; Chinese source lines 379–396; SHA-256 2128af7cfa4c63b58207521a375aa34c2aea230d47e1c04a5afec3334e290d28","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2.1; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-2-8/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-2-8/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-2-8/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-2-8/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v2.1; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"7191f83153ec2bfb89605101769d2100037fff19ea665a71a509cc061b4c114c","source_sha256":"2128af7cfa4c63b58207521a375aa34c2aea230d47e1c04a5afec3334e290d28"}},"2.9":{"title":"2.9 Missing-Data and Visibility States, Downgrading, Suspension, and Exit","lead":"A blank should not simply be filled with ‘not available’. First distinguish whether something is not yet known, conceptually inapplicable, currently unobservable, or available but protected; then determine exactly which conclusion and action must be restricted.","explanation":["Chinese source location: lines 397–410; source anchor section-2-9; short quotation: ‘Downgrading proceeds proposition by proposition, stating what each proposition depends on and how what is missing affects it’. The current source appears in the corresponding subsection on this page; the downloadable original research excerpt preserves the historical manuscript.","Unknown: knowable in principle, but current evidence is insufficient. State which materials are missing and what could change that status. Not applicable: the item has no valid counterpart in the current object's definition, with reasons given; it does not mean ‘hard to fill, so omit it’. For example, where a purely natural process has no acting subject or authorization concept, the axis of jurisdiction and authorization may be inapplicable, but a researcher's intervention in that process may still require authorization. Objects and actions must not be conflated.","Unobservable: materials cannot currently be obtained because of measurement capabilities, time windows, or channels. This means neither nonexistence nor permanent unknowability; changing the window or measurement may change visibility, but missing facts must not be falsely presented as already obtained. Withheld for protection: materials do exist, but disclosure would violate privacy, safety, or explicit protections; record the grounds for protection, minimal scope, duration, and a credible review route.","The four statuses jointly describe knowledge and visibility; they must not silently become truth labels. An unknown must not be filled with 0, an inapplicable value must not be added to zero, unobservable must not be recorded as absent, and withheld for protection must not be described as the researcher lacking materials. State the appropriate status under the definition and, where needed, additionally record whether credible review is available.","Where key variables are missing, applicability is untreated, core channels are unobservable, or protected materials cannot undergo credible review, downgrade the affected claims to candidates, a list of questions, or description. This does not mechanically reduce every conclusion to one level; assess separately whether each missing condition is necessary. A factual count may be supported while a mechanistic explanation remains unresolved; both can coexist.","Action boundaries are assessed separately: unclear authorization, risks of irreversible harm, unavailable appeal or stopping mechanisms, or limits of professional capability should suspend real-world dispositions and lead into appropriate procedures. Safe clarification of materials and discussion of options may continue; this does not permit implementation under the label of ‘probing’. Exiting or withdrawing a claim also does not require erasing genuine records of failure.","Failure of a local premise affects only outputs that depend on it; failure of a global source, common premise, or object boundary blocks all related outputs. The difference concerns actual dependency scope, not willingness to acknowledge a problem. Preserving unknowns and downgrading makes evidential ceilings and boundaries of power visible, itself a responsible conclusion."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Unknown","leftText":"An answer may be obtainable, but current grounds are insufficient.","rightTitle":"Not applicable","rightText":"The item has no valid meaning for this object; reasons are required."},{"leftTitle":"Unobservable","leftText":"The current window or channel does not permit obtaining an observation.","rightTitle":"Nonexistent","rightText":"A negative fact about the world, which does not automatically follow from the preceding status."},{"leftTitle":"Protective nondisclosure","leftText":"Materials exist; disclosure is restricted and credible review arranged.","rightTitle":"No one can review it","rightText":"Further restricts the conclusion; protection does not exempt strong assertions from review."},{"leftTitle":"Downgrading a conclusion","leftText":"Reduces the strength that the evidence can support.","rightTitle":"Suspending real-world disposition","rightText":"Stops action because of permissions, harm, appeals, or capability; these reasons may be independent."}],"boundaries":["The four missing-data states are not four truth values of the world; “unknown,” “not applicable,” and invisibility must not all be encoded as numerical 0 and averaged.","Non-disclosure for protection must not justify coercing disclosure, nor become a permanent pass for strong claims that cannot be reviewed.","Local failure does not invariably contaminate the whole; but if object identity or a common source is broadly unreliable, suspension must extend to all related judgments."],"questions":["If material exists but is withheld for privacy, should it be marked ‘unknown’ for convenience?","If a mechanism's key input is missing but independent descriptive data are credible, must every conclusion be withdrawn?","If the model is highly confident but authorization is unclear, may the action be tried once first?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-2-9/figures/section-2-9.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"Local exclusion when ancient-DNA sample identity is unresolved","href":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9581778/"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"Local exclusion when ancient-DNA sample identity is unresolved","text":"Skov et al.'s 2022 paper states that they could not be confident Chagyrskaya E was an individual distinct from D, so E was removed from subsequent analyses. This lesson uses it as an example of restricting use according to a specific identity gap, without retrospectively assigning the authors Xikari status codes.","sourceLabel":"Skov et al. 2022, explanation of the treatment of sample E","sourceUrl":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9581778/","sourceLocator":"Genetic relationships: the paragraph on contamination of Chagyrskaya E, correction, and ‘cannot be confident…distinct individual’","boundary":"Supports only the handling of this sample and its stated reasons; the genetic data were not recalculated for this lesson, and it does not establish that every other conclusion is error-free.","href":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9581778/"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"2.9","start_line":411,"end_line":424,"startLine":411,"endLine":424}],"outputId":"section-2-9","sourceHash":"fcf100a5c8688d1e0337c52750ce3ad21fb5dbcc250f97946602ce094345f825","argumentSteps":["Premise: apparently identical blanks may have different causes; filling all with zero or no changes either the object facts or the meaning of the item.","Classification: give each item an explicit status and reason; distinguish materials already available, materials missing, and materials that should not be disclosed.","Dependency check: list the necessary inputs for each claim; restrict a claim when a necessary input is missing while retaining other independently grounded claims.","Action check: incomplete evidence and incomplete authorization can each block disposition; severe irreversible risks, capability limits, and unavailable stopping mechanisms cannot be overridden by model confidence.","Conclusion: downgrade, suspend, or exit according to the scope of the gap, preserving materials and previous records needed for reassessment; do not treat inability to observe as nonexistence."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: four blanks, four different answers","text":"Suppose an analysis table contains: a daily total not yet obtained, marked unknown; a subject-authorization item for a purely natural process, marked not applicable after explaining the absence of an acting subject; an instantaneous peak beyond the instrument's range, marked unobservable; and an existing individual record involving privacy, marked withheld for protection after specifying protection grounds, scope, duration, and reviewers. The four cells must not all be filled with 0.","assumptions":"These are four teaching scenarios, containing no real personal data, experiments, or field measurements.","boundary":"Specific lawfulness and protective procedures require checking in the actual setting; the example authorizes neither collection, disclosure, nor disposition."},{"title":"Teaching construction: one missing link does not zero out the entire page","text":"Suppose independent counting records support ‘there are 20 requests in the queue’, while the explanation ‘congestion is caused by failure of interface A’ depends on interface logs not yet obtained. Retain the limited description of 20 requests and keep the mechanism as a candidate; if the count is later found to duplicate the same request, both the count and derived rates must be rechecked.","assumptions":"20 and the interface scenario are teaching assumptions; independence is an explicit premise, not an established fact.","boundary":"Does not claim that an actual system has 20 requests or a failed interface; it illustrates downgrading claim by claim only."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"real_counterevidence","text":"Skov et al. could not confirm that Chagyrskaya E was an individual distinct from D, so they removed sample E from subsequent analyses.","effect":"Unclear identity affects eligibility to use that sample, rather than automatically invalidating every other supported kinship analysis.","sourceIds":["skov-local"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Teaching boundary: a researcher records ‘not permitted to see the materials’ as ‘the materials do not exist’ and uses this to exclude an explanation.","effect":"A visibility restriction has been presented as a negative fact; the corresponding exclusion judgment should be withdrawn to unresolved status.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"If material exists but is withheld for privacy, should it be marked ‘unknown’ for convenience?","answer":"Do not substitute one status for another. Record non-disclosure for protection, its grounds, minimal scope, duration, and credible review route; separately state how much the public or current analyst can support.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"If a mechanism's key input is missing but independent descriptive data are credible, must every conclusion be withdrawn?","answer":"Not necessarily. Downgrade the mechanism judgment that depends on the missing input while retaining the independent description; expand the scope if a common source or object boundary fails.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"If the model is highly confident but authorization is unclear, may the action be tried once first?","answer":"Confidence cannot confer authority. The relevant action should be suspended while authority and procedures for stopping, appeal, and risk are supplied; proposing a reversible trial does not authorize implementation.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-2-9/figures/section-2-9.png","alt":"Unknown: knowable in principle, currently insufficient; not applicable: no valid correspondence, with reasons stated; unobservable: window/measurement/channel restricted; protectively undisclosed: basis/scope/duration/review; check dependencies claim by claim, downgrading or retaining independent judgments; check action boundaries separately, suspending/exiting/completing procedures. State-and-decision diagram based on 2.9; the four states cannot be merged into zero values, and downgrading and suspension have different grounds.","caption":"State-and-decision diagram based on 2.9; the four states cannot be merged into zero values, and downgrading and suspension have different grounds."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static diagram; interaction has not been implemented, and the selections, switches, or clicks described below are ideas for a later design only. Selecting an item's state would highlight only judgments dependent on that item; switching to failure of a shared source would expand the highlight to all relevant outputs. Unknowns must not automatically be filled with model guesses.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"An actual study discloses identity uncertainty and a local exclusion, showing how a gap corresponds to a scope of use; this is the same case as the preceding question, with an additional angle on evidence handling."},"openQuestions":[],"taskId":"S-2-9","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical research draft, full text, lines 381–394; see sourceRefs for each section’s exact range","claim_support":["2.9: terminology, contracts, burdens of argument, and boundaries of the original text; no empirical facts are certified on this basis"],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2.1.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"skov-local","title":"Skov et al., Genetic insights into the social organization of Neanderthals","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9581778/","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"2022-10-19，Nature610:519—525","locator":"Genetic relationships, Chagyrskaya E paragraph and Table 1 note","claim_support":["2.9: the authors could not confirm that E was a distinct individual and excluded it from subsequent analyses."],"limits":["A description of local handling within the same paper; the data were not recalculated, and no certification of all other conclusions is made.","The actual study is not retrospectively classified as a formal Xikari state instance."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v2.1","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2.1, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2.1 / 2026-11-19","locator":"2.9　缺失与可见性状态、降级、暂停与退出; Chinese source lines 397–410; SHA-256 2128af7cfa4c63b58207521a375aa34c2aea230d47e1c04a5afec3334e290d28","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2.1; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-2-9/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-2-9/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-2-9/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-2-9/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v2.1; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"a0a10636a737716fa5b5a758ae79079248223c6b4311dcd64d0df4fdb5f6abb2","source_sha256":"2128af7cfa4c63b58207521a375aa34c2aea230d47e1c04a5afec3334e290d28"}},"2.10":{"title":"2.10 Methodological Boundaries for Objects with Multiple Circles","lead":"Drawing more circles does not automatically improve an explanation. Yet a circle’s failure to help prediction does not mean that its membership facts, responsibilities, or authority do not exist. State the purpose first.","explanation":["Source location: Chinese source lines 411–416; source anchor section-2-10; short quotation: ‘Retaining an object to describe supported responsibilities, differences in membership, or authority over matters does not first require proving that it increases predictive accuracy.’ See the corresponding section on this page for the current original text; the downloadable original research excerpt retains the historical draft.","Families, organizations, platform groups, professional communities, and cultural categories must first be declared as explicit objects: boundaries, members, roles, channels, time window, and identity criterion. A name or clustering result does not itself create a natural entity; documents, relationships, and exposure records can nevertheless each support the relevant object facts, which cannot all be denied because support from a grouping-gain test is not yet available.","A claim that a grouping adds explanatory or anticipatory value requires comparison against appropriately simple baselines, such as no grouping or a single circle; formal qualification as a grouping-gain test (§4.3) must be established separately under its pre-registration. The question here is ‘Does this grouping help with this task?’, which differs from ‘Does this family or institution have documents and membership relationships?’ One may be supported while the other remains unsupported.","Parallel, nested, and overlapping relations require a declared dimension of comparison. Overlapping member sets do not imply overlapping authority; spatial containment does not imply organizational subordination; two circles parallel in one dimension may share members in another. Competition describes constraints on feasible sets, meaning restrictions on available actions or resource conditions within a window; temporary describes duration or persistence. Both can coexist with nesting, overlap, and other relations, rather than being mutually exclusive labels.","Potential bridging, actual transmission, and transmission effects each require separate evidence. A person belonging to two groups may constitute a potential bridge; claiming that a message, resource, or exposure actually crossed requires an actual event; claiming that this transmission caused an outcome additionally requires causal comparison and mechanism material. An edge or a shared member does not prove propagation, much less establish that the transmitter necessarily bears all responsibility.","Before adding a circle, explain which necessary question it answers, what distinction would be lost if it were removed, and the added measurement and privacy costs. If it merely circles every background factor without an identifiable use, retain it provisionally as an environmental condition or variable candidate. When a complex model lacks stable comparative support, reduce the corresponding gain claim and prefer a sufficiently adequate simple model.","A circle may also be retained for supported responsibilities, member differences, or authority over particular matters; predictive accuracy is not an entry requirement for these uses. Simplifying a predictive model should not incidentally remove people’s rights or real organizational boundaries. Description, prediction, and normative governance must each meet their own evidentiary and authorization requirements."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Object facts","leftText":"What membership, relationships, documents, or exposure records support.","rightTitle":"Grouping gain","rightText":"What a grouping explains or predicts beyond the baseline, requiring a separate comparison."},{"leftTitle":"Shared member / potential bridge","leftText":"A particular possibility of connection exists.","rightTitle":"Actual transmission and its effect","rightText":"Whether a specific event occurred and whether it affected the outcome require separate evidence."},{"leftTitle":"Membership overlap","leftText":"The sets have shared elements.","rightTitle":"Overlapping authority","rightText":"Authorization for particular matters and actions intersects; this cannot be inferred from member sets."},{"leftTitle":"A sufficiently adequate simple model","leftText":"Less complexity for the current task.","rightTitle":"Removing objects of rights and responsibilities","rightText":"A separate normative action that does not automatically follow from a lack of predictive gain."}],"boundaries":["A lack of evidence of grouping gain does not revoke an object’s existence supported by documents or relationships; conversely, an institution’s real name does not prove its usefulness for a particular prediction.","The historical water-exposure case shows only that residential location and exposure channels may not coincide; it does not establish that every family or cross-circle relationship can transmit something.","When privacy costs make an additional circle unjustified, retain necessary unknowns and scope limitations rather than covertly collecting personal relationships to fill a table."],"questions":["If adding a circle does not improve prediction, can its membership records be rejected on that basis?","Does a person’s simultaneous membership in two groups suffice to prove that a message has spread?","If two circles’ memberships overlap, can they directly be labeled as having ‘overlapping jurisdiction’?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-2-10/figures/section-2-10.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"The Hampstead drinking-water record: spatial circles and exposure circles do not coincide","href":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/72894/pg72894-images.html"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"The Hampstead drinking-water record: spatial circles and exposure circles do not coincide","text":"Page 44 of Snow’s original work records a woman living in Hampstead who had not visited Broad Street for a long time but still received water from its pump by a daily cart (the original term is ‘cart’). The focus here is not to re-estimate the cause of disease, but to show that residential boundaries cannot directly substitute for actual water-supply exposure boundaries.","sourceLabel":"Snow, second edition, 1855, Hampstead passage","sourceUrl":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/72894/pg72894-images.html","sourceLocator":"Printed p. 44, Hampstead West End case paragraph, followed by the account of her niece’s drinking water and illness","boundary":"This is Snow’s historical account based on family reports and registration records; this guide does not construct a population-wide network or treat the water-delivery edge alone as a net causal effect.","href":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/72894/pg72894-images.html"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"2.10","start_line":425,"end_line":430,"startLine":425,"endLine":430}],"outputId":"section-2-10","sourceHash":"fcf100a5c8688d1e0337c52750ce3ad21fb5dbcc250f97946602ce094345f825","argumentSteps":["Premise: a grouping may have membership or documentary support, or may merely be proposed by the analyst; these two object statuses must remain distinct.","Separate uses: obtain the relevant records when describing members and authority; make proportionate baseline comparisons when claiming grouping gain; check channels, events, and effects when claiming a propagation mechanism.","Check relations: determine parallel, nested, and overlapping relations along declared dimensions such as membership, space, organization, or authority; record competitive constraints and duration separately.","Assess complexity: an added circle must preserve a necessary distinction at proportionate cost; absent stable gain evidence, do not exaggerate predictive value, while retaining independently supported membership, rights, and responsibility facts.","Conclusion: multiple circles organize objects relative to a task. More names do not make objects more real, and predictive scores do not determine whether every object merits retention."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching set construction: overlap is not a single relation","text":"Let member circles A={甲,乙} and B={乙,丙}, so A∩B={乙}: the circles overlap in membership. If their permitted action sets for a matter are U_A={a,b} and U_B={b,c}, the action satisfying both is b. If instead U_B={c}, the jointly feasible action set is empty. Whether membership overlap exists and whether action constraints are compatible are two different judgments; one cannot infer that 乙 is authorized to perform every action from membership in both circles.","assumptions":"The people, circles, and actions are all teaching symbols; the action sets are supplied by assumption and do not constitute real authorization.","boundary":"This illustrates only set relations and constraints; it establishes no real organizations, rights, responsibilities, or actual transmission."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"real_counterevidence","text":"Snow records someone living in Hampstead who had not visited Broad Street for a long time but received pump water by a daily water cart (the original term is ‘cart’).","effect":"Grouping solely by residence near the pump may miss actual exposure channels; geographic circles and water-supply exposure must be checked separately.","sourceIds":["snow-circles"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Teaching boundary case: a legally valid responsible party fails to improve a model’s predictive accuracy and is consequently removed from the responsibility record.","effect":"This treats predictive usefulness as the entry requirement for objects of rights and responsibilities; independently supported responsibility and authority over particular matters should be retained.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"If adding a circle does not improve prediction, can its membership records be rejected on that basis?","answer":"No. What is downgraded is the corresponding predictive-gain claim; real membership and documentary relationships retain their existing bases.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Does a person’s simultaneous membership in two groups suffice to prove that a message has spread?","answer":"No. Start with a potential bridge, then check the actual message, timing, and receipt; effects require further evidence.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"If two circles’ memberships overlap, can they directly be labeled as having ‘overlapping jurisdiction’?","answer":"No. Authorization must be checked for specific objects, matters, actions, and durations; intersecting memberships cannot substitute for it.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-2-10/figures/section-2-10.png","alt":"Candidate circles: boundaries/members/roles/time window/identity criterion; object facts: documents/relationships/exposure records; grouping-gain claims: comparison with a simple baseline; potential bridging: shared members or interfaces; actual transmission: events/versions/times; transmission effects or action authority: each requires additional evidence/authorization. Use diagram based on 2.10; predictive gain on the upper route is not the sole entry requirement for object existence and rights, and cross-circle facts do not automatically authorize action.","caption":"Use diagram based on 2.10; predictive gain on the upper route is not the sole entry requirement for object existence and rights, and cross-circle facts do not automatically authorize action."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static diagram; interaction has not been implemented, and the selections, switches, or clicks described below are ideas for a later design only. Selecting ‘description/prediction/propagation/rights and responsibilities’ would display different material requirements; switching between membership, spatial, and authority dimensions would reassess overlap without carrying labels over from another dimension.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"A concrete historical water-supply connection demonstrates the difference between spatial grouping and actual channels, making it suitable for questions about boundaries between multiple circles; it is not elevated into a universal network mechanism."},"openQuestions":[],"taskId":"S-2-10","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical research draft, full text, lines 395–400; see sourceRefs for each section’s exact range","claim_support":["2.10: terminology, contracts, burdens of argument, and boundaries of the original text; no empirical facts are certified on this basis"],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2.1.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"snow-circles","title":"John Snow, 1855, Hampstead West End drinking-water account","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/72894/pg72894-images.html","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"Second edition, 1855; digital transcription dated 2024-02-07","locator":"Printed p. 44, passage on the Hampstead woman and water delivery by cart (the original term is ‘cart’)","claim_support":["2.10: a resident far from Broad Street remained connected to the pump through water delivery; spatial location and exposure channel are not equivalent."],"limits":["A historical investigation account; no complete exposure network was constructed, and no net causal effect is identified from this case alone."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v2.1","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2.1, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2.1 / 2026-11-19","locator":"2.10　多圈层对象的方法边界; Chinese source lines 411–416; SHA-256 2128af7cfa4c63b58207521a375aa34c2aea230d47e1c04a5afec3334e290d28","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2.1; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-2-10/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-2-10/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-2-10/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-2-10/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v2.1; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"052b38e963cc1db1a81870c9873359fc4217a54a304efa51ed5a401fc8653ee2","source_sha256":"2128af7cfa4c63b58207521a375aa34c2aea230d47e1c04a5afec3334e290d28"}},"2.11":{"title":"2.11 Methodological Boundaries for Personality and Actor States","lead":"An instance of silence, enthusiasm, or a score is initially material from a particular time and setting. Interpreting it as stable personality requires additional checks across time, contexts, measurements, and uses.","explanation":["Source location: Chinese source lines 417–422; source anchor section-2-11; short quotation: ‘Personality enters only as a slow-variable hypothesis with an evidential level.’ See the corresponding section on this page for the current original text; the downloadable original research excerpt retains the historical draft.","This section places personality in the position of ‘a slow-variable hypothesis with an evidence level’, rather than fixing a person as an essence. Behavior may include relatively persistent tendencies, but one action, self-report, report by others, scale score, or model cluster cannot by itself generate a conclusion about stable personality. Naming a category cannot replace evidence across windows, contexts, and measurements.","At minimum, retain competing explanations involving role activation, material constraints, immediate emotion, strategy, cultural norms, observer position, and measurement bias. Similar reticence can occur during a task requiring concentration, in an unfamiliar language, or in a discussion where someone does not wish to disclose information; a single manifestation does not distinguish these explanations. Listing candidates does not claim that any of them actually occurred.","The slow, medium, and fast time bands help prevent substitutions of time scale: the slow band concerns hypotheses of persistent tendencies requiring longer windows of support; the medium band may concern temporary roles, goals, or resource states; the fast band may concern current emotion and local interaction. These scales are declared for the task and object, rather than forming a universal day-count table for everyone or automatically averaging fast variables into slow personality traits.","Each variable must be bound to an observation window, sources, disconfirming evidence, circle conditions, privacy, and permitted uses. Across settings, ask whether the original behavioral opportunities and roles remain, whether reports are comparable, and whether the inference exceeds the consented use. Multiple sources merely retelling one event cannot masquerade as repeated observation across contexts.","The framework does not make clinical or psychiatric diagnoses; silence, refusal to disclose, and unobservability are not evidence of pathology. A model may propose variable candidates and minimal tests, but cannot assign values to real people, much less expand managerial authority through inference. A careful result may be: ‘The available material is insufficient to distinguish a persistent tendency from a contextual response.’"],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Current state","leftText":"Behavior or self-report within a particular window.","rightTitle":"Slow-variable hypothesis","rightText":"A relatively persistent tendency across windows, requiring more, proportionate evidence."},{"leftTitle":"A mean describing a distribution","leftText":"One summary of repeated manifestations.","rightTitle":"Always like this","rightText":"A strong prediction about individual actions that does not follow from an average."},{"leftTitle":"Scale or cluster label","leftText":"A measurement or model representation.","rightTitle":"A person’s essence and clinical diagnosis","rightText":"Neither can be generated by a label, and clinical diagnosis also lies outside this framework’s remit."},{"leftTitle":"Unwillingness to disclose","leftText":"A person’s boundary on information use.","rightTitle":"Evidence of pathology or a trait","rightText":"Requires independent, qualified grounds and cannot be inferred from refusal itself."}],"boundaries":["Fleeson’s experience-sampling research uses students’ self-reports; stability of aggregate parameters is assessed mainly through random split-half comparisons within the same short window. It cannot be said to prove lifelong personality invariance or predictability of every action.","Insufficient material does not mean that the person has no persistent tendencies; it only limits the judgments currently available.","The examples in this section must not be used to score a particular real person’s personality, assert their mental state, or conduct high-impact screening; this guide has measured no reader."],"questions":["Is one enthusiastic instance sufficient to establish that someone is stably extraverted?","Does a highly stable mean of repeated reports mean that every moment is the same?","If a person is unwilling to disclose information, may a model automatically fill in health or personality information from other weak clues?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-2-11/figures/section-2-11.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"A 2001 experience-sampling study: state variation and aggregate stability coexist","href":"https://simine.com/407/readings/Fleeson_2001.pdf"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"A 2001 experience-sampling study: state variation and aggregate stability coexist","text":"In three experience-sampling studies with students, Fleeson repeatedly collected self-reports of everyday behavior over approximately two to three weeks. The research found both within-person variation and stability of aggregate distribution parameters. ‘Stability’ here mainly comes from random split-half comparisons within the same observation window, not proof of lifelong invariance.","sourceLabel":"William Fleeson, 2001, Traits as Density Distributions of States","sourceUrl":"https://simine.com/407/readings/Fleeson_2001.pdf","sourceLocator":"JPSP 80(6):1011–1027; abstract on p. 1011; methods on pp. 1014–1015, 1019–1020, 1022; Table 4 note on p. 1020 and discussion on pp. 1023–1024","boundary":"The original work is a short-window self-report study and offers no personality diagnosis of this guide’s readers or any specified person. Split-half stability has not been described as lifelong stability, and the raw data were not recalculated.","href":"https://simine.com/407/readings/Fleeson_2001.pdf"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"2.11","start_line":431,"end_line":436,"startLine":431,"endLine":436}],"outputId":"section-2-11","sourceHash":"fcf100a5c8688d1e0337c52750ce3ad21fb5dbcc250f97946602ce094345f825","argumentSteps":["Premise: observed behavior is material concerning a person, time, setting, and observation method; it does not directly equal a stable cross-context attribute.","Connection: a stable-tendency hypothesis requires an appropriate time window and comparable repeated material, and must compete with explanations involving roles, constraints, emotion, strategy, and measurement.","Evidence classification: separately record the scope supported by self-reports, reports from others, scales, behavioral records, and model outputs; do not count one source or event repeatedly as evidence.","Conclusion: at most, retain slow-variable candidates within supported windows, contexts, and uses; explanations not yet distinguished remain unresolved and cannot be raised to a person’s fixed essence.","Responsibility for use: any further collection or use of information involving privacy and action requires separate checks of necessity and authority; this section’s knowledge framework confers no qualification to diagnose or take personnel action."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching mathematical construction: the same mean does not mean every occasion is identical","text":"Assume, solely to illustrate aggregation, that person A’s four state scores are 1,1,7,7 and person B’s are 4,4,4,4. Both means are 4, but their variability differs. Knowing the mean alone does not reveal a particular occasion’s score; moreover, four observations with unspecified contexts cannot establish any real personality from this setup.","assumptions":"The numbers are constructed scores in the same units; A and B are not actual participants, and no clinical meaning is assigned.","boundary":"The example only refutes ‘same mean → same behavioral distribution / identical behavior every time’; it provides no evidence about real personality."},{"title":"Teaching candidate: one refusal to speak","text":"For the hypothetical scene “someone declines to speak at a meeting,” candidate explanations include: their role does not require speaking, insufficient material, current fatigue, strategic withholding, unwillingness to disclose here, or a more persistent preference. That behavior alone cannot select among these, still less establish a conclusion about health or personality. First ask whether the current task actually requires more personal information.","assumptions":"The situation is entirely hypothetical; the candidates are not judgments about any actual person.","boundary":"No compelled disclosure or unauthorized testing is recommended. Remaining unknown is a legitimate result."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"real_counterevidence","text":"Fleeson's three experience-sampling studies in 2001 report both substantial within-person state variation and stability in aggregated distribution parameters.","effect":"Short-term variation and aggregate stability can coexist. Neither a single behavior nor one average covers every situation.","sourceIds":["fleeson-state"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Teaching boundary: an observer records refusal to provide private information as “evidence of avoidant personality.”","effect":"Refusal to disclose is not standalone evidence of pathology or stable personality. Withdraw the label and retain the information boundary.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Is one enthusiastic instance sufficient to establish that someone is stably extraverted?","answer":"No. One performance covers only its immediate situation. Comparable material across multiple windows and competing explanations are needed, and the use must be justified.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Does a highly stable mean of repeated reports mean that every moment is the same?","answer":"No. A stable mean can coexist with substantial within-person variation. Distribution and context also matter.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"If a person is unwilling to disclose information, may a model automatically fill in health or personality information from other weak clues?","answer":"No. Retain the unknown and examine necessity, privacy, and use. Silence cannot be treated as evidence of pathology or as authorization to infer.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-2-11/figures/section-2-11.png","alt":"Slow band: evidential hypotheses about enduring tendencies. Middle band: roles/phase-specific goals/resources. Fast band: current emotions/local interaction. Material conditions: observation window/source/context. Competing explanations and counterevidence: measurement/roles/constraints. Bounded permitted uses: privacy/non-diagnosis/no exceeding authority. Conceptual diagram based on 2.11; time bands are specified by object and task. It plots no actual personality curve and scores no individual.","caption":"Conceptual diagram based on 2.11; time bands are specified by object and task. It plots no actual personality curve and scores no individual."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static diagram; interaction has not been implemented. References below to selecting, switching, or clicking are future design ideas only. Switching time windows or circles would show which variable assumptions need rechecking. The three bands are not automatically classified by calendar thresholds, and refusal to disclose must not be filled in as a pathological value.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"A primary experience-sampling design can illustrate the different temporal meanings of states and aggregated tendencies. It is suitable as an example of methodological boundaries, not a personality test."},"openQuestions":[],"taskId":"S-2-11","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical research draft, full-text lines 401–406; see sourceRefs for precise section ranges","claim_support":["2.11: terminology, contracts, burden of argument, and boundaries of the original text; not certification of empirical facts"],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2.1.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"fleeson-state","title":"Fleeson, Toward a Structure- and Process-Integrated View of Personality: Traits as Density Distributions of States","url":"https://simine.com/407/readings/Fleeson_2001.pdf","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"2001-06，DOI10.1037/0022-3514.80.6.1011","locator":"JPSP 80(6):1011–1027; abstract, methods of the three studies, Table 4 note, and General Discussion","claim_support":["2.11: student experience sampling with repeated behavioral self-reports over approximately 2–3 weeks; within-person variation and stable aggregate parameters coexist.","2.11: the relevant stability uses random split-half comparisons within the same window and cannot directly be interpreted as long-term invariance."],"limits":["The abstract and relevant methods and discussion passages used were read. Original data were not recalculated, and no full-study replication is claimed.","Not for clinical diagnosis or identification or scoring of actual readers."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v2.1","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2.1, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2.1 / 2026-11-19","locator":"2.11　人格与行动者状态的方法边界; Chinese source lines 417–422; SHA-256 2128af7cfa4c63b58207521a375aa34c2aea230d47e1c04a5afec3334e290d28","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2.1; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-2-11/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-2-11/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-2-11/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-2-11/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v2.1; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"61421d43aa43f4fb912e189f42d2cae988a74e60b0463dc80a60bcb7a5d744bb","source_sha256":"2128af7cfa4c63b58207521a375aa34c2aea230d47e1c04a5afec3334e290d28"}},"2.12":{"title":"2.12 Methodological boundaries for foresight and projection","lead":"Projections may be richly developed, but the factual record cannot change along with imagination. Evaluating foresight requires knowing what was actually known at the time, what prediction was committed to, and when it counts as right or wrong.","explanation":["Source location: Chinese source lines 423–428; original anchor section-2-12. Short quotation: “hypothetical and simulated events must not in turn be written as real-world facts.” See the corresponding section on this page for the current original; downloaded original research excerpts retain the historical draft.","Projection inputs may include observations, reports, plans, assumptions, and simulated events, but must retain their types. Observations have measurement limitations; a report establishes that someone said something, while its content still requires verification. A plan concerns intended action and cannot be recorded as execution. Assumptions and simulations enter the model only under their corresponding conditions and must not rewrite what actually happened. Entering the same projection does not make materials equally true or evidentially equivalent.","Each projection fixes in advance a joint snapshot: object and circle states, evidence cutoff, mechanisms, parameter ranges, clocks, propagation rules, and stopping conditions. Distinguish particularly when an event occurred from when the researcher learned of it. Material learned afterward cannot quietly be inserted into the inputs available to an earlier foresight run, even when it describes an earlier event.","Before outcomes occur, conditional foresight registers its target, horizon, object and circle scope, input cutoff, simple baseline, paths, expression format, calibration metrics, early signals, contrary signals, suspension, retirement, and outcome write-back. “Things will improve later” is insufficiently evaluable: specify which quantity improves, within what period, for whom, and relative to what.","Multiple paths are not an insurance box from which to choose a hit afterward. Their expression and aggregation must be specified beforehand. One cannot change the target, extend the horizon, narrow the population, or merge paths after the outcome and count the new answer as the old version's success. New information can improve the next version; the old version remains evaluated under its original registration with its deviations retained.","Calibration differs from a single hit. Failure of one high-probability event to occur does not immediately establish overall miscalibration; one hit does not establish long-term model superiority either. Accumulate tests using preselected scoring, baselines, and evaluation units, accounting for dependence among events. A change in conditions or evaluation units may change the target being evaluated.","This section does not prohibit simulation or planning. Simulation explores consequences of assumptions; plans indicate possible future events; updates reflect new knowledge. The key is preserving status and versions. The real example below shows only why counterfactual simulation figures in a paper cannot be described as actual implementation outcomes. It does not claim that the paper constitutes a future prediction meeting this framework's conditions.","Fixing the evaluation target still allows reasoned challenges to the question itself. Under §2.14, if new evidence exposes affected people omitted by the original representation or mistaken constraints, a separate question may be established. Preserve the old input cutoff, target, and performance record, and state whether the new and old questions replace, parallel, or refine one another. An unfavorable result alone does not justify turning an old prediction into a success."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Report","leftText":"A source claims something occurred; its truth requires verification.","rightTitle":"Observed fact","rightText":"Has corresponding observational grounds while retaining measurement boundaries."},{"leftTitle":"Plan","leftText":"An arrangement intended for implementation.","rightTitle":"Execution","rightText":"The action and timing that actually occurred, requiring event records."},{"leftTitle":"Simulation result","leftText":"Consequences calculated under a model and parameters.","rightTitle":"Actual effectiveness","rightText":"Material and causal grounds concerning real execution and outcomes."},{"leftTitle":"New foresight version","leftText":"A separate projection under new information or conditions.","rightTitle":"Rewriting an old hit","rightText":"Treating retrospective changes as earlier success undermines evaluation."}],"boundaries":["A counterfactual calculation rearranging patient composition does not establish actual algorithm modification, increased actual care, or improved health. The source's status must be retained.","A probability forecast's single success or failure does not establish overall calibration. Sample dependence, mixing tasks, and selective reporting also affect evaluation.","A fixed snapshot guarantees neither correct mechanisms nor execution without deviation. It supplies fixed, reviewable grounds; execution and counterexamples still require subsequent examination."],"questions":["A report says an event happened yesterday but arrives today. May yesterday's prediction treat it as a known input?","If a paper's simulation shows that a proportion would increase, may one write “the proportion increased after implementation”?","If the original deadline was September 30 and the target was reached only in October, may the horizon be changed to count the original prediction as correct?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-2-12/figures/section-2-12.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"17.7% and 46.5% in the 2019 algorithm paper: counterfactual rearrangement","href":"https://www.ftc.gov/system/files/documents/public_events/1548288/privacycon-2020-ziad_obermeyer.pdf"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"17.7% and 46.5% in the 2019 algorithm paper: counterfactual rearrangement","text":"At the 97th-percentile threshold, Obermeyer and colleagues used counterfactual rearrangement to estimate that the proportion of Black patients among those automatically identified would change from 17.7% to 46.5%. The original explicitly describes a simulated scenario, not an observed improvement following actual implementation.","sourceLabel":"Obermeyer et al. 2019, counterfactual simulation and Fig.1B","sourceUrl":"https://www.ftc.gov/system/files/documents/public_events/1548288/privacycon-2020-ziad_obermeyer.pdf","sourceLocator":"Science 366:447–453; printed p.449, counterfactual simulation paragraph preceding Fig.1B and its caption; PDF page 3","boundary":"The denominator is those automatically identified at the specified threshold, not all patients. The result concerns simulated selection composition, not actual additional care, individual health, or long-term effects. This lesson did not recalculate the data.","href":"https://www.ftc.gov/system/files/documents/public_events/1548288/privacycon-2020-ziad_obermeyer.pdf"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"2.12","start_line":437,"end_line":442,"startLine":437,"endLine":442}],"outputId":"section-2-12","sourceHash":"fcf100a5c8688d1e0337c52750ce3ad21fb5dbcc250f97946602ce094345f825","argumentSteps":["Premise: prediction evaluation requires a locatable commitment made before the outcome and the information set available then. Otherwise prediction cannot be distinguished from retrospective explanation.","Fixing: specify the input cutoff, object snapshot, mechanisms and parameters, event clocks, and stopping rules; preserve material types.","Public projection: generate conditional paths or probabilities from declared inputs and rules without labeling intermediate simulated nodes as real observations.","Evaluation: compare actual outcomes and the preset baseline under the original horizon and target. New information enters only a new version and does not rewrite old inputs or success conditions.","Conclusion: conditional foresight and simulated consequences may be offered, but used only according to their status. Actual action additionally requires independent evidence, norms, and authorization."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: late-arriving information can produce only a new version","text":"Suppose V1 closes its information set on June 1 and predicts that the same project will satisfy specified acceptance conditions by September 30. A report saying “work already stopped in August” arrives only on October 5. It concerns an earlier event but was not known input to V1. After verification, it may enter V2 or explain V1's deviation; one cannot claim V1 already used it. If construction finishes in November, V1's horizon still cannot be changed to year-end to report a hit.","assumptions":"The project, dates, stoppage, and outcome are all teaching assumptions and refer to no actual construction project.","boundary":"Illustrates only versioning and the distinction between two kinds of time; it evaluates no real project."},{"title":"Mathematical teaching construction: specify the scoring rule first rather than selecting a preferred one","text":"Suppose it is agreed beforehand to score a binary outcome y by squared error (p−y)², with lower scores better. For one forecast p=0.8 and outcome y=0, the score is 0.64; a baseline p=0.5 scores 0.25 on the same event. This shows only that the former performs worse on that occasion under the preset rule. It does not establish that it is worse across every task or globally miscalibrated.","assumptions":"Probabilities, outcome, and scoring are all teaching stipulations. No actual foresight exercise was conducted.","boundary":"A single score cannot stand for overall calibration, nor may a favorable scoring rule be substituted after the outcome."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"real_counterevidence","text":"In Obermeyer and colleagues' 2019 paper, the change from 17.7% to 46.5% is the estimated proportion of Black patients among automatically identified patients under counterfactual rearrangement at the 97th-percentile threshold.","effect":"This is a simulated composition change, not an observed improvement in care following actual implementation.","sourceIds":["obermeyer-simulation"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Teaching boundary: an original prediction concerns improvement for an entire population by year-end; after the outcome, only improving subgroups are selected and the horizon is extended by six months, yet the original prediction is counted as a hit.","effect":"The target population and horizon have changed. A new record is required; evaluate the original prediction under its original registration.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"A report says an event happened yesterday but arrives today. May yesterday's prediction treat it as a known input?","answer":"No. Event time differs from information-arrival time. After verification, the report enters a new version; the old version retains its original information cutoff.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"If a paper's simulation shows that a proportion would increase, may one write “the proportion increased after implementation”?","answer":"No. Simulation is neither execution nor observation; actual implementation and outcome material are required.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"If the original deadline was September 30 and the target was reached only in October, may the horizon be changed to count the original prediction as correct?","answer":"No. A new horizon creates a new record. Close the original version under its original horizon; causes may be explained, but a hit may not be rewritten.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-2-12/figures/section-2-12.png","alt":"V1 input cutoff: types/joint snapshot/clocks. V1 conditional projection: mechanisms/parameters/paths. Evaluate V1 under its original registration: target/horizon/baseline. New material after the cutoff: do not backfill old inputs. V2 new version: new grounds and conditions. Retain both version records: simulation is still not observed fact. Diagram of versions and material status, based on 2.12; it does not imply that simulation has been realized or display invented forecasting performance.","caption":"Diagram of versions and material status, based on 2.12; it does not imply that simulation has been realized or display invented forecasting performance."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static diagram; interaction has not been implemented. References below to selecting, switching, or clicking are future design ideas only. Switching V1/V2 would show inputs available at each time and their differing results, while old versions remain unalterable. Observation, report, plan, assumption, and simulation should carry continuously visible type labels.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"The actual paper explicitly distinguishes original grouping from simulated rearrangement, making it suitable for testing “simulated events must not in turn be written as real-world facts.” It is not presented as a foresight experiment completed by this lesson."},"openQuestions":["Claiming that real deployment changed care and health requires subsequent implementation, time-window and outcome material, and corresponding comparisons of effects. This lesson does not possess those materials."],"taskId":"S-2-12","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical research draft, full-text lines 407–412; see sourceRefs for precise section ranges","claim_support":["2.12: terminology, contracts, burden of argument, and boundaries of the original text; not certification of empirical facts"],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2.1.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"obermeyer-simulation","title":"Obermeyer et al. 2019, counterfactual simulation passage","url":"https://www.ftc.gov/system/files/documents/public_events/1548288/privacycon-2020-ziad_obermeyer.pdf","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"2019-10-25，Science366:447—453","locator":"Printed p.449, paragraph preceding Fig.1B and its caption; PDF page 3","claim_support":["2.12: under counterfactual rearrangement at the 97th-percentile threshold, the proportion of Black patients among automatically identified patients changes from 17.7% to 46.5%; the original explicitly says simulated."],"limits":["The textual values, denominator, and simulation status were checked; individual-level data were not obtained for recalculation.","Do not treat a simulated change in selection composition as actual implementation effectiveness, health benefits, or a foresight instance that passed this framework."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v2.1","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2.1, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2.1 / 2026-11-19","locator":"2.12　前瞻与推演的方法边界; Chinese source lines 423–428; SHA-256 2128af7cfa4c63b58207521a375aa34c2aea230d47e1c04a5afec3334e290d28","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2.1; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-2-12/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-2-12/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-2-12/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-2-12/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v2.1; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"04e918a1d8e1b81483744ac63da7b301436e6d07f6799c92f0df6a0e0428b76c","source_sha256":"2128af7cfa4c63b58207521a375aa34c2aea230d47e1c04a5afec3334e290d28"}},"2.13":{"title":"2.13 Limits on autonomous completion of variables","lead":"When a model finds something it cannot explain, it can help propose the next question; it cannot fill in the world's answers. Completion is limited to candidates, observable implications, and minimal tests with acceptable risk.","explanation":["Source location: Chinese source lines 429–434; original anchor section-2-13. Short quotation: “keeping it ‘unknown’ is more appropriate than actively filling it in.” See the corresponding section on this page for the current original; downloaded original research excerpts retain the historical draft.","The original permits only three autonomous actions. First, propose multiple competing candidates from residuals rather than selecting the hidden variable that sounds most like an answer. Second, state which observable implications each candidate might produce through which channel. Third, design the minimal test with acceptable risk. Designing a test does not mean execution has been authorized or results obtained.","A residual is what the existing representation or model has not explained. It may arise from a change in the actual process, changed measurement or reporting, sample selection, unsuitable model form, or other unmodeled conditions. “There is a residual” does not by itself establish a new personality trait, circle, or causal mechanism. Candidates should be distinguishable through targets and material, rather than merely giving the same unknown several attractive names.","Translate candidates into observable implications: if measurement definitions changed, which items, protocols, or comparable records should differ? If resources are constrained, which timings and constraints should correspond? If sample composition alone changed, what should change after composition is aligned? Every inference must disclose its conditions and contrary signals. If candidates cannot be distinguished, retain the unresolved status.","Candidate states include proposed, under testing, supported candidate, rejected, and retired; they are not a maturity ladder requiring one-way promotion. Supported candidates still have object, window, and design boundaries. Claims of support from formal tests, stable personality, an effective circle, or an actual mechanism must also meet their respective conditions. One cannot jump directly from “a model suggestion” to “an observed fact.”","Where privacy, health, safety, legal standing, or low-power positions are involved, first review the necessity of obtaining information, its use, and harm. A question may theoretically reduce uncertainty yet be irrelevant to the current task or expose too much. Remaining unknown is preferable when information value does not justify the risk. Protection and rights should not be crudely converted into an arbitrary number to justify unlimited demands on the grounds that benefits are larger.","A minimal test is sufficient to distinguish the current key candidates while staying within acceptable risk and authority; it is not unlimited collection of all obtainable data. One can first examine nonsensitive measurement protocols and public records or improve model representation. If the required test exceeds authority or professional competence, or cannot be performed safely, stop that route and retain the gap.","Candidate promotion requires two separate grounds: the existence of an organization, relation, or event may be supported by corresponding documents and observations; a gain from using that grouping in this task requires separate support from a grouping-gain test. When a model fills a residual with a “hidden circle,” the model's own assertion can supply neither ground."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Variable candidate","leftText":"A testable proposal concerning an unknown.","rightTitle":"Actual value","rightText":"Obtained through appropriate material, not generated by the proposal."},{"leftTitle":"Designing a minimal test","leftText":"Explaining how candidates can be distinguished and with what risks.","rightTitle":"Execution and obtaining a result","rightText":"Require actual authorization, participation, and records, with a separate evidential status."},{"leftTitle":"A supported candidate","leftText":"Has bounded grounds within the corresponding design and window.","rightTitle":"Establishment of every related strong claim","rightText":"Personality, circles, eligibility for formal tests, and mechanisms must still meet their respective conditions."},{"leftTitle":"Useful information","leftText":"May reduce a particular uncertainty.","rightTitle":"Necessary and justifiably obtainable","rightText":"Requires reviewing use, risk, authority, and alternative methods."}],"boundaries":["Prediction failure cannot automatically imply that someone is concealing information, has an abnormal personality, or belongs to a hidden organization. Competing candidates and measurement problems must remain in consideration.","“Minimal” does not mean that small scale entails no risk; a sensitive question or a single irreversible action may also cross a boundary.","When evidence alone is insufficient, retain an unresolved status. Where relevant counterevidence or a prespecified reason for exclusion exists, rejection is possible; where a candidate no longer applies or is no longer worth pursuing, it may be retired for stated reasons. Preserve the failure record; retirement does not prove that it can never exist for any possible object.","This lesson conducts no actual measurement of or intervention on readers. The actual research case only illustrates how its authors designed discriminating tests; it does not turn that design into instructions for readers."],"questions":["After discovering a residual, may the most convincing story be entered directly as its cause?","Is the possibility that a piece of sensitive information could improve predictive accuracy sufficient to authorize collecting it?","Once a candidate gains support within a particular window, may it immediately be described as a stable personality or a formal G mechanism?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-2-13/figures/section-2-13.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"Fleeson's alternative measurement explanations: first make candidates distinguishable","href":"https://simine.com/407/readings/Fleeson_2001.pdf"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"Fleeson's alternative measurement explanations: first make candidates distinguishable","text":"In Study 2 of his 2001 research, Fleeson asked participants to learn standardized definitions to address differences in adjective interpretation. Study 3 used bipolar scales to test the explanation that variation might arise from the use of unipolar scales. This section uses these designs to show how researchers translated alternative explanations into comparable measurement conditions, rather than filling in real attributes directly from labels.","sourceLabel":"Fleeson 2001, methods of Studies 2 and 3","sourceUrl":"https://simine.com/407/readings/Fleeson_2001.pdf","sourceLocator":"Printed pp. 1019–1020, Study 2 introduction and methods; pp. 1021–1022, Study 3 introduction and methods","boundary":"This is a research design already reported by its author. It does not mean that this lesson performed measurements, does not prove that every scale bias or personality mechanism has been excluded, and does not retroactively confer formal G status under this framework.","href":"https://simine.com/407/readings/Fleeson_2001.pdf"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"2.13","start_line":443,"end_line":448,"startLine":443,"endLine":448}],"outputId":"section-2-13","sourceHash":"fcf100a5c8688d1e0337c52750ce3ad21fb5dbcc250f97946602ce094345f825","argumentSteps":["Premise: failure of the current model shows that existing explanations are insufficient; it does not uniquely determine a new cause.","Candidate generation: list several distinct explanations and specify which quantity each changes, through which channel, and what observable result it produces within which window.","Risk check: first check whether the information is necessary, what uses are permitted, who might be harmed, and whether lower-risk alternatives and stopping routes exist.","Connection to testing: conduct a proportionate minimal test only when the authorization and risk conditions are met. This explanation only designs a proposal; it does not actually recruit, measure, or collect personal information.","Conclusion: update support, rejection, or retirement of candidates according to the material actually obtained. Where evidence is insufficient, retain unknown values; do not create facts through automatic imputation."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: a waiting-time residual, without guessing people's health","text":"Suppose a model underestimates waiting times for a service. Three candidates can be proposed: the arrival rate changed, processing resources decreased, or the timing convention changed from the time of joining the queue to the time of submission. Check public arrival counts, staffing capacity, and the timing protocol respectively, stating in advance which findings would weaken each candidate. If these nonsensitive records have not yet been clarified, directly collecting staff health or family information is neither a necessary first step nor automatically authorized by its “information value.”","assumptions":"The service and residual are entirely teaching assumptions; no actual personal or operational data have been obtained.","boundary":"The proposal illustrates competing explanations and priority for lower-risk methods. It conducts no investigation and asserts no candidate to be true."},{"title":"Teaching conditional counterexample: several explanations cannot yet be distinguished","text":"Suppose the available records contain only the total daily waiting amount, and both increased arrivals and reduced processing can produce that same total. Adding more totals of the same kind may not distinguish them. Separate arrival and completion records could be added within existing permissions. If those records cannot be obtained and sensitive alternatives are unacceptable, the result is that both candidates remain unresolved, rather than choosing one arbitrarily for the fact table.","assumptions":"The identifiability scenario is constructed for teaching; permissions and risks must be checked separately in the actual setting.","boundary":"This does not prove that all additional data are useless, nor does it generate a real mechanism."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"real_counterevidence","text":"Fleeson's research treated differences in the interpretation of scale wording and the use of unipolar scales as measurement explanations to be tested, designing standardized-definition and bipolar-scale conditions respectively.","effect":"Competing explanations are tested by changing distinguishable measurement conditions, rather than automatically assigning apparent score differences to real personality differences. This lesson does not claim that all measurement biases have been excluded.","sourceIds":["fleeson-test"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Teaching boundary: a model proposes “possible depression” as a hidden variable for unexplained behavior, then automatically enters it as that person's actual health status.","effect":"This improperly promotes a candidate to a sensitive fact and exceeds this framework's diagnostic boundary. Retain an unknown value and stop the inappropriate completion.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"After discovering a residual, may the most convincing story be entered directly as its cause?","answer":"No. List competing candidates, their observable implications, and tests that can distinguish them; a coherent story cannot replace material evidence.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Is the possibility that a piece of sensitive information could improve predictive accuracy sufficient to authorize collecting it?","answer":"No. First check necessity, purpose, risk, effects on low-power positions, and alternative methods; retaining an unknown value may be more appropriate.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Once a candidate gains support within a particular window, may it immediately be described as a stable personality or a formal G mechanism?","answer":"No. The scope of support remains limited by the design and material. Strong claims of other types must separately satisfy their corresponding conditions.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-2-13/figures/section-2-13.png","alt":"Residuals of the existing model: insufficiency of representation or explanation; multiple competing candidates: no automatic selection of a cause; observable implications: channel / window / contrary signals; necessity and risk review: privacy / permissions / lower-risk alternatives; minimal test proposal: execution requires separate actual authorization; update status according to material: supported / unresolved / rejected / retired. Method diagram based on 2.13; candidates do not automatically become facts, and the diagram does not mean that this lesson conducted any experiment.","caption":"Method diagram based on 2.13; candidates do not automatically become facts, and the diagram does not mean that this lesson conducted any experiment."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static diagram; interaction has not been implemented. Any selection, switching, or clicking described below is only a future design proposal. The risk or permission status of a test could be switched to show the route pausing while an unknown value is retained; a candidate-generation button must not be designed to fill in sensitive facts automatically.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"The original research's competing measurement explanations and designs directly illustrate “candidate → observable difference → test,” without requiring any actual experiment on readers."},"openQuestions":["Whether an additional variable is worth obtaining must be assessed against the specific task, existing material, permissions, and potential harm. This lesson does not fill in sensitive values for any real person."],"taskId":"S-2-13","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical research manuscript, Chinese source lines 413–420; see sourceRefs for each section's exact range","claim_support":["2.13: terminology, contracts, burdens of argument, and boundaries of the original text; not a basis for certifying empirical facts"],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2.1.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"fleeson-test","title":"Fleeson 2001, tests of measurement explanations in Studies 2 and 3","url":"https://simine.com/407/readings/Fleeson_2001.pdf","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"2001-06，DOI10.1037/0022-3514.80.6.1011","locator":"JPSP 80(6):1011–1027; pp. 1019–1020, standardized definitions; pp. 1021–1022, bipolar scales","claim_support":["2.13: Study 2 uses standardized adjective definitions to address differences in interpretation; Study 3 uses bipolar scales to examine the alternative explanation involving the use of unipolar scales."],"limits":["Different designs within one research project do not gain additional independent support merely through the number of files or examples.","Only the methods passages read are used; no claim is made that all measurement biases have been excluded, and no personality test is administered to readers."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v2.1","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2.1, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2.1 / 2026-11-19","locator":"2.13　自主完善变量的上限; Chinese source lines 429–434; SHA-256 2128af7cfa4c63b58207521a375aa34c2aea230d47e1c04a5afec3334e290d28","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2.1; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-2-13/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-2-13/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-2-13/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-2-13/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v2.1; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"ed6a81075e496c9ad6ea2d53d3ecdb58a31f28a8bf0d5b27c0d58c7d2f682ee2","source_sha256":"2128af7cfa4c63b58207521a375aa34c2aea230d47e1c04a5afec3334e290d28"}},"2.14":{"title":"2.14 Fixing and Reopening a Question","lead":"Fixing makes the old question evaluable; reopening allows omissions and the framing itself to be challenged.","explanation":["Fixing objects, aims, boundaries, and constraints before outcomes preserves reviewable comparisons; it does not permanently give the original questioner control over framing. Affected people, excluded people, and analysts may identify inadequate representation, uncounted losses, omitted members, or constraints mistakenly treated as given.","Whether a reopening request is accepted or rejected, record the request, reasons, and disposition. If accepted, establish a new question and state whether it replaces, sits alongside, or refines the old one. Preserve the old question, results, failures, and costs. Analysts can answer both questions side by side; actual institutional changes still depend on authorized procedures.","An unfavorable answer alone is insufficient reason to change the question. An answer exposing omitted affected people or defective representation can supply a reason. Distinguishing evidenced reflection prompted by results from retrospectively moving the success line depends on whether new reasons, old records, and the decision's grounds remain traceable.","For example, expanding ‘short-term output’ to ‘output and sustained labor burden’ leaves the original output result evaluated under its original aim. The new multi-objective comparison proceeds separately: it cannot erase failure of the original task, and the fact that the question has been fixed cannot justify refusing to acknowledge labor burdens."],"keyTerms":[{"term":"Reasons to reopen","text":"Evidenced problems such as inadequate original representation, omitted positions or losses, and contestable constraints."},{"term":"Relationships between versions","text":"Whether the new question replaces, accompanies, or refines the old one must be stated publicly."}],"sourceQuotes":[{"text":"Fixing the question does not give the person who posed it a monopoly over it.","ref":"§2.14 · author's English reference edition","href":"/en/theory/part-02#section-2-14"}],"argumentSteps":["Fixing objects, aims, boundaries, and constraints before outcomes preserves reviewable comparisons; it does not permanently give the original questioner control over framing. Affected people, excluded people, and analysts may identify inadequate representation, uncounted losses, omitted members, or constraints mistakenly treated as given.","Whether a reopening request is accepted or rejected, record the request, reasons, and disposition. If accepted, establish a new question and state whether it replaces, sits alongside, or refines the old one. Preserve the old question, results, failures, and costs. Analysts can answer both questions side by side; actual institutional changes still depend on authorized procedures.","An unfavorable answer alone is insufficient reason to change the question. An answer exposing omitted affected people or defective representation can supply a reason. Distinguishing evidenced reflection prompted by results from retrospectively moving the success line depends on whether new reasons, old records, and the decision's grounds remain traceable."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Fixing","leftText":"Preserving the question, input cutoff, and evaluation rules as they stood.","rightTitle":"Reopening","rightText":"Establishing a separate new question with reasons and explaining its relationship to the old one."}],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Constructed teaching scenario","text":"A plan required completion within three months, but the task was unfinished. Moving the deadline to six months cannot make the old plan successful. If necessary care responsibilities were also found missing from the original plan, retain the three-month failure and separately assess a new plan including care conditions.","boundary":"Illustrates conditions and counterexamples only; it reports no real event or completed mechanism test."}],"caseNotes":[],"caseLinks":[],"boundaries":["A new question does not overwrite old failure; fixing does not cancel standing to challenge.","Parallel analytical answers do not confer authority to change institutional objectives."],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"Constructed conditional counterexample","text":"A plan required completion within three months, but the task was unfinished. Moving the deadline to six months cannot make the old plan successful. If necessary care responsibilities were also found missing from the original plan, retain the three-month failure and separately assess a new plan including care conditions.","effect":"A new question does not overwrite old failure; fixing does not cancel standing to challenge.","sourceIds":["theory-v2"]}],"questions":["If flawed framing is discovered after results, is revision forever prohibited?"],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"If flawed framing is discovered after results, is revision forever prohibited?","answer":"Reopening with reasons is possible, while retaining the old task and results, establishing a separate new question, and stating its decision procedure. Success under a new aim cannot be backfilled into the old one.","basis":"v2 §2.14"}],"openQuestions":[],"summary":"Fixing makes the old question evaluable; reopening allows omissions and the framing itself to be challenged.","sourceRefs":[{"section":"2.14","start_line":449,"end_line":464,"startLine":449,"endLine":464}],"sourceHash":"fcf100a5c8688d1e0337c52750ce3ad21fb5dbcc250f97946602ce094345f825","sourceSliceHash":"1644a3ce39105448847a589827937b8561324616864c2768f80091becaa825e0","outputId":"section-2-14","taskId":"S-2-14","sources":[{"id":"theory-v2.1","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2.1, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2.1 / 2026-11-19","locator":"2.14　问题的固定与重开; Chinese source lines 435–444; SHA-256 2128af7cfa4c63b58207521a375aa34c2aea230d47e1c04a5afec3334e290d28","claim_support":["This section's definitions, argumentative boundaries, and explicitly labeled constructed teaching explanations."],"limits":["No new external empirical test was added; examples do not serve as real-world evidence."],"access_status":"read"}],"applicability":{"realCase":"not_applicable","diagram":"not_applicable","reason":"This section explains the new v2 argument through conceptual distinctions, explicitly labeled teaching scenarios, and contrary conditions, without adding external empirical research."},"lecturePath":"","sourcesPath":"","sourceSlicePath":"","diagramsPath":"","supplementaryFiles":[],"figures":[],"diagramBrief":"Item-by-item comparison preserves conditions without adding a figure that would portray the process as a mandatory sequence.","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v2.1; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"77411cb1aa5ade8f1137bcacc420886af0cde55847e84bdf9cc049c539174c7b","source_sha256":"2128af7cfa4c63b58207521a375aa34c2aea230d47e1c04a5afec3334e290d28"}}}},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/theory/part-02.md","filePath":"en/theory/part-02.md"}`),d={name:"en/theory/part-02.md"};function h(u,e,p,m,f,g){const t=o("SectionLesson");return r(),c("div",null,[e[0]||(e[0]=n("div",{class:"theory-source"},[n("p",null,[n("a",{id:"part_2"})]),n("h1",{id:"part-title-2",tabindex:"-1"},[l("Part 2 Boundaries and Methods "),n("a",{class:"header-anchor",href:"#part-title-2","aria-label":'Permalink to "Part 2 Boundaries and Methods {#part-title-2}"'},"​")]),n("p",null,"The ultimate object domain of this framework is the broadly conceived empirical structural world. Natural processes, technical systems, ecological processes, human actions, organizations, and institutions can all enter structural explanation if they can leave comparable materials and allow researchers to state candidate objects, states, relations or transitions, interfaces, time windows, scale profiles, and identity criteria. The human structured world is not a synonym for the world as a whole. It is a specialized domain within that world, requiring additional human-domain clauses, normative commitments, and authorization procedures. Purely metaphysical assertions, objects for which no empirical elements can be given, and “systems” established solely through naming or narrative coherence do not enter empirical judgment. They may be discussed, but they must not borrow this framework's levels of empirical conclusions."),n("p",null,"The current scope of application must still be stated separately each time: the object; the scale, time window, and identity criterion; the region or environment; the data window; the extrapolation unit; and exclusions. That an object belongs to the broadly conceived empirical structural world means only that it can in principle be defined. It does not imply that any conclusion can be directly generalized across windows, objects, or domains."),n("p",null,"This choice of scope also sets the framework's limits. The framework seeks to explain how objects form identifiable structures, how they operate, how they change or undergo dissolution under the effects of conditions, and how descriptions at different scales can be converted. It does not presuppose a unified purpose for the world, project human intentions onto nonhuman processes, or infer “how one should choose” directly from “what is.” Real-world choices are taken up separately by normative premises and operational procedures.")],-1)),a(t,{section:"intro-02-part"}),e[1]||(e[1]=i("",1)),a(t,{section:"2.1"}),e[2]||(e[2]=i("",1)),a(t,{section:"2.1.1"}),e[3]||(e[3]=i("",1)),a(t,{section:"2.1.2"}),e[4]||(e[4]=i("",1)),a(t,{section:"2.2"}),e[5]||(e[5]=i("",1)),a(t,{section:"2.3"}),e[6]||(e[6]=i("",1)),a(t,{section:"2.3.1"}),e[7]||(e[7]=i("",1)),a(t,{section:"2.4"}),e[8]||(e[8]=i("",1)),a(t,{section:"2.4.1"}),e[9]||(e[9]=i("",1)),a(t,{section:"2.4.2"}),e[10]||(e[10]=i("",1)),a(t,{section:"2.4.3"}),e[11]||(e[11]=i("",1)),a(t,{section:"2.5"}),e[12]||(e[12]=i("",1)),a(t,{section:"2.5.1"}),e[13]||(e[13]=i("",1)),a(t,{section:"2.6"}),e[14]||(e[14]=i("",1)),a(t,{section:"2.6.1"}),e[15]||(e[15]=i("",1)),a(t,{section:"2.7"}),e[16]||(e[16]=i("",1)),a(t,{section:"2.7.1"}),e[17]||(e[17]=i("",1)),a(t,{section:"2.7.2"}),e[18]||(e[18]=i("",1)),a(t,{section:"2.8"}),e[19]||(e[19]=i("",1)),a(t,{section:"2.9"}),e[20]||(e[20]=i("",1)),a(t,{section:"2.10"}),e[21]||(e[21]=i("",1)),a(t,{section:"2.11"}),e[22]||(e[22]=i("",1)),a(t,{section:"2.12"}),e[23]||(e[23]=i("",1)),a(t,{section:"2.13"}),e[24]||(e[24]=i("",1)),a(t,{section:"2.14"}),e[25]||(e[25]=i("",3))])}const v=s(d,[["render",h]]);export{y as __pageData,v as default};
